reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/altar/notes.md:34:## Disagreements with WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/altar/notes.md:36:No numeric disagreement with the BMJ report was found. Checked: `docs/05-special-populations/05e-womens-health/recurrent-uti.mdx` (lines 134 to 138, 260, 332 to 338), `docs/01-foundations/pharmacology/infection-prophylaxis/non-antibiotic-uti-prevention.mdx` (lines 52 to 73, 268, 286, 327 to 329), `uti-suppressive-prophylactic.mdx` (lines 45, 92, 163, 176), `urinary-diversion-specific/urinary-acidifiers.mdx` (line 66), `infection-prophylaxis/index.mdx` (line 8). All quoted numbers (240 randomized, 205 in the primary analysis, 103 and 102, 1.38 versus 0.89, difference 0.49, margin 1, 46/64 versus 39/70, P = 0.05, 9/45 versus 2/39, P = 0.06, 11/191 isolates from five participants, four methenamine and one nitrofurantoin) match the sources. Points a reviewer may want to consider:
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/altar/notes.md:39:- `recurrent-uti.mdx` line 136 and `non-antibiotic-uti-prevention.mdx` line 66 describe the 72% versus 56% resistance finding as "during treatment" and "at 6 or 12 months"; the source reports it at the 6- or 12-month visits, and the month-18 multidrug resistance finding (higher with methenamine, 20% versus 5%) points the other way. The non-antibiotic page includes both; `recurrent-uti.mdx` line 136 gives only the first.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/altar/notes.md:40:- The page `non-antibiotic-uti-prevention.mdx` line 61 says trials studied 6 to 12 months; ALTAR treated for 12 months and followed participants for 6 further months.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/altar/notes.md:41:- Statements about neurogenic bladder exclusion (non-antibiotic page line 73) and acidification not being tested (urinary-acidifiers line 66; non-antibiotic line 52) agree with the BMJ methods and limitations.
