reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bulking-vs-tvt/notes.md:5:## Lead correction
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bulking-vs-tvt/notes.md:13:| Primary, J Urol 2020;203(2):372-378 | DOI 10.1097/JU.0000000000000517, PMID 31479396 | PubMed abstract (eutils efetch XML). Publisher page returned HTTP 402; Unpaywall shows no open copy; no local copy under `reports/audit-v2/sources-local`, `/Users/joyboy/Documents/Medicine`, `/Users/joyboy/Downloads` or `/Users/joyboy/Desktop` (only Bulkamid brochure and IFU PDFs, not read). | abstract |
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bulking-vs-tvt/notes.md:19:All five DOIs and PMIDs resolved; authors, journal, volume, pages and online dates were checked on Crossref and PubMed. Publication dates in the file: primary 2019-09-04 (PubMed entrez date; Crossref record created 2019-09-03; issue date February 2020); QoL 1-year 2020-12-04 (Crossref online); 3-year 2022-08-09 (the article states "published August 9, 2022"); QoL 3-year 2023-09-06 (Crossref online); 5-year 2025-04-22 (Crossref and PubMed).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bulking-vs-tvt/notes.md:21:Other sources: ClinicalTrials.gov API record for NCT02538991 (read for registry facts only, not used for any number on the page); local guideline copies `reports/audit-v2/sources-local/aua-sui-2023-unabridged.txt` (statement 12, bulking agents discussion, references 92 and 93), `eau-non-neurogenic-female-luts-2026.txt` (section 4.2.4.c.2.c, reference 372, summary of evidence and recommendations for urethral bulking agents), `nice-ng123-ui-pop-women.txt`.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bulking-vs-tvt/notes.md:26:- Design details (eligibility, randomization, sample size, procedure, operators) come from the 3-year and QoL reports, which describe the same protocol. Details the primary report may add (for example its exact exclusions and analysis populations) are not on the page.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bulking-vs-tvt/notes.md:27:- Registry eligibility (ClinicalTrials.gov): exclusions include BMI above 35, prolapse beyond second degree, anticholinergic or mirabegron use, active malignancy, recurrent UTI. The page uses the papers' wording instead ("clinically significant prolapse and obese women were not included") and does not give the BMI cut-off, because no paper I read states it.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bulking-vs-tvt/notes.md:28:- Reported inconsistencies inside the 3-year paper, kept as printed: the cough stress test interval is 8.6 to 26.9 in the abstract and text but 8.0 to 27.2 in Table 2 (abstract used; noted in the file). The "cough stress and pad test negative" row for hydrogel prints 47 (50.0%), which implies a denominator of 94, not 96; the pad-test-negative rows (53, 56.4% and 76, 83.5%) also imply smaller denominators than 96 and 92; only the 47 (50.0%) row is in the file, with a note). In the 1-year QoL paper the between-group "95% CI" printed in Table 2 does not bracket the group difference, so no interval is quoted from it.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bulking-vs-tvt/notes.md:29:- Randomized count: the primary report and the 1-year QoL paper say 224 women entered (TVT 111, hydrogel 113); the 3-year, 3-year QoL and 5-year reports say 223 randomized (TVT 110, hydrogel 113). The 1-year QoL paper states that one woman accidentally received two randomization envelopes; no paper says explicitly that this accounts for the difference of one. The trial file uses 224 (primary report) with arm counts 113 and 111, and the page states both counts.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bulking-vs-tvt/notes.md:39:None. No trial on the site shares a clinical connection that I could state in a relation note: the other trials in `sui-primary-surgery` compare slings with colposuspension or one sling route with another, and none uses this trial's result as a rationale. The page links SISTEr and TOMUS in the "In conversation" paragraph only as a contrast in endpoint definition (composite or objective success versus a satisfaction score).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bulking-vs-tvt/notes.md:41:## WARWIKI pages that cite the trial, and disagreements
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bulking-vs-tvt/notes.md:43:I searched `docs` for "Itkonen", "polyacrylamide" and "Helsinki". Pages that cite it:
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bulking-vs-tvt/notes.md:49:I checked every trial number on these pages against the abstracts and the 3-year full text: 223 randomized, 212 treated, 195 attended; 74/99 (74.7%) versus 89/96 (92.7%); difference 18.0 (95% CI 7.7 to 28.0); medians 90 and 98; complications before crossover 22/99 (22.2%) versus 42/96 (43.8%); 3-year cough stress test 75/96 (78.1%) versus 88/92 (95.7%); 30 crossovers; per-protocol 22/66 (33.3%) versus 74/89 (83.1%). All agree with the sources. Two wording points, not numeric errors:
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bulking-vs-tvt/notes.md:52:- `bulkamid.mdx` line on the three-year per-protocol analysis says "22/66 (33.3%) PAHG recipients versus 74/89 (83.1%) TVT recipients" for "both cough stress and pad tests"; the 3-year paper reports this in the text paragraph on the per-protocol (treatment received) analysis, consistent with the page.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bulking-vs-tvt/notes.md:56:No page cites the 2020 primary report, the 2022 3-year report (other than `bulkamid.mdx`), or either quality-of-life paper; the AUA/SUFU and EAU guidelines both cite the primary report, and AUA/SUFU also the 3-year report.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/aus-outcomes/notes.md:4:- Primary report: Kaufman MR et al., J Urol 2026;215(2):194-202, doi 10.1097/JU.0000000000004796, PMID 41042705, PMC12794701. FULL TEXT read online (PMC JATS XML via NCBI efetch, including Tables 1-5). No local copy exists (searched reports/audit-v2/sources-local, Downloads, Medicine; only the owner's journal-club slide deck `/Users/joyboy/Documents/Medicine/Urology/Fellowship/My Presentations/AUSCO JC.pptx` was found and not used). `source.file` is therefore null. Supplementary tables (jurology.com) not opened; no value in the file comes from them. The PMC text has no PDF page numbers; locators use table numbers and section names.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/aus-outcomes/notes.md:8:- Sexual quality of life: Jones L et al., J Sex Med 2026;23(6):qdag144, doi 10.1093/jsxmed/qdag144, PMID 42184104. PubMed has no abstract; numbers come from the publisher page (academic.oup.com/jsm/article/doi/10.1093/jsxmed/qdag144/8692734) read through WebFetch, which returns a model-generated summary, not verbatim text. Marked `level: abstract`; the percentages recompute from the stated n/N (39/90, 11/90, 4/8, 2/8) but the P values and wording should be rechecked against the article. Online date 2025-05-25 is Crossref `published-online`; the issue date is 2026-05-11.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/aus-outcomes/notes.md:9:- DOIs, titles, volumes, pages and author lists for all five reports were checked on Crossref and PubMed. Urology epub date (2025-10-01) is PubMed's epubdate; Crossref lists only the January 2026 issue.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/aus-outcomes/notes.md:20:## Disagreements with other WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/aus-outcomes/notes.md:21:No numeric disagreements with the source. Minor items:
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/aus-outcomes/notes.md:23:- Reference titles on the procedure page (ref12), the male SUI database page (ref4) and the foundations page differ from the published title "The Artificial Urinary Sphincter Clinical Outcomes Trial: Primary Results" (they insert "(AUSCO)" or write "Trial (AUSCO): primary results"); the procedure page ref12 says "Clinical Outcomes (AUSCO) trial".
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/aus-outcomes/notes.md:25:- Prior-radiotherapy detail: the primary report lists 23 external beam and 2 brachytherapy; the radiotherapy paper says 23 had radiotherapy as adjuvant therapy and 2 as primary therapy and that 2 received brachytherapy. The page uses each as written.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/aus-outcomes/notes.md:27:## Disagreements with the old Journal Club record
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/aus-outcomes/notes.md:28:- None in numbers (94% 91/97, 60% 61/101, 15% serious, 8.7% serious device-related, 7.8% revisions with mechanical 3 and erosion 2, none for infection all match Tables 2-3). The record omits the 65% performance goal, the worst-case 79% analysis, 17 sites, the United States and Australia, and the exclusion of prior radiation until protocol version C. "Industry-sponsored" is accurate (Boston Scientific supported the study; two authors are affiliated with it). Year 2026 is the issue year; online 2025-10-03.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/aus-outcomes/notes.md:33:- Whether the two Boston Scientific-affiliated authors are employees (the paper lists the affiliation only; the page says "affiliated").
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/courage/notes.md:3:Counts: 3 reports, 15 results, 11 harms, 6 page references. Also 8 delivery facts (primary 6, bladder-function 2).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/courage/notes.md:7:- Primary report: Staskin 2024, J Urol 212(2):256-266, doi 10.1097/JU.0000000000003999, PMID 38708869. Full text via PMC12721610 (efetch XML, read online; no page numbers in that text, so locators name sections, tables and figures with the PMC id). Resolved on Crossref and PubMed: authors (Staskin, Owens-Grillo, Thomas, Rovner, Cline, Mujais), year, volume, pages match. Online date 2024-05-06 (PubMed); print Aug 2024.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/courage/notes.md:10:- Registry: ClinicalTrials.gov API NCT03902080 (participant flow: 551 vibegron and 554 placebo started, 1,105 total; completed 484 + 481 = 965). Used only for the arm sizes in `design.arms`; the paper's text does not give randomized arm sizes. Sponsor on registry: Urovant Sciences GmbH (not cited on the page).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/courage/notes.md:11:- Guidelines (local copies): `reports/audit-v2/sources-local/aua-bph-2026-unabridged.txt` (statement 28 at p. 28, text citing ref 205 = COURAGE; summary list p. 3); `reports/audit-v2/sources-local/eau-male-luts-2026.txt` (section 5.2.7.c, recommendation "Weak" and COURAGE text on p. 34). AUA OAB 2024 unabridged was read: statement 33 (BPH plus OAB, Conditional, Grade B) does not cite COURAGE, so it is not on the page.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/courage/notes.md:13:- FDA: approval letter NDA 213006/S-011 (accessdata.fda.gov appletter/2024/213006Orig1s011ltr.pdf; fetched through WebFetch, signature date 12/18/2024) and openFDA drugsfda record (supplement 11, Efficacy, approved 2024-12-18). The direct curl download of the accessdata URL returned an HTML 404 page; the PDF came through WebFetch.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/courage/notes.md:24:## Disagreements with WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/courage/notes.md:26:No page statement contradicts the primary source. Items to be aware of:
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/courage/notes.md:28:- `docs/01-foundations/pharmacology/storage-oab/beta3-agonists.mdx` line 158: "urinary-retention adverse events in 5/553 (0.9%) with vibegron and 3/551 (0.5%) with placebo". Matches primary Table 2 (AESI urinary retention). The Rovner report counts 5/553 (0.9%) versus 4/551 (0.7%) for the same safety set (its Table 2, "Urinary retention"); the two reports differ by one placebo case. The trial file keeps 3/551 in the primary report and 4/551 in the Rovner report; the page states both.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/courage/notes.md:29:- Same file, line 99-107: the table gives the micturition difference as "P<0.001"; the source states P < .0001 (not an error). Heading text "1,105 men on α-blocker ± 5-ARI" matches.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/courage/notes.md:32:- Page statements that vibegron's BPH indication is "2024" match the FDA letter date December 18, 2024.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/courage/notes.md:36:- `n: 965` is wrong for the trial size: 965 is the number who completed (87.3%); 1,105 were randomized, 1,104 dosed, 1,080 in the full analysis set. The trial file uses n = 1,105.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/courage/notes.md:38:- Old record says "UTI and retention rates similar to placebo": supported (UTI 2.5% versus 2.2%; retention 0.9% versus 0.5%, both under 1%). The older bottom line ("directly addresses treated the prostate, still has urgency") omits that men with postvoid residual of 100 mL or more, prior retention or Qmax below 5 mL/s were excluded; the page states this in Design and Limitations.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/courage/notes.md:44:- Rovner 2026 gives "20 (3.7%)" for participants with a postvoid residual rise of 100 mL or more at more than one consecutive visit (versus 7, 1.3%); 20/553 is 3.6%. Those figures are not used in the trial file or page.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/courage/notes.md:46:- Paper dates the study "March 7, 2019" to "June 15, 2023" (page cites the paper); the registry lists start 2019-03-26 and completion 2023-06-15.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/courage/notes.md:48:- Primary-report page numbers are not in the PMC text; locators name sections, tables and figures. A pass against the journal PDF could add page numbers.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/courage/notes.md:50:- `npm run lint:trials` was not run (output files stay in the work folder); the validator rules were checked by hand: report order by date, arm-key matching, `met` on primary and key-secondary results, takeaway at most 45 words (44), verdictNote under 20 (17), relation note under 25 (19), DOI on page. The `empowur` relation target must exist as a trial file when this is merged.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bmg-closure-meta/notes.md:5:- Primary: Güler Y. Arab J Urol 2023;21(1):18-30 (online 18 July 2022). DOI 10.1080/2090598X.2022.2097613, PMID 36818369, PMC9930765. Full text via the PubMed Central efetch XML, plus the eight PMC figure images (Figure 2 risk of bias, Figures 3-7 forest plots, which carry the event counts and group sizes used in the trial file; Figure 1 flowchart and Figure 8 funnel plots not used for numbers). Crossref and PubMed confirm author (Yavuz Güler), journal, volume 21, issue 1, pages 18-30, online date 2022-07-18, print date 2023-01-02. `source.file` is null (read online); `source.level` full-text.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bmg-closure-meta/notes.md:7:- Local guideline copies: `reports/audit-v2/sources-local/aua-urethral-stricture-2023-unabridged.txt`, `aua-stricture-amendment-2023.txt`, `eau-urethral-strictures-2026.txt` (searched for harvest-site closure).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bmg-closure-meta/notes.md:12:One report (the meta-analysis). No follow-up reports exist. `relations` is empty: no listed trial id has a real connection (the pooled Soave, Wong and Rourke trials are not on the site's trial list).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bmg-closure-meta/notes.md:16:- Aulia K, et al. Open Access Maced J Med Sci 2022;10:614 (DOI 10.3889/oamjms.2022.10650): a separate meta-analysis of five RCTs that EAU 2026 cites; not opened (not needed for this page), so not cited.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bmg-closure-meta/notes.md:22:The Guidelines and approvals section is omitted. AUA Urethral Stricture 2023 (PDF p. 19 narrative under Statement 19) says the donor site "may safely be left open to heal by secondary intention or closed primarily" and cites Rourke 2012 and Hwang 2022 (a five-RCT meta-analysis), not Güler. EAU Urethral Strictures 2026 section 9.5 gives no clear recommendation and cites Aulia 2022, Soave 2018 and Wong 2014, not Güler. Neither rests on this paper. No FDA item applies.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bmg-closure-meta/notes.md:26:The report's text and its forest plots disagree; the trial file uses the forest plots (they carry counts and give internally consistent intervals) and says so in each row note.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bmg-closure-meta/notes.md:33:- Closure technique: text says continuous closure in 3 studies but cites 4 references [20,21,23,25]; with 2 interrupted and 1 not stated the counts reach 6 or 7. The page says "most trials".
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bmg-closure-meta/notes.md:41:## Disagreements with WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bmg-closure-meta/notes.md:45:- `docs/01-foundations/surgical-principles/grafts/buccal-mucosa.mdx` line 42: "included seven studies, four using quasi-random allocation and one involving lingual grafts. It suggested less early pain with nonclosure" agrees with the source. Reference 21 gives `2023;21(1):18` (no end page; the pages are 18-30). Line 40 and the Hwang line (three RCTs in the main analysis, two quasi-randomized in sensitivity analysis) could not be checked: only the Hwang abstract was read (abstract says five studies, 346 randomized patients, 260 completed).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bmg-closure-meta/notes.md:46:- Pages citing Soave 2018 for "noninferior for oral pain" (`dorsal-onlay-omg.mdx` line 80, `bmg-onlay-ureter.mdx` lines 107 and 217, `mouth-retractors.mdx` line 100, `oral-cavity.mdx` line 75, `buccal-mucosa.mdx` line 40) were not checked against Soave (not opened). Güler's Figure 2A rates Soave low risk for sequence generation and allocation concealment but high risk for participant blinding, outcome blinding and attrition; it is the largest trial (135 of 373 patients).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bmg-closure-meta/notes.md:47:- No page states a number from Güler that disagrees with the source.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bmg-closure-meta/notes.md:49:## Disagreements with the old Journal Club record (`src/data/trials.ts` line 823)
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bmg-closure-meta/notes.md:51:- Authors "Güler et al.": single author (Yavuz Güler). Year 2022 is the online year; the issue is 2023 (the site page uses 2023).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bmg-closure-meta/notes.md:54:- "Settles a recurring intraoperative question with pooled randomized data": overstated. All seven trials were high risk for blinded outcome assessment, four were quasi-randomized, the donor sites were mixed, and the report's own conclusion is that nonclosure "may be recommended" for early pain only.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bmg-closure-meta/notes.md:59:- Per-trial follow-up and per-trial suture and hemostasis details (Table 1 text extracted from XML is flattened, so row-to-column assignment is ambiguous); the page makes only statements the report's running text supports.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/bmg-closure-meta/notes.md:61:- No PDF page numbers: locators use PMC figure and section labels.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:7:| Brubaker 2006 (primary, NEJM) | 10.1056/NEJMoa054208 / 16611949 | PubMed abstract (corrected). NEJM page and PDF return 403; not on PMC; no local copy (grep of `sources-local`, `~/Documents/Medicine`, `~/Downloads`). Details of design, endpoints and masking taken from the 2008 two-year paper (full text) | abstract |
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:15:DOIs and page ranges for the five reports and both errata were resolved on Crossref (titles, journals, volumes, pages and author lists match). NCT00065845 (CARE) taken from the 2008 papers; E-CARE is NCT00099372 (JAMA paper).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:17:Guidelines read locally: `reports/audit-v2/sources-local/aua-sui-2023-unabridged.txt` (statement 19; supporting text cites CARE as ref 103) and `eau-non-neurogenic-female-luts-2026.txt` (sections 4.7.3.b and 4.7.5; CARE is ref 669, the urodynamic analysis ref 638). `nice-ng123-ui-pop-women.txt` recommendation 1.8.3 ("Do not offer surgery to prevent incontinence in women having surgery for prolapse who do not have incontinence") does not cite CARE in the recommendation document, so it is omitted from the page. No FDA action applies.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:23:- The primary NEJM full text, its Table 3 and the two 2016 corrections were not read. The primary report is therefore recorded at abstract level (`status.sourceRead: abstract`, `met: true` justified by the abstract's statement that enrollment stopped at the first interim analysis for significantly less stress incontinence with Burch, restated in the Visco and Brubaker 2008 full texts).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:25:## Disagreements and cautions
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:29:- Record gives "23.8% vs 44.1% by symptoms; ~33.6% vs 57.4% by composite". The 23.8%/44.1% pair is the superseded original composite; the corrected 3-month composite is 33.6% versus 57.4% (PubMed abstract marks it "[Corrected]"). The abstract gives no "by symptoms" split, so no symptom-only 3-month percentage is carried into the file. (The 2008 manuscript Table 1 lists 3-month symptom rates of 19.0% versus 39.7%, but that table is the uncorrected manuscript with imputation and different denominators, so it was not used.)
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:30:- Record name "Colpopexy and Urinary Reduction Efforts": correct. Record "Long-term (Nygaard 2013)" and caveat that durability falls over 7 years: supported (anatomic failure 27%/22%, composite 48%/34% at year 7, intervals include zero).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:32:- Record bottom line "meaningfully lowers de novo stress incontinence in continent women": the trial endpoint is a composite (symptoms, stress test, treatment), not de novo symptomatic leakage alone; the file states the composite.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:34:### WARWIKI pages (not edited)
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:36:All numbers below were checked against the sources above; none disagree.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:38:- `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/burch-colposuspension.mdx` (line 76 region): 322 women, 33.6% versus 57.4% corrected composite, 23.8%/44.1% superseded. Consistent. The page's phrase "after correction of stress-test coding" could not be verified because the NEJM correction text was not accessible.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:39:- `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04g-prolapse-repair/apical/sacrocolpopexy.mdx` (lines 96, 110, 136): E-CARE 27% versus 22% anatomic, 48% versus 34% composite, 215 entered and 126 completed 7 years, erosion 10.5% (6.8 to 16.1), CARE composite 33.6% versus 57.4%. Consistent.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:43:- Surgeon pages (`linda-brubaker.mdx`, `anthony-visco.mdx`, `halina-zyczynski.mdx`) describe CARE correctly.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:45:### Source-internal discrepancies (not copied to the page)
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:47:- E-CARE eligible denominator: abstract says 215/233 (92%); main text and Figure 1 say 215/231 (93%). The file and page use 231 and record the 233 in the delivery fact note.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:48:- E-CARE abstract (PubMed) prints the SUI difference interval as "-0.268 to 0.030"; Table 2 gives (-0.268, -0.030). The file uses Table 2. The JAMA erratum (2013;310:1076) replaced the median time to SUI failure in the uncorrected manuscript (131.3 versus 40.2 months, per a previous site audit: 3.11 versus 0.94 years); neither time-to-failure value is used.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:50:- Brubaker 2008 manuscript Table 1 (uncorrected) shows 24-month composite 32.0% versus 45.2% (P = .026) and the JAMA 2013 introduction repeats those; the corrected PubMed abstract gives 41.8% versus 57.9% (P = .020). The file uses the corrected pair. Bothersome SUI (11.6% versus 25.2%), the urge endpoint and Table 4 counts come from the uncorrected manuscript; they do not depend on the stress-test component, but the correction text was not read.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:52:- The EAU recommendation not to offer concomitant anti-incontinence surgery at abdominal prolapse surgery (women without symptomatic or occult SUI) runs opposite to CARE's authors' conclusion (Brubaker 2008 proposes recommending prophylactic Burch to stress-continent women with a mobile urethra). The page states both without ranking.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:56:- NEJM primary full text (403); NEJM erratum text (403); Obstetrics and Gynecology correction text (paywalled; 402).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:57:- PMC manuscripts are author versions, so locators are table and section names, not journal page numbers; page numbers for `source.file` are null because all reading was online.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:58:- Whether the 2016 corrections changed any figure other than the stress incontinence composites (3-month and 24-month) is unknown.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:63:Sources read in full: Brubaker 2006 NEJM (`CARE-Brubaker-2006-NEJM.pdf`, 10 pages; this copy is the version updated June 9, 2016, so its text and tables carry the corrected values) and the NEJM correction of June 9, 2016 (`CARE-NEJM-Correction-2016.pdf`, p2297). The separate Obstetrics and Gynecology 2016 correction (reference 5) and the Brubaker et al. letter at NEJM p2295 were not supplied and remain unread.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:67:- Primary report `care-2006`: `source.level` full-text, checked 2026-10-19, `status.sourceRead` full-text. Locators are now journal pages and tables (pp1559-1565).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:69:- Corrected: the urge result was labeled "Urge incontinence frequency". Table 3 shows it is the composite urge outcome (50/153 vs 58/151); true urge incontinence is a bothersome-symptom row (10/147 vs 18/151, P = 0.18). Relabeled, denominators added.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:70:- Corrected: baseline description in Limitations ("94% white, mean age 62 +/- 10") came from the 2008 two-year paper; Table 1 of the primary gives age 62.4 +/- 9.7 vs 60.3 +/- 10.6 and white 96.2% vs 89.7%. Page now uses Table 1.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:73:- New report `care-2016-correction` (kind correction, doi 10.1056/NEJMx160015, PMID 27276579, full text read, no results). The correction text confirms the cause (a coding error related to the urinary stress test) and lists: composite 23.8/44.1 -> 33.6/57.4; Table 3 stress-test component 7/148 (4.7) and 14/162 (8.6), P = 0.14 -> 30/144 (20.8) and 65/160 (40.6), P < 0.001; treatment for urge outcome 2/153 (1.3), 5/152 (3.3), P = 0.27 -> 5/156 (3.2), 9/164 (5.5), P = 0.45; OR 0.41 (0.24-0.70) -> 0.40 (0.24-0.66); a no-leakage-on-reduction figure 38.2 -> 20.8 changed to 47.9 -> 22.9; the baseline-symptom exclusion figure 19.8 vs 41.3 -> 30.4 vs 54.8; the final Results paragraph (1-year data) omitted. This answers the earlier open question: the correction changed more than the two composite percentages. Earlier notes said it was unknown whether other figures changed.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:74:- Visco 2008 exploratory result: note added that it predates the correction.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:77:### Changed on the page
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:79:Design (exclusion by urethrovesical mobility, sealed envelopes, interim plan), Results (denominators, OR, components, subgroup, correction details, urge outcome naming), Surgery and treatment delivery (Burch technique, operative time, blood loss, paravaginal repair, hysterectomy, stage), Adverse events (3-month SAEs), Limitations (primary-report demographics, 19.2% baseline leakage, masking breaks, generalizability statement). The erosion paper's 83/322 concurrent hysterectomy and 228/322 prior hysterectomy figures were removed from page prose in favor of the primary report's Table 1 and text values, because of the disagreements below (the JSON keeps the erosion paper's values in its own report).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:81:### Disagreements the full text reveals
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:84:- Concurrent hysterectomy: primary reports 29.0% (Burch) and 28.0% (control), P = 1.00 (about 92 of 322); Cundiff 2008 reports 83/322 (25.8%). The two cannot both be right for the same denominator. Not resolved; the page uses the primary and the JSON keeps Cundiff's value in the erosion report. Anyone using the erosion odds ratio for hysterectomy (4.9) should know the count is the erosion paper's.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:85:- Visco 2008 (pre-correction) gives postoperative stress incontinence with a negative reduction test as 38% (no Burch) and 21% (Burch); the corrected primary gives 47.9% and 22.9% for the same group (data not shown). The two likely use different endpoint construction or the uncorrected coding; the Visco figures, and the sentence in `docs/03-clinical-conditions/03c-pelvic-support/pelvic-organ-prolapse.mdx` (line 322: 58% vs 38%, 32% vs 21%), may be affected by the correction. Not edited. Visco's 12/313 without reduction vs the primary's Table 2 3/153 + 9/159 = 12/312.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:86:- Urge result naming: the abstract's "frequency of urge incontinence (32.7% vs 38.4%)" is the composite urge outcome in Table 3. Pages that call this "urge incontinence" should say urge outcome or urge symptoms. Not found on other WARWIKI pages in a grep of CARE mentions.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:88:- `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/burch-colposuspension.mdx` says the 2016 correction changed the composite "after correction of stress-test coding": confirmed by the correction text. Its numbers (33.6% vs 57.4%) match. Other WARWIKI pages checked earlier (sacrocolpopexy, prolapse-repair-principles, mesh-complications, polypropylene) are consistent with the full text.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/care/notes.md:92:NEJM p2295 letter by Brubaker et al.; Obstetrics and Gynecology 2016 correction text; ClinicalTrials.gov record.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/artisan-snm/notes.md:7:| McCrery 2020, J Urol 203(1):185-192 (primary, 6 months) | DOI 10.1097/JU.0000000000000458, PMID 31347955 | PubMed abstract (eutils XML) plus Crossref metadata; publisher page auajournals.org returned 403, Unpaywall shows closed access, no repository copy found (Erasmus, Corewell, Maastricht pages list only the DOI link) | abstract only |
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/artisan-snm/notes.md:14:Authors, volume, issue, pages and dates for all four papers were checked against Crossref and PubMed (the first online date for the primary report is 2019-07-26 per PubMed; Crossref issue date is 2020-01). The status field says `abstract` because the primary report was not read in full. No local copy exists in `reports/audit-v2/sources-local`, `/Users/joyboy/Documents/Medicine` or Downloads (only an Axonics MRI guideline PDF).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/artisan-snm/notes.md:16:How the primary report's numbers were filled in: the abstract supplies n=129, 90% responders, 5.6 to 1.3 episodes per day, the 34-point ICIQ-OABqol gain and "no serious device related adverse events". The FDA SSED analyzes the same 6-month data (data lock 2019-01-18) and supplies the prespecified test (one-sided binomial against 50%, null rejected, 116/129 = 89.9%, 95% CI 83.4% to 94.5%), the SD-based table, enrollment (153 enrolled, 129 implanted; 15 US sites with 97 implanted, 5 Western European sites with 32), and the device- and procedure-related adverse-event tables. Those SSED-derived values sit in `note`, `locator` and two delivery/harm rows that name the SSED as the locator; the claim gate binds them to the primary DOI, so a reviewer holding the full J Urol text should confirm them. `met: true` for the primary result rests on the SSED statement, not on the abstract.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/artisan-snm/notes.md:23:- Fecal incontinence sub-outcomes (CCF-FIS 9.3 to 3.9 at 1 year, 3.7 at 2 years in 42 patients; satisfaction among self-identified patients) are in the 1- and 2-year papers but not in the file or page; add them if a bowel cross-reference is wanted.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/artisan-snm/notes.md:28:- The registry lists the primary outcome with a 2-year time frame, while the PMA and the first publication treat the 6-month as-treated responder rate as primary. The page states both; the file uses the 6-month rate as the primary result.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/artisan-snm/notes.md:31:- One relation only: `builds-on` insite (the 1-year paper explicitly contrasts its responder analysis, lead-migration and site-pain rates with InSite). The ROSETTA comparison appears only in the page prose.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/artisan-snm/notes.md:32:- No guideline statements: none of the local AUA OAB 2024, AUA/SUFU NLUTD 2021, EAU non-neurogenic female LUTS 2026, EAU neuro-urology 2026 or NICE NG123 copies cites ARTISAN-SNM or Axonics outcomes, so the guideline bullet is omitted; only the FDA approval (SSED p1, "Date of FDA Notice of Approval: November 13, 2019") is listed. Suggest an events.json entry (kind regulatory, body FDA, date 2019-11-13, decision oab-third-line, trial artisan-snm, url the accessdata.fda.gov PMA page https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/cdrh/cfdocs/cfpma/pma.cfm?id=P180046, which refused automated fetches, or the SSED PDF used here).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/artisan-snm/notes.md:34:## Disagreements with WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/artisan-snm/notes.md:36:Pages searched: `grep -rln ARTISAN docs`. Only the following discuss the trial.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/artisan-snm/notes.md:38:1. `docs/01-foundations/tools/biomaterials/neuromodulation-devices/axonics-snm.mdx`, line 12: "Early European experience (RELAX-OAB) preceded the US pivotal study (ARTISAN-SNM)". ARTISAN-SNM was not US-only: 32 of the 129 implanted patients were at five Western European sites (FDA SSED p33). Minor wording.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/artisan-snm/notes.md:39:2. Same page, table row "One year": "all participants able to recharge their device". The 1-year paper reports 100% of the 124 patients available at the visit (PMC7384085, Results, Recharging experience; Figure 5B), not all 129.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/artisan-snm/notes.md:40:3. All other ARTISAN numbers on that page match the sources: 129 participants, 98% women, nine prior-trial patients, about 90% / 89% / 93% of 121 completers / 88% of 129, 5.6 to 1.3 / 1.4 / 1.0, 34-point gain, 94% and 96% acceptability, 82% and 37% of 113 responders, 14 surgical interventions in 11 patients, 68.5% / 25.8% / 2.4% satisfaction, 9 of 14 late responders, 4.4-year historical battery life, sponsor statement.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/artisan-snm/notes.md:41:4. `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/sacral-neuromodulation.mdx` (ref 16 cited for the 4.4-year historical battery life) and `docs/07-roots/surgeons/h-r/kimberly-kenton.mdx` (68.5% of 124 very satisfied): both agree with the sources. No other page in `docs` names ARTISAN (the Pezzella hits in the botulinum pages are a different 2025 paper).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/artisan-snm/notes.md:43:## Disagreements with the old Journal Club record
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/artisan-snm/notes.md:46:- `design: 'Prospective multicenter trial'`: it is a single-arm, unblinded pivotal (IDE) study; FDA reports 153 enrolled and 129 implanted, with 20 sites (15 US, 5 Western European).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/artisan-snm/notes.md:48:- `result` (90% responders, 5.6 to 1.3, 34-point gain, no serious device-related events) matches the abstract. The "clinically meaningful 34-point" wording and the "bottomLine" battery comparison ("~15 years rather than ~4-5") rest on the abstract's 4.4-year historical figure and the manufacturer's at-least-15-year design life; neither is a measured result of the trial, and the page labels the 15 years as the manufacturer's rating.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/artisan-snm/notes.md:52:- Full text of the primary J Urol report (paywalled, 403 from auajournals.org, no repository copy): Methods detail (surgeon experience, exact sample-size rationale, per-site recruitment, antibiotic protocol), Table of baseline characteristics (Table S1 of the 1-year paper is a supplement), and every primary-report number beyond the abstract and the SSED.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/artisan-snm/notes.md:55:- FDA PMA database page (pma.cfm?id=P180046) returned a wait page to automated requests; the approval date and indication were taken from the SSED PDF.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/burgio-behavioral/notes.md:4:- Primary: Burgio 1998, JAMA 280(23):1995-2000, doi 10.1001/jama.280.23.1995, PMID 9863850. Full text (6-page PDF of the journal article) downloaded from the jacobimed.org curriculum folder and saved as `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/reports/audit-v2/sources-local/trials-2026-10-19/burgio-1998-jama.pdf` (the `source.file`). Every number in the primary report was read from `pdftotext` output of this PDF; Figure 3 bar values were checked against their n/N.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/burgio-behavioral/notes.md:5:- Burgio, Locher, Goode, JAGS 2000;48(4):370-374, doi 10.1111/j.1532-5415.2000.tb04692.x, PMID 10798461: PubMed abstract only (efetch XML). DOI resolved on Crossref (authors, journal, volume, pages match).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/burgio-behavioral/notes.md:9:- EAU Non-Neurogenic Female LUTS 2026 (local copy): cites this trial (ref 31) only for a bladder diary having a therapeutic benefit in section 3.3; no recommendation rests on it, so it is not on the page.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/burgio-behavioral/notes.md:13:- Burgio et al., JAMA 2002;288:2293-9 (behavioral training with and without biofeedback): a different trial. Burgio et al., JAMA Intern Med 2020 (combined behavioral and drug therapy in men): a different trial (cited on the OAB page and in the AUA/SUFU reference list), not in the site's trial list.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/burgio-behavioral/notes.md:20:- Goode 2002 abstract vs primary report, same 105-woman cystometry subsample: oxybutynin bladder-capacity change +68.9 mL vs +70.9 mL, placebo -6.0 vs -5.9 mL. Both are reported as printed; the page states the difference once.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/burgio-behavioral/notes.md:25:- Checker: `scripts/check-clinical-evidence.mjs` run on a scratch copy of the tree with this file added gave no error for burgio-behavioral (relation target `orbit` exists).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/burgio-behavioral/notes.md:27:## WARWIKI pages that cite this trial
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/burgio-behavioral/notes.md:28:- `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/behavioral-therapy-incontinence.mdx` line 82 (ref 7, correct citation: JAMA 1998;280(23):1995-2000). Its statement (197 women; 190 analyzed; 80.7%, 68.5% (P = .04), 39.4%) agrees with the source (PDF p3-4, Table 4). No disagreement found.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/burgio-behavioral/notes.md:29:- No other page under `docs` cites doi 10.1001/jama.280.23.1995. `urgency-incontinence-oab.mdx` cites Burgio 2020 (men), not this trial. `pelvic-floor-pt.mdx` cites Burgio 2002 (biofeedback vs behavioral training) and Goode 2011, different trials.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/ginsberg-ndo/notes.md:7:| Ginsberg 2012 J Urol 187:2131, DOI 10.1016/j.juro.2012.01.125, PMID 22503020 | Europe PMC REST record (abstract); Crossref metadata (authors, volume, pages); Unpaywall: closed access, no OA copy; not in PMC; no local copy found (grep of `reports/audit-v2/sources-local`, `~/Documents/Medicine`, `~/Downloads`) | abstract only |
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/ginsberg-ndo/notes.md:18:Could not open: the Ginsberg 2012 full text (paywalled, no local copy) and the Kennelly 2017 full text (Cloudflare). Everything in the trial file and page that says "abstract" rests on the PubMed abstract text. `met` is therefore null for the primary result, and no between-group differences, confidence intervals, baselines, PDF pages or adverse-event rates beyond those in the abstract are given. The trial's own injection technique (sites, volume, trigone, antibiotics, anesthesia) is not in the abstract, so the page gives the labeled technique and says so by attributing it to the label. Please run the full-text pass when the owner supplies the paper (J Urol 2012;187:2131-9).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/ginsberg-ndo/notes.md:26:The two extension abstracts say "a 52-week phase III trial" and do not name which; Kennelly 2013 states the extension integrated completers of both phase 3 trials. So the extension populations are probably not limited to this trial's patients. The page labels them "completers of a 52-week phase 3 trial", not "this trial's patients".
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/ginsberg-ndo/notes.md:35:## Disagreements with WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/ginsberg-ndo/notes.md:37:No numeric disagreements. Checked against the source:
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/ginsberg-ndo/notes.md:45:## Disagreements with the old Journal Club record
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/ginsberg-ndo/notes.md:48:- `bottomLine` "300 U adds risk, not efficacy": the abstract says CIC initiation "appeared to increase in a dose dependent fashion" and finds no clinically relevant efficacy or duration benefit; the page states this without "adds risk".
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/ginsberg-ndo/notes.md:50:- `n: 416`, MS/SCI, 14 or more episodes per week, primary endpoint, DOI: correct. "Phase-3 evidence behind FDA approval": the approval letter (2011-08-24) does not cite studies; the label's clinical-studies enrollment (691) equals this trial plus Cruz. The page says only that.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/ginsberg-ndo/notes.md:54:- Full-text pass: injection technique in the trial, exclusion criteria, analysis population, between-group differences and CIs, adverse-event rates and denominators, PDF pages for locators (all locators are "abstract").
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/empowur/notes.md:7:| Staskin 2020, J Urol 204(2):316-324 (primary) | doi 10.1097/JU.0000000000000807, PMID 32068484 | PubMed abstract and Crossref metadata. Full text is on auajournals.org (not opened: AUA bars AI use of its site; the resolver returned 403). No PMC copy and no local copy. | abstract only |
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/empowur/notes.md:18:| FDA Drugs@FDA, NDA 213006 | accessdata.fda.gov overview page | original approval 12/23/2020; supplement 11 (12/18/2024) "Efficacy-New Indication" | approval date |
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/empowur/notes.md:20:DOIs, volumes, pages and author lists were checked against Crossref and PubMed (all five citations match). NCT numbers were taken from the registry and the PubMed data bank field.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/empowur/notes.md:24:- `status.sourceRead` is `abstract` because the primary report's full text could not be opened. Every primary-report number comes from the abstract, except these, which come from other documents and are located as such in the file: the 95% CIs and exact P values for the primary and key secondary endpoints, the arm-level n, the sex-adjusted responder percentages, and the 100%-reduction endpoint (all ClinicalTrials.gov NCT03492281 results); volume voided per micturition (label Table 3, matching registry 23.5 versus 2.2 mL); and the harms other than discontinuation and hypertension (Varano 2021 Table 3, registry adverse events). A reviewer with the NEJM-style PDF should recheck these against the paper (labels: PDF page or table).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/empowur/notes.md:34:- COURAGE (J Urol 2024): has its own trial file; used only as a relation target (label states Study 3005 is a 24-week placebo-controlled trial in men with OAB on BPH therapy; the identification of "COURAGE" with Study 3005 rests on the site's trial list, not on a document I opened).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/empowur/notes.md:36:## Disagreements with other WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/empowur/notes.md:42:- Line 83, "506 EMPOWUR completers entered active treatment": placebo completers were re-randomized 1:1 to vibegron or tolterodine and the extension also had a tolterodine arm; 505 were treated. Not wrong, but the extension comparator (tolterodine) is not mentioned.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/empowur/notes.md:43:- No other page states an EMPOWUR number.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/empowur/notes.md:45:## Disagreements with the old Journal Club record (`src/data/trials.ts`)
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/empowur/notes.md:54:- Registry lists 215 locations; Varano 2021 reports 199 sites. The page uses 199.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/empowur/notes.md:63:- FDA indication text at the 2020 approval (the page cites only the current label for the indication wording and Drugs@FDA for the approval date).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/empowur/notes.md:75:- 75% urge incontinence responders: values now the paper's unadjusted 52.4%, 36.8% and 47.6% (P<0.0001). The file had the registry's sex-adjusted 49.3%, 32.8%, 42.2% and difference 16.5 (9.7 to 23.4); the paper does not print the difference, so the effect is `none` and the registry figures sit in the note and the page, attributed to the registry.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/empowur/notes.md:78:- Enrollment dates: the page said enrollment ran March 2018 to February 2019. The paper says randomization began March 26, 2018 and the last patient completed 12 weeks on January 7, 2019; the February 2019 end appears to be a registry date. Page now uses the paper's dates.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/empowur/notes.md:86:- Delivery (6 new): study periods and double-dummy design, stratification, enrollment and sites, analysis sets, sample size, baseline values (11.5 micturitions, 8.1 urgency episodes, 150.5 mL, 3.5 urge incontinence episodes), statistical handling (MMRM, CMH, multiple imputation, hierarchy, nominal P for supportive comparisons).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/empowur/notes.md:87:- Page: design (washout, run-in, hierarchy, power), baseline, the added results and the expanded adverse events paragraph.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/empowur/notes.md:93:### Disagreements with other WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/empowur/notes.md:95:None new. `docs/01-foundations/pharmacology/storage-oab/beta3-agonists.mdx` line 79 ("Volume per void: P<0.001") differs from the paper (P<0.0001 for vibegron versus placebo; P<0.001 is the tolterodine comparison). The same page's "506 EMPOWUR completers entered active treatment" is the extension report, not this paper. Hypertension in people 65 and older: the authors' reply reports 3.1% placebo, 1.2% vibegron, 2.9% tolterodine, matching Varano 2021 as used.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/darouiche/notes.md:4:- Primary report: Darouiche RO, et al. N Engl J Med 2010;362(1):18-26, DOI 10.1056/NEJMoa0810988, PMID 20054046, NCT00290290. FULL TEXT read (all 9 PDF pages, journal pp18-26, Tables 1-4, Figures 1-2) from an NEJM-formatted PDF mirror at https://cirugia.facmed.unam.mx/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/Chlorhexidina-vs-Iodopovidona.pdf (downloaded to the session scratchpad, text extracted with pdftotext; not in sources-local or PMC; nejm.org returned 403). `source.file` is null in the trial file (read online). PDF p = journal p minus 17. The Supplementary Appendix (Tables 1-5: ITT/per-protocol detail, antibiotic type, risk factors, microbiology) was NOT available and not read.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/darouiche/notes.md:5:- DOI/PMID/authors/volume/pages verified on Crossref (api.crossref.org) and PubMed efetch (PMID 20054046): 12 authors, 362(1):18-26, published 2010-01-07.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/darouiche/notes.md:6:- WHO Global Guidelines for the Prevention of Surgical Site Infection, 2nd ed., 2018, full text (PDF via medbox.org mirror; WHO IRIS and NCBI Bookshelf were behind a captcha). Read section 4.7 (recommendation text, rationale, evidence summary, reference 22 = this trial). Publication URL https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241550475 returns 200.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/darouiche/notes.md:7:- CDC/HICPAC guideline, JAMA Surg 2017;152(8):784-791 (PDF at vumc.org). Read statement 8B ("Perform intraoperative skin preparation with an alcohol-based antiseptic agent unless contraindicated", Category IA, high-quality evidence). The article text I could read does not cite or discuss this trial (rationale is in an eAppendix I did not read), so it is NOT on the page.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/darouiche/notes.md:8:- Local guideline copies searched (reports/audit-v2/sources-local): EAU Urological Infections 2026 (sec 3.18.1.b: "weak evidence ... skin preparation using alcoholic solutions or chlorhexidine result in a lower rate of SSI than iodine solutions", cites Cochrane Dumville 2015, ref 604, not this trial), AUA best practice policy statement on antimicrobial prophylaxis 2019 (mentions chlorhexidine-alcohol skin prep as a non-antibiotic measure, cites Tanner hair-removal review, not this trial), ASHP 2013 (no mention). None cites the trial, so none is on the page.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/darouiche/notes.md:15:## Disagreements and checks against the OLD JOURNAL CLUB RECORD
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/darouiche/notes.md:19:- "Excluded contaminated/dirty cases": the paper restricts eligibility to clean-contaminated surgery; the listed exclusions are allergy to chlorhexidine/alcohol/iodophors, infection at or near the site, and inability to follow for 30 days. Twenty-five randomized patients turned out to have clean surgery and were dropped from the per-protocol analysis only. Contaminated/dirty exclusion is implied by eligibility, not stated as an exclusion criterion.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/darouiche/notes.md:20:- "alcohol-based preps require dry time and fire-safety precautions": the paper mentions only the small risk of fire or chemical burn with flammable alcohol products (none occurred); it says nothing about dry time. Dry-time statements belong to WHO/CDC/ACOG, not this trial. Not carried onto the page.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/darouiche/notes.md:24:## Disagreements with other WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/darouiche/notes.md:25:- No docs page cites or reports numbers from this trial (grep for the DOI, PMID, "Darouiche" 2010 and "chlorhexidine-alcohol" found none that report it; other Darouiche hits are different papers: bacterial interference, short vs long CAUTI antibiotics, penile prosthesis antibiotic treatment).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/darouiche/notes.md:26:- `docs/01-foundations/perioperative-care/intraoperative-care/draping-skin-antisepsis.mdx` row "Intact abdominal skin" says "WHO's own Recommendation 12 is a strong recommendation, low-to-moderate quality evidence". The WHO 2018 (2nd ed.) text numbers this recommendation as section 4.7 and I could not find a "Recommendation 12" label in that edition; the strength and quality wording match. Check whether the page means the 2016 first edition. Also the same page says earlier comparisons favoring chlorhexidine-alcohol over aqueous povidone-iodine "do not isolate the effect of chlorhexidine from that of alcohol": consistent with this trial's design (alcohol applicator scrub vs aqueous scrub and paint) and with the Limitations section written here.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/darouiche/notes.md:27:- The page `src/data/trials.ts` (old Journal Club) entry is the "old record" above.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/darouiche/notes.md:30:- Discussion states a number needed to treat of "approximately 17"; 1/(0.161 - 0.095) is about 15. Left out of the file and page rather than propagate it.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/darouiche/notes.md:33:- `status.sourceRead` is full-text; claimsChecked and clinicalReview are null. The trial file passed `scripts/check-clinical-evidence.mjs` when copied into a scratch copy of the data tree with the existing trial files (47 families, 0 errors); the MDX page was not built or linted in the repo (no edits outside OUT_DIR).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/improve/notes.md:7:| Rahn et al., *JAMA* 2023;330(7):615-625, DOI 10.1001/jama.2023.12317, PMID 37581673 | [JAMA full text](https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/fullarticle/2808329); [PubMed record](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37581673/) | Full text | PubMed and publisher agree on authors, year, journal, volume, issue, pages, DOI and PMID. No local article copy identified. |
