reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/improve/notes.md:10:The local `reports/audit-v2/sources-local` DOI search found audit logs mentioning the paper, but no article text or PDF. Filename searches in `~/Documents/Medicine` and `~/Downloads` found no matching Rahn or IMPROVE copy. PMC article pages presented a browser check, so the accessible JAMA publisher full text was used for the primary report. DOI redirects were inaccessible through the web tool; PubMed and publisher records supplied the metadata checks.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/improve/notes.md:14:- Rahn et al., "Effects of Preoperative Intravaginal Estrogen on Pelvic Floor Disorder Symptoms in Postmenopausal Women with Pelvic Organ Prolapse," *Am J Obstet Gynecol.* 2023;229(3):309.e1-309.e10, DOI 10.1016/j.ajog.2023.05.023, PMID 37244454: a preoperative symptom ancillary analysis. The PMC page challenged the browser, the publisher page blocked access, and the PubMed page did not return readable content. Search snippets alone were not treated as an opened report. Its findings were not entered into the trial file or appraisal.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/improve/notes.md:15:- Rahn et al., "Design of a Randomized Clinical Trial of Perioperative Vaginal Estrogen Versus Placebo with Transvaginal Native Tissue Apical Prolapse Repair," *Female Pelvic Med Reconstr Surg.* 2021;27(1):e227-e233, DOI 10.1097/SPV.0000000000000899: protocol/design paper, without surgical outcomes; the primary report provides the implemented methods. The PMC page challenged the browser.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/improve/notes.md:16:- No guideline or regulatory section was added to the page: the guideline material encountered addresses prolapse or GSM generally, rather than a recommendation citing or resting on IMPROVE. No FDA approval or clearance was checked for this trial's perioperative use.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/improve/notes.md:18:## Disagreements and source details
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/improve/notes.md:22:- **WARWIKI `docs/01-foundations/pharmacology/hormonal-therapies/preoperative-hormonal-priming.mdx`, 12-month results table, “Failure components | Anatomic recurrence was the most common component, often partial apical descent without prolapse beyond the hymen or symptoms”:** the JAMA report identifies anatomical recurrence as the most common primary failure component, but discusses the predominance of partial, asymptomatic apical descent in **one per-protocol analysis**, not as a breakdown of all primary-analysis failures. JAMA full text, Results/Primary Outcome and Discussion. The page should keep that distinction if revised.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/improve/notes.md:23:- **Primary article's own discrepancy:** JAMA Table 2 gives placebo suture exposure as 15/87 (17%) after month 6, while the article prose says 15/97 (17%); 15/97 is 15.5%. The trial file follows the table's explicit assessment denominator. JAMA full text, Table 2 and Results/Adverse Events.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/improve/notes.md:24:- No other confirmed source disagreement was found on the WARWIKI pages returned by the exact `IMPROVE` search. The separate `IMPROVE-multi` blood-pressure trial was excluded from this comparison.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/improve/notes.md:29:- The primary report's 19% and 9% are time-to-event model estimates. They are not 20/93 and 10/93. Numerical percentages attached to raw counts in the data and page were recalculated from their stated denominators.
