reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/esteem/notes.md:5:1. **Primary report:** Sung et al., *JAMA* 2019;322(11):1066–1076, DOI [10.1001/jama.2019.12467](https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2019.12467), PMID [31529007](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31529007/). Opened the [publisher's full text](https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/fullarticle/2749872), including Methods, Tables 1–4, Figure 1, and adverse-event text. No local copy of this report was found by DOI/acronym search in `reports/audit-v2/sources-local` or author/acronym filename search in `Documents/Medicine` and `Downloads`. The publisher citation supplies author order, online date, journal, volume, issue, pages, and DOI; PubMed identifies the PMID. `report.source.file` is therefore null.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/esteem/notes.md:6:2. **Corrected planned secondary analysis:** Sung et al., *Obstet Gynecol* 2021;138(2):199–207, DOI [10.1097/AOG.0000000000004444](https://doi.org/10.1097/AOG.0000000000004444), PMID [34237755](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34237755/). Opened the PubMed citation and abstract. PMC's page surfaced in search but its full-text open returned a browser challenge, so the report is marked `abstract` and only abstract-supported findings are used. PubMed confirms author order, journal, year, volume, issue, pages, DOI, PMID, and replacement status. `report.source.file` is null.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/esteem/notes.md:10:- Sung et al., *Obstet Gynecol* 2020;136(3):482–491, DOI `10.1097/AOG.0000000000003989`, PMID `32769647`: **retracted and replaced** by the 2021 report after missing Valsalva leak point pressures were miscoded in the predictive model. The [retraction/replacement notice](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34293762/) has PMID `34293762`; the [PMC indexed notice](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8489382/) explains the error. Its predictors were not carried into the trial file.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/esteem/notes.md:11:- Harvie et al., ESTEEM cost-effectiveness analysis: economic evaluation rather than an additional clinical-outcome cutoff; not needed for the surgical efficacy and complications appraisal. Identified by [PMC record](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8633051/) but not opened in full, so it is not a trial report here.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/esteem/notes.md:12:- ESTEEM microbiome, postoperative weight-change, and treatment-protocol reports: mechanistic, cross-trial, or methods analyses without a new randomized clinical endpoint relevant to this page. They were found in publication searches but not opened as trial reports.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/esteem/notes.md:17:- Read local `reports/audit-v2/sources-local/aua-sui-2023-unabridged.txt` and `reports/audit-v2/sources-local/eau-non-neurogenic-female-luts-2026.txt`. They contain recommendations for conservative management and surgery in stress or mixed urinary incontinence, but no statement located in these copies cites or rests specifically on ESTEEM. The page therefore has no “Guidelines and approvals” section. No FDA claim is made.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/esteem/notes.md:19:## Existing WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/esteem/notes.md:21:Exact-acronym search found these pages:
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/esteem/notes.md:23:- `docs/03-clinical-conditions/03a-storage-incontinence/mixed-incontinence.mdx`, line 138: reports a 13.4-point UDI difference and 35-point clinical-importance threshold, with fewer additional treatments. These agree with the primary report's Table 2, Methods, and Table 3. Its positive CI `1.0–25.9` is the magnitude of the report's signed difference `−25.9 to −1.0`, so no substantive disagreement was found.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/esteem/notes.md:27:No number or statement on those pages was found to disagree with the opened primary report. Unrelated lowercase “self-esteem” matches were excluded from this trial search.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/esteem/notes.md:38:- The primary article does not give a single data-cutoff date; `cutoff` is null.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/esteem/notes.md:39:- The 2021 secondary abstract reports `56/379` as `14.7%`; direct one-decimal rounding gives `14.8%`. The trial file and page retain the fraction only. The corrected report's full tables were not accessible in this pass.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/mtops/notes.md:7:| McConnell 2003, NEJM 349:2387 (primary) | doi 10.1056/NEJMoa030656; PMID 14681504 | Unofficial scanned copy of the NEJM PDF on farm.ucl.ac.be (Université catholique de Louvain course site), fetched with WebFetch and saved by the tool to `/Users/joyboy/.claude/projects/-Users-joyboy-Documents-WARWIKI-warwiki/9d03f117-3fc6-4067-a0c4-5962e10dc9cb/tool-results/webfetch-1792414446085-5ldb1e.pdf`; text extracted with pdftotext, Table 3 read from a rendered page image (rotated table). nejm.org returned 403; no copy in `reports/audit-v2/sources-local`, `/Users/joyboy/Documents/Medicine` or Downloads. | Full text (12 pages, journal pp2387-2398) |
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/mtops/notes.md:14:All six DOIs resolved on Crossref and every PMID on PubMed (E-utilities) with matching authors, year, journal, volume and pages. The four abstract-level secondary reports (2006, 2013, 2014, 2016) have no numbers in their abstracts, so their results carry qualitative notes and no `values`; Kaplan 2011 was read in full.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/mtops/notes.md:23:- MTOPS-cohort comparisons with minimally invasive therapy (McVary 2018 and Gupta 2018 water vapor therapy, Roehrborn 2022 prostatic urethral lift, Long 2025 and Han 2026 Rezum): they borrow the MTOPS arms as a comparator cohort rather than report MTOPS; not opened. They could be added as relations if the Rezum or other BPH trials get pages.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/mtops/notes.md:26:## Disagreements and cautions
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/mtops/notes.md:35:6. AUA 2026 unabridged text (rationale under statement 24) describes MTOPS as a 4-year trial that enrolled men with an IPSS of 8 or more, prostate volume of 30 cc or more and Qmax of 15 mL per second or less, and says clinical outcomes were "similar between combination therapy and finasteride". The paper gives mean follow-up of 4.5 years, an AUA symptom score of 8 to 35 (8 to 30 in the full-scale study), Qmax 4 to 15 mL per second with voided volume of at least 125 mL, no minimum prostate volume, and combination was better than finasteride for overall progression (P<0.001) though not for retention or invasive therapy. The page does not repeat the guideline's description.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/mtops/notes.md:37:### WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/mtops/notes.md:39:Pages searched: `grep -rln "MTOPS"` and "doxazosin" across `docs`. Every MTOPS number on these pages matches the paper (3,047 men; mean 4.5 years; 39%, 34% and 66% risk reductions; only finasteride and combination reduced retention and invasive therapy; the volume subgroup statements). Minor items:
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/mtops/notes.md:40:- `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/docs/01-foundations/pharmacology/voiding-outlet/5-alpha-reductase-inhibitors.mdx`, reference 7: "Long-term doxazosin, finasteride and combination therapy: MTOPS" is an abbreviated title, not the published title ("The long-term effect of doxazosin, finasteride, and combination therapy on the clinical progression of benign prostatic hyperplasia"); the DOI is right. Same page, volume sentence: the finding for under 25 mL is from the abstract of Kaplan 2006 (doxazosin alone was as good as combination), consistent with the file.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/mtops/notes.md:41:- `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/docs/01-foundations/pharmacology/voiding-outlet/alpha-blockers.mdx` ("Relative reductions in the composite ... 39% ... 34% ... 66%"; "Finasteride and combination therapy, but not doxazosin alone, reduced acute retention and invasive treatment"), `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/docs/03-clinical-conditions/03b-voiding-outlet/bladder-outlet-obstruction.mdx` (table row for alpha blocker + 5-ARI; "MTOPS · 2003" timeline) and `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/src/data/evidence-registry.json` (`mtops-2003`: 4.5, 2.7, 2.9, 1.5 per 100 person-years) agree with the source. Registry "Men at least 50 years with symptomatic BPH and reduced urinary flow" is consistent with eligibility.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/mtops/notes.md:46:No number conflicts: n 3,047, NEJM 2003, doi 10.1056/NEJMoa030656, 66% reduction with combination, acute retention and surgery reduced by the 5-ARI (finasteride and combination). Differences in framing, not corrected on the site: (a) the old text says the combination was best "in men with larger prostates"; the trial enrolled men with no minimum prostate size (median 31.0 mL), and the volume-stratified benefit comes from later analyses (Kaplan 2006: combination no better than doxazosin below 25 mL); (b) "Combination therapy ... outperforming either agent alone" applies to overall progression and symptom score; for retention and invasive therapy the combination and finasteride alone were similar and doxazosin did not reduce them; (c) the old record carries no PMID; the correct PMID is 14681504 (12944571, which a first lookup returned, is a different NEJM paper, Grossman 2003 on neoadjuvant cystectomy, and is not used).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/mtops/notes.md:50:- Official NEJM page (403). The primary text comes from an unofficial scan of the version of record; page numbers in locators are journal pages 2387 to 2398 (PDF p1 = journal p2387).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/mtops/notes.md:53:- The EAU statement about 1-year MTOPS results (read in `eau-male-luts-2026.txt`) cites reference 66 (McConnell 2003) for one-year data; the primary report does not present separate 1-year efficacy analyses except Table 3 year-1 rows, so that statement is attributed to EAU on the page, not to the trial.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/master/notes.md:5:1. **Primary, full text:** Abrams P, Constable LD, Cooper D, et al. *Eur Urol.* 2021;79(6):812–823. DOI [10.1016/j.eururo.2021.01.024](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eururo.2021.01.024); PMID [33551297](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33551297/). Read the owner's PDF at `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/reports/audit-v2/sources-local/pdfs-2026-10-06/master-abrams-2021-eur-urol.pdf` with its local text extraction. The PubMed record confirms author order, online date (February 4, 2021), journal, volume, issue, and pages.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/master/notes.md:6:2. **Full trial and economic report, full text:** Constable L, Abrams P, Cooper D, et al. *Health Technol Assess.* 2022;26(36):1–152. DOI [10.3310/TBFZ0277](https://doi.org/10.3310/TBFZ0277); PMID [35972773](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35972773/). Read the [184-page NCBI Bookshelf PDF](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK583141/pdf/Bookshelf_NBK583141.pdf), particularly journal pp 3, 19–31, 46–47. No local copy read. PubMed and the report confirm author order, August 2022 publication, journal, volume, issue, and pages. The exact publication day was not given; August 1 is marked approximate in the trial file.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/master/notes.md:7:3. **24-month journal update, abstract:** Constable L, Drake MJ, Cooper D, et al. *Eur Urol Focus.* 2026;12(4):636–643. DOI [10.1016/j.euf.2026.07.012](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.euf.2026.07.012); PMID [42648940](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42648940/). Read the [PubMed abstract](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42648940/) and publisher search excerpt. PubMed confirms August 26, 2026 online date, September issue, authors, volume, and pages. The ScienceDirect full article returned 403; no local copy was found. The fuller 2022 report supplies counts and operative detail for this same 24-month timepoint.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/master/notes.md:9:Guideline opened: local `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/reports/audit-v2/sources-local/eau-male-luts-2026.txt`, pp 72–75 (§5.6.5), including its explicit discussion of MASTER and its **Weak**, **Weak**, and **Strong** recommendations. I also read `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/reports/audit-v2/sources-local/aua-ipt-2024-unabridged.txt`, pp 3 and 19–22 (statements 17–24); it does not cite the MASTER report by name or DOI, so it was not used for a trial-linked recommendation on this page. Neither AUA website nor an FDA source was opened.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/master/notes.md:19:- The 2021 article's **abstract** prints the adjusted difference as `+3.6%` with a CI of `−11.6 to 4.6`; its Results and Table 2 give **−3.6 percentage points** for the sling-minus-AUS *continence* difference. The 2022 full report re-estimates the same endpoint at **−3.4 points** (95% CI −11.7 to 4.8). The trial file and page use the 2021 Results/Table 2 value for the primary report and preserve the difference in the file note. Source: Abrams 2021 PDF pp 812, 814, 817; HTA PDF p 53, Table 7.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/master/notes.md:20:- The 2021 article records **6 sling events in 6 men** and **13 AUS events in 11 men** among 180 and 173 operated men. The full 2022 report records **8 events in 8 sling men** and **15 events in 13 AUS men** among 180 and 175 operated men, adding two sling mesh erosions and two AUS-arm operations. These are report-version differences. Source: Abrams 2021 PDF pp 817–819, Table 3/Figure 1; HTA PDF pp 49–50, Tables 5–6.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/master/notes.md:23:## Disagreements with existing WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/master/notes.md:25:- `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/male-sui/male-stress-incontinence-database.mdx:54` says: “In the 2021 article, 6 sling and 13 AUS recipients had serious adverse events.” The 2021 article reports 6 sling **recipients** but 13 AUS **events in 11 recipients**. Locator: Abrams 2021 PDF pp 817–819, Surgery and AEs/Table 3. The same page's immediately following description of the 2022 report's 8/180 and 13/175 **recipients** (15 AUS events) is correct.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/master/notes.md:26:- The other seven pages with `MASTER` in their body were checked: `docs/01-foundations/tools/biomaterials/prosthetics/artificial-urinary-sphincter.mdx`, `docs/03-clinical-conditions/03a-storage-incontinence/sui-male.mdx`, `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/index.mdx`, and `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/{adjustable-continence-devices,advance-sling,artificial-urinary-sphincter,male-urethral-slings}.mdx`. No further numerical disagreement with the opened reports was identified. The rounded 24-month further-surgery figures of 11% versus 2% correspond to 20/190 (10.5%) versus 4/190 (2.1%) in the HTA report.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/labrie/notes.md:5:- Primary report, Labrie J, et al. N Engl J Med 2013;369:1124-1133, doi 10.1056/NEJMoa1210627, PMID 24047061: full text read at https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa1210627 (Browser pane; WebFetch and curl were blocked with 403). Tables 1-4 and Figure 1 were read as the NEJM images (`.../assets/images/large/nejmoa1210627_t1..t4.jpg`, `_f1.jpg`). Every Result, Harm and Fact number comes from the article text or Tables 2-4 and Figure 1; locators are table, figure or section names (no PDF was available, so no page numbers). Crossref confirms volume, issue, pages and online date 2013-09-19; PubMed abstract (efetch) matches the full text. `source.file` is null.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/labrie/notes.md:9:- Validation: `scripts/check-clinical-evidence.mjs` run on a copy of the data folder with the new trial and page added; no errors for `labrie`. Every percentage recounted against its numerator and denominator; every percentage in the page also appears in the trial file.
