reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/insite/notes.md:25:## Disagreements with other WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/insite/notes.md:26:- No WARWIKI page discusses the randomized InSite comparison (61% versus 42%) at all; `docs/01-foundations/tools/biomaterials/neuromodulation-devices/medtronic-interstim.mdx` line 69 and `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/oab-uui/oab-uui-database.mdx` line 41 cite only the 5-year paper. Their numbers match the 5-year abstract (340/272; 67% and 82%; 2.0 leaks and 5.4 voids; 22%/15%/13%). No disagreement.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/insite/notes.md:27:- Statements in the local guideline copies that disagree with the InSite abstract (guideline text, not WARWIKI pages):
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/insite/notes.md:28:  - AUA/SUFU 2024, Sacral neuromodulation versus antimuscarinic medications paragraph (PDF p31): "Eighty-six percent of OAB patients randomized to SNM were responders". The abstract's 86% is the share reporting improved or greatly improved urinary symptom interference; ITT success was 61%. Same paragraph: continence rates 39% versus 21% (p=0.06) and a one-year implant site infection of 4% / permanent explant 5% could not be checked against an abstract; the 12-month AE paper abstract gives implant site infection 3% (9/272).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/insite/notes.md:29:  - EAU non-neurogenic female LUTS 2026, section 4.1.4.c.2: says InSite showed adverse events "none were serious", "higher rates of UTIs", and "implant site infection and lead migration in 3.4% of cases each"; none of this is in the abstracts I read (the 12-month paper says only one device-related AE was serious).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/insite/notes.md:33:- Report ids follow the citation year (insite-2015, insite-2017, insite-2018); `published` is the online date (2014-01-10 from Europe PMC/Unpaywall; 2016-08-04 and 2017-07-11 from PubMed ArticleDate).
