reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/open/notes.md:13:DOIs and bibliographic details of all three reports and the AUA amendment were checked against Crossref and PubMed (authors, year, journal, volume, issue, pages). Online dates: Eur Urol epub 2020-07-04 (PubMed ArticleDate); HTA Crossref/PMC give month only (2020 Nov), so the file uses 2020-11-01 with `dateApprox`; BMC Urol 2021-05-03. Registry: ISRCTN98009168 (no NCT number, so `nct` is null and `registry` is set); the registry page itself was not opened.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/open/notes.md:24:## Disagreements and inconsistencies in the sources (not WARWIKI)
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/open/notes.md:27:- Primary report, internal: the Discussion gives serious adverse events of 10.9% versus 11.3%; Table 4 gives 7/82 (8.5%) versus 10/115 (8.7%). The file and page use Table 4 (the EAU guideline also gives 8.5% and 8.7%).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/open/notes.md:28:- Primary report, internal: Results says 1,262 men were identified; supplementary Table 1 totals 1,249 (page uses 1,262, the text figure).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/open/notes.md:30:- Primary report, internal: the sexual-function scale is 1 (very dissatisfied) to 5 (very satisfied) in Methods but reversed in the Table 1 footnote. The file records the direction conflict and the page states only that the groups did not differ.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/open/notes.md:33:- The primary report abstract (accepted manuscript) says "ICIQ voiding symptom score"; the published PubMed abstract and Methods say the voiding symptom score from the stricture-surgery PROM (Jackson 2011). The page uses the latter.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/open/notes.md:34:- AUA guideline discussion (2023): describes OPEN as enrolling men with stricture "<2 cm in length"; the primary report has no length criterion (mean length 2.0 and 1.7 cm, recorded in 130 of 220 men). The same text states recurrence as "39% experienced a recurrence versus 19%", but the paper reports 39 and 19 men (39/112 = 34.8%; 19/108 = 17.6%). The page does not repeat either point.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/open/notes.md:37:## Disagreements with WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/open/notes.md:41:- `docs/03-clinical-conditions/03b-voiding-outlet/urethral-stricture.mdx` (lines 322, 356, 368, 422) and `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/minimally-invasive/dviu.mdx` (lines 122, 139-150): statements (similar symptom improvement, fewer reinterventions, HR 0.52 with 95% CI 0.31-0.89, higher cost) agree with the sources. The dviu.mdx row "Cost over 24 months ... Urethrotomy appeared more cost-effective" agrees with the BMC Urol analysis. No numeric disagreement found. Both pages cite the monograph (hta24610) for the Eur Urol results as well; the monograph abstract confirms HR 0.52 (0.31-0.89) and the two-versus-five complication figure only.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/open/notes.md:44:## Disagreements with the old Journal Club record
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/open/notes.md:55:- HTA monograph full text (captcha/403/500); only its abstract was read. Surgeon volume, technique standardization, catheter duration, length of stay and other operative detail that the monograph may contain are not on the page.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/open/notes.md:56:- Typeset Eur Urol page numbers for tables (AM PDF locators used).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/open/notes.md:58:- The EAU recommendation on repeated DVIU (strong) is quoted from the local guideline copy; its supporting references are [240] and [241] in that document, not OPEN, so the page presents it as a related recommendation, not as resting on OPEN. The AUA statement 11a discussion explicitly cites OPEN.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/opus/notes.md:5:1. **Wei JT, et al., 2012, primary randomized report.** Full text read through the indexed [PMC article](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3433843/) (especially Methods, Results, Tables 2 and 4, and Figure 1); no local source file was found. DOI [10.1056/NEJMoa1111967](https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1111967); PMID [22716974](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22716974/). PubMed confirms authors, *N Engl J Med*, 2012;366(25):2358-2367 and June 21 publication.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/opus/notes.md:6:2. **Jelovsek JE, et al., 2014, OPUS-derived risk model.** Full text read through the indexed [PMC article](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3906626/) (Methods, Results, Figures 1–4); no local source file was found. DOI [10.1097/AOG.0000000000000094](https://doi.org/10.1097/AOG.0000000000000094); PMID [24402598](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24402598/). PubMed confirms authors, *Obstet Gynecol*, 2014;123(2 Pt 1):279-287. PubMed identifies the February 2014 issue; the exact online publication day was not verified, so the trial file marks its February 1 timeline date approximate.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/opus/notes.md:8:The source search covered `reports/audit-v2/sources-local` text files, filenames in `/Users/joyboy/Documents/Medicine` and `/Users/joyboy/Downloads`, and online full text. No owner-held OPUS report PDF or text was identified. The PMC pages returned a browser challenge when opened directly in the web tool, but the indexed full-text search returned the article methods, results and tables used here. The report `source.file` fields are therefore null, with `source.level` set to `full-text`.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/opus/notes.md:12:- Local AUA/SUFU 2023 SUI unabridged copy: `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/reports/audit-v2/sources-local/aua-sui-2023-unabridged.txt`, statement 19 and discussion on printed pages 23–24. The copy cites Wei as reference 104. No `auanet.org` page was opened.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/opus/notes.md:13:- Local ACOG/AUGS Practice Bulletin 214: `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/reports/audit-v2/sources-local/pdfs-2026-10-09/pelvic-organ-prolapse-acog-practice-bulletin-number-214.txt`, recommendations on printed page e136, under Level B. DOI [10.1097/AOG.0000000000003519](https://doi.org/10.1097/AOG.0000000000003519), PMID [31651832](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31651832/).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/opus/notes.md:15:- [NICE NG123 recommendations](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng123/chapter/recommendations), published 2019, recommendations 1.8.3–1.8.4. NICE conveys strength through its recommendation verbs rather than a letter grade; the page badges preserve `Do not offer` and `Explain`.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/opus/notes.md:19:- [Wei et al., *Clin Trials* 2009, OPUS design and methods](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2878478/): full-text methods report opened, but it predates outcomes and adds no separate efficacy or harm estimate beyond the primary paper.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/opus/notes.md:20:- [Nager et al., 2014, sham-incision blinding analysis](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4346086/): considered; it evaluates masking rather than a new clinical treatment endpoint.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/opus/notes.md:21:- [Concomitant anterior-repair secondary analysis](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5995601/) and [pooled sexual-function analysis](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7483870/): considered; both combine OPUS with other trials and answer different questions.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/opus/notes.md:24:## Disagreements and unresolved details
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/opus/notes.md:26:- **Old Journal Club record:** Its `name` is the acronym expansion, not the primary paper's published title. Its outcome values, randomized sample, comparison and approximate 12-month NNT match the primary paper. The short `primaryOutcome` wording omits that the 3-month endpoint is a composite including treatment and that 12-month incontinence is measured despite interval treatment (Wei 2012, Methods: Study Outcomes; Table 2). No numerical discrepancy was found.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/opus/notes.md:27:- **Primary paper itself:** The PubMed/PMC abstract states major bleeding complications as 3.1%, while full-text Table 4 lists 5/164 (3.0% to one decimal). The trial file and page use the table's count, denominator and rounding.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/opus/notes.md:28:- **WARWIKI pages searched for `OPUS`:** `docs/03-clinical-conditions/03c-pelvic-support/pelvic-organ-prolapse.mdx` (line 332), `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04g-prolapse-repair/anterior/anterior-colporrhaphy.mdx` (lines 142 and 146), and `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/female-slings-suspensions.mdx` (line 236). Their OPUS-specific numbers and statements agree with Wei 2012, Tables 2 and 4, and the Methods; no page-level source disagreement was found. The AUA/SUFU *guideline copy*, outside those site pages, describes the 27.3% versus 43.0% OPUS composite as SUI; Wei 2012 defines it more broadly as stress, urge or mixed incontinence (Methods: Study Outcomes; Table 2). The appraisal uses the paper's definition.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/opus/notes.md:29:- The full text reports `327/337` one-year follow-up completion but only `287/337` evaluable 12-month primary-outcome data (Wei 2012, Figure 1 and Results: Study Population). The latter distinction is retained in the trial file and page.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/inhibizone/notes.md:7:| Carson CC III, Mulcahy JJ, Harsch MR. J Urol 2011;185(2):614-618 (primary) | DOI 10.1016/j.juro.2010.09.094, PMID 21168870 | PubMed abstract via NCBI efetch; Crossref for authors, affiliations, volume, pages | abstract only |
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/inhibizone/notes.md:10:| Carson CC III. J Urol 2004;171(4):1611-1614 | DOI 10.1097/01.ju.0000118245.66976.e1, PMID 15017233 | PubMed abstract; cited on the page only for the definition of InhibiZone (rifampin plus minocycline hydrochloride surface treatment) | abstract only |
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/inhibizone/notes.md:11:| AUA Erectile Dysfunction guideline, approved April 2018 | local copy `reports/audit-v2/sources-local/aua-ed-2018-website.txt` | statements 18-20 (lines ~134-146), "Infection" discussion (lines ~1878-1950), references 786-801, 887-888 | full text |
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/inhibizone/notes.md:13:| openFDA PMA listing for N970012 (original approval 1998-11-02, "AMS 700 series") | api.fda.gov/device/pma.json | read as JSON; not used on the page | - |
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/inhibizone/notes.md:15:Full text of the primary report could not be opened: auajournals.org, jurology.com and sciencedirect.com returned HTTP 403; Europe PMC lists the article as subscription-only and not in PMC. No copy exists under `reports/audit-v2/sources-local` or in `/Users/joyboy/Documents/Medicine` or `/Users/joyboy/Downloads` (searched by DOI, "InhibiZone", "Carson", "Mulcahy"). All values therefore come from abstracts (`met: null`, `status.sourceRead: abstract`). Nothing in the file or page is taken from an AI summary of a page; the FDA record was read from raw HTML.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/inhibizone/notes.md:19:- Carson 2004 (J Urol 171:1611, PMID 15017233): the 2011 abstract calls it the earlier 1-year report. Its PubMed record carries an erratum (J Urol 2004 Aug;172(2):781, two "ERRATA" items on that page in Crossref, DOIs 10.1097/00005392-200408000-00103 and -00104), whose text sits behind the publisher's paywall and was not read. Its 2001-2003 cohort also falls inside the 2011 window. Its numbers (60-day infection 0.28% versus 1.59%, 180-day 0.68% versus 1.61%, 2,261 versus 1,944 men, mean follow-up 5 versus 8 months, per the PubMed abstract) were therefore kept out of the trial file; the paper is cited only for the InhibiZone definition. If the erratum is obtained and does not change those figures, it could be added as a `secondary` report.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/inhibizone/notes.md:24:## Disagreements with WARWIKI pages (none found for the numbers)
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/inhibizone/notes.md:26:Pages that discuss the study: `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04j-sexual-dysfunction/penile-implants/infection.mdx`, `.../implant-models.mdx`, `docs/01-foundations/pharmacology/infection-prophylaxis/prosthetic-infection-biofilm.mdx`, `docs/07-roots/history/prosthetics-ed.mdx`.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/inhibizone/notes.md:31:- `prosthetics-ed.mdx` line 99: "FDA cleared an infection-reduction promotional claim in 2009" could not be confirmed from any FDA page opened here (the openFDA/accessdata PMA N970012 listing shows no such entry; its pre-2010 supplements are largely absent from the data). Unverified.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/inhibizone/notes.md:33:- AUA ED guideline (2018) text, "Infection": it describes the Carson 2011 comparison as antibiotic-impregnated inflatable devices "compared to non-inflatable devices". The abstract compares impregnated with nonimpregnated inflatable prostheses, so the guideline wording is a slip; the page does not repeat it. The same passage gives Mulcahy 1.5% of 6,071 versus 4.2% of 624 and Nehra 2.5% of 9,300 versus 3.7% of 1,764, which agree with the abstracts (1.47%/4.17% and 2.5%/3.7%).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/inhibizone/notes.md:35:## Disagreements with the old Journal Club record
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/inhibizone/notes.md:37:- `domain: 'Male SUI / Prosthetics'` is wrong; the study concerns penile prostheses (area `sexual-medicine`).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/inhibizone/notes.md:39:- `population` and `comparison` match. The record calls the data "manufacturer-registered"; the abstract says patient information forms "voluntarily filed with the manufacturer", which the page uses.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/inhibizone/notes.md:45:- Whether the log-rank comparison was the only analysis, whether any adjustment was made, group follow-up distributions, event counts, and the surgical and perioperative details: all need the full text. The page therefore says only that the log-rank comparison does not control for patient, surgeon or perioperative differences.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/inhibizone/notes.md:48:- Any FDA clearance or approval date for InhibiZone itself; the page states only the PMA record title.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/impress/notes.md:3:Output: `impress.json` (passes a copy of `scripts/check-clinical-evidence.mjs` run against a scratch tree) and `impress.mdx` (ten references, numbered in order of first use, all markers resolve; primary DOI on the page).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/impress/notes.md:9:| Gelbard 2013, J Urol 190(1):199-207, doi 10.1016/j.juro.2013.01.087, PMID 23376148 | PubMed efetch XML (title, authors, pages, full abstract); Crossref (volume, pages, print date July 2013); Unpaywall says closed access, Europe PMC "subscription required" | abstract only. Journal full text could not be opened. |
