reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/poppy/notes.md:7:| Hagen 2014 Lancet (primary) | DOI 10.1016/S0140-6736(13)61977-7, PMID 24290404 | Accepted (final peer-reviewed) manuscript, Aberdeen University repository, https://aura.abdn.ac.uk/bitstreams/456fe6b2-7cee-45dc-847e-fd8ea78b1cbf/download (found through Europe PMC's Unpaywall link, hdl.handle.net/2164/3322); read with pdftotext, 37 pages. No local copy existed in sources-local, Medicine or Downloads. The Lancet site returned 403. PubMed abstract (efetch) also read. | full text, but the accepted manuscript, not the typeset article |
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/poppy/notes.md:12:Resolved on Crossref: Hagen (authors, Lancet 383(9919):796-806, March 2014), Fenocchi (Int Urogynecol J 34(1):239-246, online 28 June 2022), AUGS statement (authors, volume, pages). PubMed lists Hagen's electronic publication date as 28 November 2013 (used as `published`). ClinicalTrials.gov API confirms NCT00476892 as POPPY with secondary ID ISRCTN35911035.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/poppy/notes.md:14:`source.file` is null for both reports (read online). Locators for the primary report cite the PDF page of the accepted manuscript.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/poppy/notes.md:20:- PREVPROL (Hagen 2017 Lancet): a separate trial, not on the site list and not part of this family.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/poppy/notes.md:22:- NICE NG123 (local copy): recommendation 1.7.5 (supervised PFMT of at least 16 weeks as a first option for stage 1 or 2 prolapse) is in the file, but the guideline text on disk does not name POPPY and its rationale says only that "a small amount of evidence" supports training, so no NICE statement is attached to the trial page.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/poppy/notes.md:27:1. The accepted manuscript differs from the published abstract in places. I used published-abstract values where they exist (6-month questionnaire return 377 (84%) in the abstract, versus 381/447 (85%) in the manuscript text and 189 + 199 = 388 in its Figure 1; the abstract also gives trial dates 22 June 2007 to 9 April 2010, the manuscript says recruitment ran September 2007 to February 2010). Only the 12-month return (295/447, 66%) agrees everywhere. All table values (Tables 1 to 5) come from the manuscript and should be rechecked against the typeset Lancet article when it is available.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/poppy/notes.md:29:3. The manuscript abstract gives the cost-per-point CI as GBP 50.81 to 286.11; its Results give 51.81 to 286.11. The page and file quote only the point estimate (GBP 86.59).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/poppy/notes.md:30:4. The manuscript's Table 3 labels one POP-SS item "Abdominal pain when standing", while the Results text calls it "lower abdominal heaviness"; the page does not name the item.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/poppy/notes.md:36:## Disagreements with WARWIKI pages and the old record
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/poppy/notes.md:38:No old Journal Club record exists. Pages that cite POPPY:
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/poppy/notes.md:40:- `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04g-prolapse-repair/lifestyle-modifications.mdx` (line 50, refs 8 and 11): "447 women were randomized and 295 returned 12-month questionnaires; ... adjusted between-group difference 1.52 points" matches the source. The record-linkage sentence (293 Scottish participants, 65/149 versus 76/144, adjusted OR 0.61, 95% CI 0.37 to 0.99) matches Fenocchi; "over 11 years" is a rounding of "up to 10 years 9 months" (Fenocchi's own wording is "up to 11 years" and "more than 10 years"). No disagreement.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/poppy/notes.md:41:- `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/pelvic-floor-pt.mdx` (line 85, ref 17): "POP-SS reduction 3.77 versus 2.09; adjusted difference 1.52, 95% CI 0.46 to 2.59; 295 of 447 randomized women (66%)" matches the source. No disagreement.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/poppy/notes.md:44:Other grep hits for "POPPY" (colovesical fistula and cystography pages) are unrelated words.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/poppy/notes.md:48:- The typeset Lancet article and its webappendix (the Lancet site blocks automated access); published values were checked only through the PubMed abstract.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/nitti-oab/notes.md:10:| FDA BOTOX label, Reference ID 3247784, revised 01/2013 | https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2013/103000s5251lbl.pdf | PDF pages cited in the file (p5 section 2.3, p12 Tables 6-7, p13 Table 10, p14 Table 11, p20-21 Tables 16-17, p22 duration of response). Gives Study OAB-1 ANCOVA least-squares means, differences and CIs. | full label |
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/nitti-oab/notes.md:14:| Crossref for 10.1016/j.juro.2012.12.022, 10.1016/j.juro.2016.03.146, 10.1097/JU.0000000000003985 | authors, volume, pages checked | | |
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/nitti-oab/notes.md:16:I also sent one request to the auajournals.org article URL for the primary paper before reading the rule that the AUA site is off limits; it returned HTTP 403 and nothing was read or used. No auanet.org page was opened.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/nitti-oab/notes.md:33:- Number of sites, randomization stratification, blinding procedures, technique details actually used in the trial (anesthesia, cystoscope, volume), the exact definition of the treatment benefit scale response and complete continence, the prespecified significance level and any multiplicity handling, discontinuation reasons, and the paper's own adverse-event table. The page therefore labels technique details as FDA label instructions or as AUA guideline statements about the group of trials.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/nitti-oab/notes.md:35:- Retention: the abstract states a 5.4% rate; the registry counts 16/278 (5.8%) with the term. Both are in the file and the page states each once. The paper's denominator is unknown.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/nitti-oab/notes.md:37:- AUA statement 26 text: the page extraction drops the comparison symbol before "100-200 mL" in the sentence on the PVR exclusion criterion of references 222, 236 and 239; the page says "threshold in the 100 to 200 mL range".
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/nitti-oab/notes.md:41:## Disagreements and gaps on existing WARWIKI pages (not edited)
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/nitti-oab/notes.md:43:No number on the pages below disagrees with the abstract. Items that cannot be confirmed from the abstract or the sources above:
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/nitti-oab/notes.md:51:Matches the abstract on n = 557, population, comparison, week 12, -2.65 vs -0.87 (not given in the record but consistent), 22.9% vs 6.5%, 60.8% vs 29.2%, UTI as most common event, 5.4% retention, DOI. Differences: its "bottomLine" calls this "the pivotal trial" for FDA approval; the label rests on two pivotal studies (this one and the companion). Its guideline text is consistent with AUA/SUFU 2024 statements 25 (Moderate Recommendation; Evidence Level: Grade A) and 24 (Expert Opinion); the new page cites statements 25 and 26 and the EAU 2026 recommendations because those cite this trial. Its "double-blind" is supported by the label and registry (quadruple masking), not by the abstract.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/nitti-oab/notes.md:55:`nitti-oab.json` parses; I ran `scripts/check-clinical-evidence.mjs` on a scratch copy of the repo data with the new files added: the only errors were the missing relation target `ginsberg-ndo` (not yet in the repo) and the pre-existing `rosetta` relation to `abc`. Percentages were recounted from numerators and denominators; MDX reference markers resolve and are numbered in order of first use.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/pessri/notes.md:5:- **Primary report:** Cundiff GW, Amundsen CL, Bent AE, et al., *Am J Obstet Gynecol.* 2007;196(4):405.e1-405.e8. DOI [10.1016/j.ajog.2007.02.018](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ajog.2007.02.018); PMID [17403437](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17403437/). PubMed abstract opened and read: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17403437/ . **Source level: abstract.** PubMed verifies authors, title, year, journal, volume, issue, electronic pages, DOI and PMID. The publisher's indexed Materials and Methods and Results excerpts at https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0002937807002529 supplied stage II eligibility and the initial sequence allocation (71 ring, 63 Gellhorn), but the article page itself returned HTTP 403. These excerpts were not counted as full-text access.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/pessri/notes.md:10:- No PESSRI paper text or author-named PDF was found in `reports/audit-v2/sources-local`, `/Users/joyboy/Documents/Medicine`, or `/Users/joyboy/Downloads`. The owner source search found audit logs and guideline references, not the primary article.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/pessri/notes.md:11:- The open Zenodo record https://zenodo.org/records/896561 links to `article.pdf` at https://zenodo.org/records/896561/files/article.pdf . The PDF endpoint failed in the web reader with a Unicode decoding error; direct shell retrieval failed DNS resolution. Its existence does not count as a full-text read. An owner-accessible download of this PDF is the clear next source for page-level PFDI/PFIQ scores, crossover completion, fitting methods, harms, and any formal outcome threshold.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/pessri/notes.md:31:- Read the full PESSRI PDF before clinical review. Confirm the endpoint hierarchy, statistical hypothesis, paired completion count, quantitative scores and effect estimates, adverse events, fitting/crossover technique, and any washout or carryover handling. The page and trial file are explicitly marked abstract-level until that work is done.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/pessri/notes.md:32:- No percentage was used in the trial file or page, so there is no numerator/denominator percentage to recalculate. The denominator distinction that matters here is 134 enrolled versus 94 and 99 device-period data sets.
