reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/merit/notes.md:35:6. `recurrent-uti.mdx` line 296 and the other pages cite pages 619–628 and volume 184(6): correct. `uti-suppressive-prophylactic.mdx` ref 12 and `non-antibiotic-uti-prevention.mdx` ref 16 add "(MERIT)" inside the quoted title; the published title does not contain it.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/merit/notes.md:41:- The AUA 2025 rationale (ref 250 paragraph) gives "microbiological recurrence (RR 0.90; 95% CI 0.60 to 1.33)" for MERIT; in the source that RR is for resistant uropathogens cultured during infection (41/294 versus 45/289; PDF p4), not microbiological recurrence, and the AUA also labels the incidence rate ratio of 0.88 (0.72 to 1.08) "UTI-related symptoms" when it is the number of clinically suspected UTIs. The page quotes only the AUA's RR 0.92 (0.80 to 1.05), which matches.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/merit/notes.md:42:- The EAU 2026 text describes the primary outcome as "cystitis" throughout where the source says "UTI"; the quote on the page follows the EAU wording for its statement.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/merit/notes.md:43:- Percentages in the report for menopause (63.1%) and current symptoms (23.9%) use the number answering, not 598; the page says so.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/merit/notes.md:52:- The earlier D-mannose trials (Kranjčec 2014, Porru 2014) are described only as the MERIT report summarizes them (PDF p5-6); I did not open them, so they are not cited as references on the page.
