reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/robust/notes.md:28:## Disagreements with other WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/robust/notes.md:30:1. `docs/04-surgical-techniques/drug-coated-balloon.mdx` line 188 and `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/minimally-invasive/drug-coated-balloon.mdx` line 31: "hematuria 9/79 versus 0/48, dysuria 5/79 versus 0/48, UTI 5/79 versus 1/48" (cited to the IFU). The published 1-year report says hematuria and dysuria were each 11.4% versus 2.1% (PMC12721643, Safety Results), i.e. 1/48 in controls, and does not give a UTI split. The FDA SSED text gives related hematuria 11.4%, dysuria 6.3% and UTI 6.3% (p 19), but its Table 13 lists related hematuria 3/79 and dysuria 5/79. Not wrong against the SSED, but it differs from the paper; the pages should say which source and whether the events are adjudicated-related or all-cause.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/robust/notes.md:34:5. Wording of AUA statement 11b: the full guideline reads "Surgeons may offer urethral dilation or direct visual internal urethrotomy, combined with drug-coated balloons" while the J Urol amendment article prints "Surgeons may perform urethral dilation, or direct visual internal urethrotomy, combined with drug-coated balloons". Strength and grade are identical (Conditional; Grade B). The page uses the guideline wording; the WARWIKI pages that say "may offer" agree with it.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/robust/notes.md:36:No other number on the pages I grepped (ROBUST in `docs/`: DCB pages, DVIU, pharmacology, instruments, history, surgeon profiles) disagrees with the sources: 74.6% (50/67) and 26.8% (11/41), difference 44.4 (27.6–61.1), 83.2% versus 21.7%, 71.9% at 3 years, 27/56 (48%), ROBUST I 71.7%, 58% (25/43), 17/22 versus 8/21, and the semen detection rates all match.
