reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/oasis-revi/notes.md:34:## Disagreements between WARWIKI pages and the sources
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/oasis-revi/notes.md:39:- "98 participants were not taking OAB medication" (cited to the technique guide). FDA DEN220073 p20 gives 49 taking and 101 not taking OAB medication; the primary report gives 53 (35%) taking at baseline (p1501); the QoL report gives 32.7%. The sources disagree with each other. The 89% versus 70% responder split matches FDA p20.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/oasis-revi/notes.md:43:- All other OASIS numbers on this page (76.4% with CI 68.7-82.6%, 78.4% post hoc, 77/97, 88/91, 3/151 by 24 months, 90 with 36-month diaries at 79%, 83/87, 74/90) match the sources. The 36-month report counts 5/151 explants, which the page does not mention.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/oasis-revi/notes.md:47:## Inconsistencies inside the source papers (not resolved)
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/oasis-revi/notes.md:53:- Third explant: the 24-month report says a nonresponder requested explantation in year 2; the 36-month report lists later explants for painful stimulation (753 and 1,230 days) and foot fusion surgery (1,233 days), giving 5, not 6. The page notes that the reasons differ.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:7:| Primary report, Ziegelmann et al., J Urol 2019;202(3):599-610 | DOI 10.1097/JU.0000000000000245, PMID 30916626 | PubMed record via NCBI eutils (abstract, authors, ClinicalTrials.gov link, electronic date 2019-08-08); Crossref (volume, issue, pages, seven authors) | **abstract only** |
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:10:| Protocol and SAP, April 9, 2018 | https://cdn.clinicaltrials.gov/large-docs/54/NCT03389854/Prot_SAP_000.pdf | downloaded and read with pdftotext (12 pages): eligibility, randomization strata, power analysis, non-compliance definition, conflict of interest | full text |
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:13:**Could not open:** the full text of the primary report and of Joseph 2020. Both are paywalled and not in PMC (Europe PMC: `inPMC N`); auajournals.org, doi.org, ScienceDirect, the J Sex Med site and academic.oup.com returned HTTP 403 (Cloudflare) to curl and WebFetch. No local copy exists in `reports/audit-v2/sources-local`, `/Users/joyboy/Documents/Medicine` or `/Users/joyboy/Downloads`. Consequences:
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:18:- Not taken because the paper was not read: the "primary curvature" figure on the penile-traction-therapy page (see below), the number of men analyzed per questionnaire in the paper, the paper's ITT/analysis-population definitions, and the paper's own adverse-event tabulation.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:27:- Green B, et al. J Urol 2023;210(5):791-802 (PMID 37811752): randomized CCH versus surgery, each with RestoreX and sildenafil; a different trial (no id on the site list).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:31:- DOIs and PMIDs resolved on Crossref and NCBI: author list, year, journal, volume, issue and pages match for both reports (Crossref lists the primary report as September 2019 print; electronic date 2019-08-08 from PubMed is used as `published`).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:33:- JSON parses; the repository validator `scripts/check-clinical-evidence.mjs` passes for this trial when run on a scratch copy (the only error it prints, `rosetta` relation to `abc`, comes from the other trial ids not being present yet). Percentages recomputed: 62/82 = 75.6%, 27/28 = 96.4%, 63/82 = 76.8%, 34/82 = 41.5%, 33/82 = 40.2%, 24/63 = 38.1% (removed), 19/63 = 30.2% (removed). Takeaway is 41 words; verdictNote 14 words.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:36:- FDA: openFDA 510(k) searches for RestoreX, PathRight and penile traction returned no match, so no approval or clearance statement was written. The protocol states that penile traction devices with a deformity-correction claim are generally Class I (orthotic) devices.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:39:## Registry internal inconsistencies noticed
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:41:- 3-month erythema: the outcome measure lists 43%, 41% and 37% by dose group, whereas the adverse events module counts 12/27, 12/28 and 10/27 (44%, 43%, 37%). The file and page use the counts (34/82).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:42:- Registry open-label (6-month) adverse events: the outcome measure gives erythema 35% and mild new pain 27% of 63, whereas the adverse events module lists 24/63 and 19/63 for the open-label group. The file and page use the outcome-measure percentages; the module counts were dropped.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:44:- Abstract: no exclusion for prior Peyronie's therapy; protocol/registry excluded men currently on intralesional injections and asked others to stop therapy for 4 weeks. Both are on the page.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:47:## Disagreements and unverifiable items on other WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:49:1. `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/docs/04-surgical-techniques/04j-sexual-dysfunction/penile-traction-therapy.mdx` line 44: "**+1.5 cm vs 0 cm stretched length (p < 0.001), −8.5° vs −1.1° primary curvature (p < 0.001), and −11.7° vs +1.3° composite curvature (p < 0.01)**". The +1.5 versus 0 cm and −11.7 versus +1.3 figures match the abstract and registry (+1.5 versus 0.0 cm is length to the penile tip; length to the corona is +1.3 versus +0.3 cm, P = 0.001). The −8.5° versus −1.1° "primary curvature" values and their P < 0.001 appear in neither the abstract nor the registry; I could not verify them (Urology Times, a conference report on a different enrollment of 120, gave −9 versus −1.0 degrees). The "62 traction and 27 control men" matches the registry.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:50:2. Same page, line 45 (and `docs/03-clinical-conditions/03g-genital-scrotal/peyronies-disease.mdx` line 189): "Among 37 original traction-arm men using RestoreX ≥15 min/day for 6 mo, 95% had length gains and 61% had curvature improvements" and "mean use 31 min/day". The Joseph abstract says 95% of men treated for 6 months had length gains and 61% curve improvements, and separately that the as-treated analysis used at least 15 minutes per day; it does not state that the 95% and 61% denominators are the 37 men who used the device at least 15 minutes daily, nor that 31.1 minutes is per day. The 37 comes from the registry (original traction men with 6-month data). Unverified without the full text.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:54:6. Nothing found that contradicts the primary report on `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04j-sexual-dysfunction/peyronies.mdx`, `male-cosmetic.mdx` (those cite the prostatectomy trial) or `docs/07-roots/surgeons/s-z/matthew-ziegelmann.mdx` / `landon-trost.mdx` (the Landon Trost page says he invented RestoreX; the protocol's conflict-of-interest section confirms this).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:56:## Disagreements with the old Journal Club record
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:61:- `design` "single-blind" is correct; "all-comers, acute or chronic phase" is correct for the abstract's description (no exclusions for complexity or prior treatment, curvature of at least 30 degrees); enrolling site is Mayo Clinic Rochester only.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:68:**Corrected (existing items that differed or were wrong):**
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:78:- Takeaway and opening: curvature is stated as composite curvature; "caused only transient, mild adverse events" became "had only transient, mild adverse events".
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:81:**Added:** arm-level values with SDs and denominators for length (tip and corona), primary and composite curvature, composite response (24.6% of 61 versus 4% of 25; the table prints "61 (24.6)" and "25 (4)"), IIEF erectile function in men with erectile dysfunction and all men, IIEF intercourse and overall satisfaction, Sexual Encounter Profile question 2 (4/8 versus 0/4), PDQ domains and question 12; key secondary endpoints classed `key-secondary` with `met` (the paper calls Table 3 "key secondary outcomes", with two-tailed P below 0.05 and no hierarchy or multiplicity adjustment); responder proportions (77% curvature, 94% length, with the 27%, 48% and 29% thresholds); device preference (15 men), first-line preference (74.1% of 54) and satisfaction; exploratory dose-group and total-minutes analyses; harms with denominators; baseline arm values, whole-cohort severity, recruitment (800 mailed, 110 randomized), randomization strata, power, analysis populations, withdrawals (6, 5, 8 and 1), compliance (100%, 82%, 47%), actual use (mean 43 minutes per day, range 16 to 84), counter-bending and white-line analyses, funding and disclosure; and a Spanish Penimaster PRO trial comparison to the In conversation paragraph (cited to the paper, no new reference).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:83:**Judgment calls and paper inconsistencies:**
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:90:**Disagreements with other WARWIKI pages revealed by the full text:**
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:91:1. `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04j-sexual-dysfunction/penile-traction-therapy.mdx` line 44: the "−8.5° vs −1.1° primary curvature (p < 0.001)" figure, which could not be verified from the abstract, is now verified (Table 3, p603: −8.5 (SD 10.1) versus −1.1 (SD 7.5), P < 0.001). The page's "−11.7° vs +1.3° composite (p < 0.01)" and "+1.5 cm vs 0 cm (p < 0.001)" also match. The mean use of 43 minutes per day (range 16 to 84) over the randomized phase is the paper's figure; the 31 minutes cited on that page is the open-label phase from Joseph 2020 and still cannot be checked against this paper.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:92:2. Same page and `peyronies-disease.mdx`: the 6-month 95% and 61% denominators (37 men using the device at least 15 minutes a day) remain unverified; they concern the Joseph report, not this paper.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:93:3. Any page that describes RestoreX as having "no significant adverse events" or as free of mild events should add that 45.2% had erythema or discoloration and 53.2% new mild pain at 3 months (all mild and transient).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/restorex/notes.md:94:4. Any page giving the exclusion criteria should use the paper's (stretched length under 7 cm, severe diabetes with end-organ failure); the registry adds erectile dysfunction unresponsive to therapy.
