reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/rosetta/notes.md:22:## Disagreements with existing WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/rosetta/notes.md:26:The other five ROSETTA-containing pages checked had no source-discordant ROSETTA number or statement: `docs/01-foundations/pharmacology/neuromodulation-adjuncts/botulinum-toxin.mdx`, `docs/01-foundations/tools/biomaterials/neuromodulation-devices/medtronic-interstim.mdx`, `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/intradetrusor-botox.mdx`, `docs/07-roots/surgeons/a-g/cindy-amundsen.mdx`, and `docs/07-roots/surgeons/s-z/halina-zyczynski.mdx`.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/rosetta/notes.md:38:- The 2018 abstract gives P = 0.15 for the 24-month mean episode comparison, while its Results paragraph rounds this to P = 0.2. The file and page use the abstract value and record the discrepancy in the result note.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/save-u/notes.md:13:Limits: the 10-year paper (Springer, open access CC BY) could not be opened. link.springer.com returns a bot challenge (Client Challenge) to curl and a cookie-authorization redirect to WebFetch, doi.org redirects there, and the paper is not in PMC or Europe PMC full text. Only the PubMed abstract was read, so the 10-year Result rows carry `met: null`, no harms, and no denominators beyond those in the abstract (3/44, 7/49; posterior failure given only as percentages). No local PDF copy exists in `/Users/joyboy/Downloads`, `/Users/joyboy/Documents/Medicine` or `sources-local` (searched by DOI text and file name). The three trial reports have `source.file: null` because they were read online; the 2015 and 2019 locators are table and section names (PMC text has no PDF page numbers).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/save-u/notes.md:15:DOIs, volumes, pages and author lists of all four references were checked against Crossref and PubMed (the 10-year paper has no volume or pages yet; it is cited as published online August 13, 2026).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/save-u/notes.md:28:## Disagreements with the old Journal Club record
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/save-u/notes.md:35:## Disagreements and checks on other WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/save-u/notes.md:37:Pages searched: `grep -rln "SAVE-U" docs`. Findings against sources:
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/save-u/notes.md:39:- `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04g-prolapse-repair/apical/sacrospinous-hysteropexy.mdx`, five-year paragraph (line 56): 1/102, 8/102 (7.8%), difference -6.7 (-12.8 to -0.7), composite 89/102 versus 77/102, anterior 41/102 versus 36/101, beyond-hymen 6/102 versus 8/101: all agree with Schulten 2019 Table 2 (Table 2, Results). No disagreement. The same paragraph's ten-year posterior denominators 5/43 and 24/46 cannot be checked from the abstract (percentages 11.6% and 52.2% and the difference agree).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/save-u/notes.md:40:- Same page (line 25) and lines 33 to 39: eligibility, posterior approach, two permanent sutures at least 2 cm from the ischial spine, right-sided fixation: agree with Detollenaere 2015 Methods.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/save-u/notes.md:43:- Nothing on these pages cites the 12-month report's secondary findings (operating time, buttock pain 9/103, retention 15/103); the hysteropexy page's buttock-pain section cites SAM only.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/save-u/notes.md:44:- Source-to-source difference: the AUGS 2025 statement (text on SSHP) reports that Dietz et al found apical recurrence of 3% after hysterectomy with uterosacral suspension versus 27% after hysteropexy; the SAVE-U 2015 paper reports 7/34 (21%) versus 1/31 (3%). The page avoids the numbers.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/save-u/notes.md:48:- Schulten 2019 Table 2 prints composite success for hysterectomy (LOCF) as 77/102 (76%); 77/102 is 75.5% (75.49). The source's printed 76% (and the abstract's "87% v 76%") is kept in the file and the page.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/save-u/notes.md:49:- 2015 Table 1 baseline stage distribution is for 100 and 103 patients in the overall-stage row; the page uses only the uterine point C stage rows (103 and 105).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/save-u/notes.md:58:- Whether the SUPeR and SAM pages will describe themselves as the page's one-line comparison says; those trials were not opened.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sam/notes.md:4:- Primary report, Enklaar et al., JAMA 2023;330(7):626-635, doi 10.1001/jama.2023.13140, PMID 37581670: FULL TEXT read as the PMC HTML copy (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10427949/), including Tables 1 to 3 and the Discussion. No local file (none in reports/audit-v2/sources-local, ~/Documents/Medicine, ~/Downloads or ~/Zotero). The JAMA publisher page (jamanetwork.com) is bot-blocked for direct download; WebFetch summaries of it matched the PMC tables on every number. Crossref (title, authors, volume, pages, issue date 2023-08-15, abstract) cross-checked. `source.file` is null.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sam/notes.md:6:- Economic evaluation, Schulten et al., PLoS One 2025;20(11):e0336030, doi 10.1371/journal.pone.0336030, PMID 41201997: FULL TEXT (open access PDF, 12 pages), locators are PDF pages.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sam/notes.md:7:- Sexual function, Stoter et al., Am J Obstet Gynecol 2026;234(4):1112-1120, doi 10.1016/j.ajog.2025.12.035, PMID 41419154: ABSTRACT only (Europe PMC record); the journal page was not opened. Published online 2025-12-17 (Crossref created date), volume date April 2026. `met` is null for its primary equivalence result for that reason.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sam/notes.md:14:- JAMA correction (doi 10.1001/jama.2023.17655, group authorship), JAMA letters (Ye 2023, doi 10.1001/jama.2023.21795) and the authors' reply (10.1001/jama.2023.21798), Nager editorial: not result reports; not opened.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sam/notes.md:17:- 2026 global consensus statements cited on WARWIKI pages (Gupta et al.; Stoter U-POP) were not opened, so no statement from them is on the page.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sam/notes.md:19:## Disagreements with WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sam/notes.md:20:I checked docs/04-surgical-techniques/04g-prolapse-repair/{index,prolapse-repair-principles}.mdx, apical/{manchester-fothergill,sacrospinous-hysteropexy,sacrohysteropexy}.mdx, 03-clinical-conditions/03c-pelvic-support/apical-prolapse.mdx and 01-foundations/anatomy-physiology/genitalia/cervix.mdx. Every SAM number on them matches the source (172/197 and 151/196, 434 randomized and 393 analyzed, 2/212 cervical stenosis, 4/164 abnormal histology with 1 CIN2, 4 severe or persistent buttock pain with 3 suture removals, 82.1% versus 80.0% PGI-I, PISQ-IR 0.09 (0.01 to 0.17) with margin 0.31, de novo dyspareunia 5% versus 13%, 101 and 99 sexually active). Points to be aware of, none a clear error:
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sam/notes.md:22:- manchester-fothergill.mdx line 74: de novo dyspareunia "without a statistically significant between-group difference" follows the abstract; the abstract's own interval (0.1% to 16.5%) excludes zero, so the abstract is internally inconsistent. The page repeats the abstract's wording, not the interval.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sam/notes.md:24:- Source of the 4/164 histology figure: SAM text says "abnormal cervical histology findings, including 1 CIN2" (164 of the Manchester specimens examined); the AUGS 2025 statement paraphrases it as CIN in 4/164 (2.4%). The trial pages state SAM correctly.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sam/notes.md:26:## Disagreements with the old Journal Club record
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sam/notes.md:27:- "The Manchester procedure met noninferiority and outperformed sacrospinous hysteropexy": wrong framing. The tested hypothesis was that sacrospinous hysteropexy is noninferior to the Manchester procedure; it failed (lower CI limit -17.8 versus a -9 point margin), and the authors conclude hysteropexy is inferior on the composite.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sam/notes.md:32:## Source inconsistencies kept as published
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sam/notes.md:33:- Noninferiority P for the as-randomized analysis: 0.63 (Abstract) versus 0.65 (Results text); per-protocol 0.62. File and page quote 0.65 and name the abstract value.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sam/notes.md:38:- Two Manchester participants lost to follow-up after retreatment: Figure 2 footnote says they were analyzed as not meeting the composite, then says they "did meet the criterion" (for retreatment); the page states they were counted as not meeting the composite.
