reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sumit/notes.md:38:## Disagreements with other WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sumit/notes.md:40:Pages mentioning SUmiT: `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/percutaneous-tibial-nerve-stimulation.mdx`, `.../implantable-tibial-nerve-stimulation.mdx`, `docs/01-foundations/tools/biomaterials/neuromodulation-devices/ptns-systems.mdx`, `docs/01-foundations/anatomy-physiology/donor-sites/leg-thigh.mdx`, `docs/07-roots/surgeons/h-r/kenneth-peters.mdx`.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sumit/notes.md:42:- `percutaneous-tibial-nerve-stimulation.mdx` line 69 (comparison table): "Superior for all OAB parameters; 54.5% vs. 20.9% improvement (SUmiT)". The abstract supports significant diary differences for frequency, nighttime voids, moderate-to-severe urgency and urge incontinence, and the global response for overall symptoms; the ICS abstract shows no significant overall difference at week 7 (P = 0.07). "All OAB parameters" is broader than the sources (locator: abstract; ICS Table 1). Everything else on that page (n = 220, 54.5% vs 20.9%, P < 0.001, diary outcomes, 0.5-9 mA protocol, STEP 35 at 24 months, 1.3 per month, five mild events) matches.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sumit/notes.md:46:- AUA/SUFU 2024 guideline (local copy, p 31) reports SUmiT as "55% ... versus 21% ... (p<0.01)"; the abstract and registry-era data give P < 0.001. The page uses P < 0.001 from the abstract.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sumit/notes.md:48:## Disagreements with the old Journal Club record
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sumit/notes.md:53:- Bottom line: "the 2024 AUA/SUFU guideline permits its use without prior therapy trials through shared decision-making (Expert Opinion)" is Statement 24 and applies to minimally invasive therapies as a group; it does not cite SUmiT. The statement that does (Statement 25, Moderate Recommendation, Evidence Level: Grade A) offers PTNS after inadequate response or intolerance to pharmacotherapy or behavioral therapy. The page uses Statement 25.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sumit/notes.md:54:- Word "pivotal" appears in the abstract's conclusion; the page does not repeat it.
