reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/super/notes.md:17:All `source.file` values are null (nothing was read from a local file for the trial reports). DOIs and PMIDs were resolved on Crossref and PubMed; authors, years, volume and pages match PubMed. DOI 10.1001/jama.2019.12812 has 12 PubMed authors, 10.1016/j.ajog.2021.03.012 has 11.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/super/notes.md:21:1. **Primary JAMA 2019 report.** The PDF and the publisher HTML are behind Cloudflare for scripted requests (not bypassed); the Unpaywall PDF link also returned a challenge page. PMC holds only the abstract. The Results, Table 2 and Table 3 values were read from the publisher HTML through the WebFetch summarizer. The headline numbers were obtained in two independent passes and agree with the abstract and with the 5-year report's 1-, 2- and 3-year rows (13/86 vs 21/85, 19/79 vs 28/83, 24/78 vs 33/80). The following values rest on the summarized publisher text only and should be rechecked against the PDF at the claim gate: PGI-I 70/78 vs 67/75; PFDI change −79.8 vs −80.1; dyspareunia 9/93 vs 2/90; pelvic pain 6/88 vs 9/87; adverse-event denominators 88/87; sling counts 29/88, 31/87, 12/88, 13/87; masked at 36 months 131/173; mesh exposure 7/88; midurethral sling exposure 0/41 vs 2/44.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/super/notes.md:22:2. **Erratum.** The February 16, 2021 JAMA erratum corrects "data in the Results section, including in the tables and figures." Its text is not available. The PMC abstract (pre-correction) says hazard ratio 0.62 (95% CI 0.38–1.02) and 36-month failure 26% vs 38%; the publisher page (post-correction) says 0.61 (0.37–1.02) and 24% vs 36%. The file uses the corrected values. Other corrected values (if any) in the JAMA tables may differ from what I read; PMC also still shows the old Key Points ("0.62").
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/super/notes.md:23:3. **Internal inconsistency on the publisher page** (fetch tool report, unverified): the abstract says 169 (97%) completed 3-year follow-up while the Results text said 153 attended the 36-month visit. The file uses the abstract figure only for `followUp`.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/super/notes.md:36:## Disagreements with WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/super/notes.md:38:- `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04g-prolapse-repair/apical/sacrospinous-hysteropexy.mdx` line 70: "The interval-censored primary analysis favored mesh hysteropexy (adjusted hazard ratio 0.58, 95% CI 0.36–0.94)". The 0.58 analysis is the 5-year report, which the authors describe as a secondary objective with descriptive P values and no multiplicity adjustment (AJOG 2021, Statistical Analysis). The trial's primary analysis was at 36 months: adjusted hazard ratio 0.61 (95% CI 0.37–1.02; P = .06), not significant (JAMA 2019 abstract, as corrected February 2021). The page also omits the 3-year result and the 8% mesh exposure; "postmenopausal women with prolapse beyond the hymen" is a shorthand for amenorrheic women aged 21+ (design report, Methods B).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/super/notes.md:41:- The AUGS statement (local copy) says the 5-year SUPeR analysis involved "193 patients"; the trial randomized 183 (175 analyzed). Not a WARWIKI page but relevant if the page quotes it.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/super/notes.md:43:## Disagreements with the old Journal Club record
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/super/notes.md:45:- `primaryOutcome: Time to composite treatment failure (to 5 years)`: the JAMA 2019 primary analysis was at 3 years (36-month estimate; failure modeled through 48 months). The 5-year analysis is AJOG 2021.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/super/notes.md:49:- `caveat: ... different question ... and different mesh` (ASPIRe): not verified. I did not open the ASPIRe mesh details; the site's mesh-graft page says ASPIRe used Uphold LITE. The trial file relation to `apical-suspension` states only what the ASPIRe abstract and the SUPeR design paper support (same network, same composite failure definition, vault prolapse after hysterectomy).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/super/notes.md:55:- Roles for the 5-year and 10-year composite are `secondary`, with no `met`, because the 5-year analysis is described as a secondary objective with descriptive P values; the 10-year paper is abstract-level.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/super/notes.md:58:- Validator run in a scratch mirror: the only errors were the three missing relation targets (plus the existing `rosetta` to `abc` one).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/super/notes.md:62:Sources read in full: the saved PMC page of the JAMA 2019 report (`html/super-2019-source.html`, PMC6749543, author manuscript text with Tables 1 to 3), Supplement 1 (protocol, amendments, SAP draft) and Supplement 2 (eFigures 1 to 6, eTables 1 to 5, eligibility, certification), and the saved PMC page of the 2021 erratum (`html/super-2021-correction-source.html`, one paragraph). The 5-year AJOG text was re-read from PMC8328912 (NCBI efetch) to check the 3-year row.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/super/notes.md:64:**Critical limitation: the supplied JAMA page is the pre-erratum text.** It says "This article has been corrected" but prints the uncorrected primary result (adjusted HR 0.62, 95% CI 0.38-1.02; 36-month failure 26% [15-35] versus 38% [25-49]; cumulative failure at 36 months 26/79 versus 34/81; at 12 and 24 months 15/86 versus 22/85 and 21/80 versus 29/83). Supplement 2 (eFigures 4 and 5) is also pre-erratum (HR 0.65 unadjusted, 0.63 all randomized). The erratum text itself lists no values. The file keeps the corrected headline values from the earlier publisher-page read (HR 0.61, 95% CI 0.37-1.02; 24% [14-33] versus 36% [24-47]); these could NOT be confirmed verbatim in any supplied text. What is confirmed verbatim: the post-erratum 3-year row (24/78 versus 33/80, risk difference -10, 95% CI -25 to 4) and the 12- and 24-month rows (13/86 versus 21/85; 19/79 versus 28/83) in AJOG 2021 Table 2. Action for the owner: supply the corrected JAMA PDF (or the publisher page text after February 2021), then recheck the six primary-result values (HR, CI, both incidences and their CIs) and the Figure 2 hazard-rate figures. Table 2 and Table 3 values other than the cumulative-failure rows were taken from the pre-erratum page; the 5-year report agrees with the ones it restates (anterior repair 79/88 versus 62/87, mesh exposure 8% unchanged, operative time), but a changed Table 2 cell cannot be excluded.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/super/notes.md:72:**Added (about 35):** prespecified alpha .047 (interim .009) with `met: false` explained; proportional-hazards violation and hazard pattern; failure-type breakdown at 36 months; secondary results (Ba, total vaginal length, PISQ-IR, dyspareunia among sexually active); harms (sling exposure, SUI, urgency, fecal incontinence, transfusion, return to OR, ICU, UTI, retention, bladder injury, constipation, uterine pathology); delivery facts (baseline characteristics, posterior repair, blood loss, catheter at discharge, hospital stay, in-hospital Dindo, masking by arm, randomization detail, sample-size error); scope of the erratum.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/super/notes.md:74:**Disagreements and inconsistencies found:**
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/super/notes.md:75:- JAMA Key Points and abstract (pre-erratum) give HR 0.62 and 26% versus 38%; the corrected abstract on the publisher page gives 0.61 and 24% versus 36% (unverified here). Any WARWIKI page quoting 0.62 or 26%/38% predates the erratum; PubMed and PMC abstracts still carry the uncorrected values.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/super/notes.md:82:- WARWIKI page `sacrospinous-hysteropexy.mdx` (see earlier notes) still presents the 5-year HR 0.58 as the primary analysis; the full text confirms the primary analysis was the 3-year one (P = .06, alpha .047) and that all other analyses, including the 5-year one, were exploratory or supportive with descriptive CIs and P values.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/topsy/notes.md:7:| Primary, 18 months: Hagen S, et al. EClinicalMedicine 2023;66:102326 | DOI 10.1016/j.eclinm.2023.102326; PMID 38078194; PMC10701109 | Europe PMC full-text XML (read online; `source.file` null). Text, Tables 1 to 7 and figure captions read; figures (CONSORT, forest plots) are images and were not read. No page numbers in the XML, so locators cite tables and Results subsections. Crossref and PubMed checked for authors (23), volume, article number and electronic date (2023-11-23). | full text |
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/topsy/notes.md:8:| 4-year follow-up: Bugge C, et al. BJOG 2025;132(12):1762-1771 | DOI 10.1111/1471-0528.18333; PMID 40832737; PMC12501713 | PMC BioC full text (CC BY). Text and Tables 1 to 4 read; supporting-information tables and figures (A to G) not available. Crossref: online 2025-08-20, issue 12, pages 1762-1771. | full text |
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/topsy/notes.md:22:No guideline text I could read cites TOPSY or rests on it. The AUGS-SUNA 2023 consensus statement (published before TOPSY) and NICE NG123 (2019) discuss self-managing pessary users but predate the trial. The TOPSY authors state that the UK Clinical Guideline for best practice in vaginal pessary use (2021) recommends offering self-management to willing and suitable women; I did not read that guideline (BJOG reference list shows a newer entry, PMID 42324994, also not opened). No FDA action applies (pessaries are an existing device class; no clearance statement tied to the trial was sought).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/topsy/notes.md:24:## Disagreements with WARWIKI pages
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/topsy/notes.md:26:Pages that cite TOPSY: `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04g-prolapse-repair/obliterative-pessary/prolapse-pessaries.mdx` (line 83, refs 10 and 20) and `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04g-prolapse-repair/prolapse-repair-principles.mdx` (line 23, refs 18 and 22). Other grep hits for "topsy" (autopsy) are unrelated.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/topsy/notes.md:28:No number on either page disagrees with the sources. Points to be aware of:
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/topsy/notes.md:30:1. `prolapse-repair-principles.mdx` line 23: "the adjusted clinic-care minus self-management difference in this measure was 3.01 percentage points (95% CI −0.58 to 6.61)" at 4 years. The BJOG text gives "AMD 3.01" and labels adjusted differences SM minus CBC elsewhere (Table 1) but reports a positive value with the lower rate in the SM group (17.91% versus 21.97%, Table 2); the sign convention for this outcome is therefore the page's reading (and mine), not stated in the paper. The 18-month figure is stated as SM minus CBC (−3.83). Also called "percentage points": the unit is the difference in mean percentage of applicable complication types.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/topsy/notes.md:31:2. `prolapse-pessaries.mdx` line 83: "17.9% for self-management versus 22.0% for clinic-based care in the results table" matches Table 2 (17.91 and 21.97). The BJOG abstract prints 17.7% for self-management; the page's note about the table is correct. The page's statement that "many clinic-care participants also removed their devices themselves" is a loose paraphrase: the paper says 55 clinic-care women reported removing their pessary at 18 months (27 of them had received self-management training) and that nearly 40% of clinic-care women inserted the pessary themselves at least once.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/topsy/notes.md:32:3. `prolapse-pessaries.mdx` line 83: the 18-month QoL statement and the 16.7% (142) versus 22.0% (152) complication figures match Table 3; the 4-year statements match the BJOG text. No disagreement.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/topsy/notes.md:49:- The 4-year primary-outcome status of the economic endpoint: the abstract lists both PFIQ-7 and incremental net benefit as primary; `met` for the economic result uses the paper's criterion (positive net benefit), and the confidence intervals are not given for the 4-year value (standard error only).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/topsy/notes.md:51:- `relations` left empty: no other site trial has a direct connection beyond sharing the pessary lane (PESSRI compares device types).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/steenkamp-dilation/notes.md:4:- Primary J Urol 1997;157(1):98-101, PMID 8976225, DOI 10.1016/S0022-5347(01)65296-0: PubMed abstract only (efetch), plus Crossref metadata (authors, volume, pages verified). Full text not obtainable: auajournals.org/doi.org return 403; Scribd "dviu" is a stub; Stellenbosch repository (scholar.sun.ac.za/handle/10019.1/14132) has the record and abstract but no file (bundles empty). `source.file` is null.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/steenkamp-dilation/notes.md:7:- Heyns 2012 S Afr J Surg 50(3):82-87 (scielo.org.za PDF, full text read): background only; it describes the trial as run at the authors' hospital, January 1991 to January 1994, 106 filiform dilation versus 104 optical urethrotomy. Not cited on the page.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/steenkamp-dilation/notes.md:13:- The EAU statement that the trial used retrograde urethrography at seven visits and "non-obliterative strictures at all locations" is the guideline's description, attributed as such on the page.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/steenkamp-dilation/notes.md:14:- J Urol 1997;157:102-3 (editorial comment on the trial) not opened. The Cochrane review cited by EAU and by the filiforms page was not opened.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/steenkamp-dilation/notes.md:15:- Pooled vs arm-specific: the length analyses (12-month rates, Cox HR) and the 1998 repeated-treatment estimates are stated in the abstracts without an arm split; the page says "both arms" only for length predicting failure in the Results sentence ("predicted failure in both arms" is the abstract conclusion "Both methods become less effective with increasing stricture length"), and "together" for the 1998 estimates.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/steenkamp-dilation/notes.md:16:- `relations` left empty: no allowed relation type runs correctly from this earlier trial to OPEN (urethroplasty versus urethrotomy) or ROBUST III (control: dilation or urethrotomy). Suggest `open` and `robust` files add `extends`/`builds-on` to `steenkamp-dilation` (inverse then shows here). The page links both in "In conversation".
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/steenkamp-dilation/notes.md:20:Pages citing the trial: `docs/01-foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/filiforms-followers.mdx` (ref 7 is the 1998 report); `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/minimally-invasive/dviu.mdx` (AUA 35-70% statement, which the AUA cites to refs 100-102).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/steenkamp-dilation/notes.md:21:- filiforms-followers.mdx line 68: "In the Heyns 1998 RCT, 210 men were randomized to filiform dilation (106) or internal urethrotomy (104)". Numbers match the abstracts (210, 106, 104; 55-60%/50-60%; 30-50%/0-40%; 0% at 24 months). Wording: the 1998 paper is a repeated-treatment analysis of the 1991-1994 trial whose primary report is Steenkamp 1997; "Heyns 1998 RCT" could say "randomized trial (Steenkamp 1997; repeated-treatment analysis Heyns 1998)". The page cites only the 1998 report.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/steenkamp-dilation/notes.md:23:- No stated disagreement with the source abstracts on any page found. No old Journal Club record exists.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/upstream/notes.md:3:Files: `upstream.json` (5 reports, 25 results, 7 harms, 11 delivery facts), `upstream.mdx` (7 references). The JSON validates with `scripts/check-clinical-evidence.mjs` run against a scratch copy of the repo containing both files (53 trial families, no errors). Percentages recounted against numerators and denominators; every reference marker resolves and is numbered in order of first use.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/upstream/notes.md:5:## Lead correction
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/upstream/notes.md:7:The lead names "Lewis AL, Young GJ ... Eur Urol 2020". The peer-reviewed primary report is Drake MJ, Lewis AL, Young GJ, et al., Eur Urol 2020;78(5):701-710 (the WARWIKI pages already cite it that way). Lewis AL, Young GJ, Selman LE, et al. is the full NIHR HTA monograph (HTA 2020;24(42)), filed as a `secondary` report. The only Lewis/Young Eur Urol Focus paper found is the 2019 baseline report (left out below).
