reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/ward-hilton/notes.md:29:## WARWIKI page consistency
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/ward-hilton/notes.md:31:- `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/burch-colposuspension.mdx`, evidence table and reference 25: its 5-year pad-test values (44/49 versus 58/72), P=0.21, 121/344 availability, prolapse finding and late tape-complication summary agree with the five-year report (*BJOG* pp 227–229, Tables 1–2; pp 231–232, Tables 5–6). No numerical or factual disagreement found.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/ward-hilton/notes.md:32:- `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/historical-bladder-neck-suspensions.mdx` names the UK and Ireland TVT trial as a later comparison; no numerical or factual disagreement found (trial identity in *BMJ* title and Methods).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/ward-hilton/notes.md:36:- The two-year article body was not available. Its abstract supports the rounded 63% and 51% intention-to-treat cure values and OR 1.67 (95% CI 1.09–2.58), but does not supply cure numerators or a prespecified test threshold. Consequently its structured `met` is null and no two-year surgical harms were entered.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/ward-hilton/notes.md:38:- The primary article does not name the colposuspension incision or explicitly call its operation Burch in the methods, so the structured comparator is “colposuspension.”
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/ward-hilton/notes.md:39:- The five-year report's Table 6 prints 4/170 repeat stress-incontinence operations as 2.3%; direct calculation is 2.35%, which rounds to 2.4% to one decimal. The page and trial file retain the numerator/denominator without that disputed percentage.
