reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/sister/notes.md:26:## Old Journal Club record disagreements
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/value/notes.md:5:1. **Primary report:** Nager CW, Brubaker L, Litman HJ, et al. *N Engl J Med.* 2012;366(21):1987–1997. DOI [10.1056/NEJMoa1113595](https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1113595); PMID [22551104](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22551104/). Full 16-page author manuscript opened at [NIH Public Access PDF mirror](https://portaldeboaspraticas.iff.fiocruz.br/wp-content/uploads/2019/04/nihms381522.pdf). `source.file: null` because it was read online. The original publication date, authors, volume, issue and pages were checked against PubMed and the PDF front matter. The official NEJM PDF and PMC HTML appeared in search but direct opening was blocked; the author manuscript supplied the full text.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/value/notes.md:6:2. **Treatment-plan secondary analysis:** Sirls LT, Richter HE, Litman HJ, et al. *J Urol.* 2013;189(1):204–209. DOI [10.1016/j.juro.2012.09.050](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.juro.2012.09.050); PMID [22982425](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22982425/). The [PMC full-text article](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4363108/) was read through indexed full-text methods, results and tables; the direct PMC view presented a browser challenge. PubMed confirmed the author order, print year, journal, volume, issue and pages; online publication was October 8, 2012. `source.file: null`.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/value/notes.md:10:- AUA/SUFU, *Surgical Treatment of Female Stress Urinary Incontinence*, 2023 unabridged copy: `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/reports/audit-v2/sources-local/aua-sui-2023-unabridged.txt`, Statement 5 and discussion on printed pp13–14. The journal amendment's DOI and bibliographic record were checked in the [journal record](https://doi.org/10.1097/JU.0000000000003435). The AUA site was not opened.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/value/notes.md:12:- [ACOG Committee Opinion No. 603 (2014)](https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2014/06/evaluation-of-uncomplicated-stress-urinary-incontinence-in-women-before-surgical-treatment) was located through indexed text, but direct access returned a payment error. It was omitted from the page because its full document and any published recommendation-strength label could not be verified here.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/value/notes.md:16:- Zimmern et al., *Neurourol Urodyn.* 2014, [PMC3830633](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3830633/): full text opened. Its physician-confidence analysis adds no separate surgical endpoint or treatment decision beyond the Sirls treatment-plan analysis.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/value/notes.md:22:## Disagreements with the old Journal Club record
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/value/notes.md:28:## WARWIKI page check
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/value/notes.md:30:Targeted search for “VALUE trial,” “ValUE,” Nager 2012 and the primary DOI found substantive mentions in `docs/02-evaluation/ancillary-tests/urodynamics.mdx:323`, `docs/03-clinical-conditions/03a-storage-incontinence/sui-female.mdx:103`, `docs/07-roots/surgeons/h-r/charles-nager.mdx:35`, and `docs/07-roots/surgeons/a-g/ann-gormley.mdx:46`. **No source-discordant number or statement was found on these pages.** The first two accurately confine omission to demonstrable, uncomplicated/index SUI. Citation-only occurrences were checked and did not make additional trial claims. No WARWIKI page was edited.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/value/notes.md:34:- The main primary article reports any-adverse-event rates (**21.3%** and **19.4%**) and operative-route shares (**64.7%/64.6%** retropubic; **29.0%/28.1%** transobturator) without numerators. It points to Supplementary Appendix Tables 7–8, which were not accessible. These percentages are therefore transcribed from the opened article and cannot be independently recounted from event or procedure counts. No specific mesh exposure, retention, catheterization, infection, or reoperation rate was asserted.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/unblocs/notes.md:4:- Primary report: Hashim H, et al. Lancet 2020;396(10243):50-61, DOI 10.1016/S0140-6736(20)30537-7, PMID 32622397. FULL TEXT read online as CC BY full-text XML from Europe PMC (PMC7339133, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC7339133/fullTextXML): abstract, methods, results, Tables 1 to 7, discussion. No local copy exists (nothing in `reports/audit-v2/sources-local`, `/Users/joyboy/Documents/Medicine` or `/Users/joyboy/Downloads`), so `source.file` is null. Locators are table, section or abstract names, not PDF pages. The appendix and Figure 1 and 2 images were not read; Figure 1 counts come from the Results text.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/unblocs/notes.md:6:- Bibliographic checks: Crossref and PubMed (efetch) for both DOIs (authors, volume, issue, pages). Published dates: Lancet 2020-07-04 (PubMed PubDate; no separate electronic date exists), BJU Int 2020-08-10 (PubMed electronic date).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/unblocs/notes.md:11:- Worthington J, et al. Health Technol Assess 2020;24(41):1-96 (DOI 10.3310/hta24410, PMID 32901611): abstract read via Europe PMC; the NIHR page returned 403 and the NCBI Bookshelf copy (NBK561601) is behind a reCAPTCHA, so no full text. Left out because its abstract repeats the Lancet results. Its abstract states 25% of the ThuVARP arm did not receive the allocated operation and gives the same cost and QALY figures as the BJU Int paper.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/unblocs/notes.md:18:- Lancet internal inconsistency: the Results paragraph on complications says "96 [47%] of 203 men in the TURP group vs 91 [45%] of 204 men in the ThuVARP group", which transposes the arms. The abstract, Table 1 (ThuVARP n=203, TURP n=204) and Table 5 (no complication in 107 ThuVARP versus 113 TURP) agree with 96/203 for ThuVARP and 91/204 for TURP; the trial file follows those.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/unblocs/notes.md:23:- EAU 2026 text says the TURP advantage for Qmax was strengthened in men under 70 and in those with LUTS rather than retention; that comes from the appendix or HTA report, which I did not read, so the page does not state it (the page reports the LUTS-versus-retention interaction p values from the Lancet text).
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/unblocs/notes.md:27:## WARWIKI pages that cite or relate to the trial
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/unblocs/notes.md:28:- `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04m-bph-male-luts/turp.mdx` lines 207 and 235 and reference 20: cite UNBLOCS for "approximately 25,000 UK prostate operations annually ... TURP approximately 80%". This matches the Lancet introduction ("Around 25 000 prostate operations ... around 80%"; Lancet Introduction, paragraph 2). No disagreement.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/unblocs/notes.md:29:- Possible disagreement, `docs/01-foundations/tools/technology/energy-devices.mdx` line 50: "EAU 2026 classifies thulium vaporization as under investigation." EAU 2026 section 5.3.1.c carries a weak recommendation to offer ThuVARP as an alternative to TURP; only "ThuVAP" appears under "Vaporisation techniques under investigation" (section 5.3.3.c). The sentence may conflate the two; the page does not mention UNBLOCS.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/unblocs/notes.md:30:- No other docs page mentions UNBLOCS or ThuVARP. `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04m-bph-male-luts/index.mdx` names "thulium platforms" in a card description only.
