docs/02-evaluation/ancillary-tests/urodynamics.mdx:349:| **UPSTREAM five-year follow-up (2025)** | From 820 randomized men with bothersome LUTS considering further treatment, IPSS difference was 0.41 points (95% CI −1.10 to 1.93), with no detected difference in surgery (OR 0.96, 95% CI 0.71–1.28). Questionnaires were returned by only about half of each arm; routine data were available for 801/820.<sup>[[27]](#ref27)[[29]](#ref29)</sup> | The results do not support adding UDS routinely for every man. **Five-year non-inferiority was not established** because of uncertainty around the symptom estimate; selective testing remains appropriate. |
docs/02-evaluation/ancillary-tests/urodynamics.mdx:389:**Telemetric AUM (TAUM)**: Wireless systems differ in the signals they record. The FDA-cleared Glean system records bladder pressure during natural filling; its April 2026 abdominal-sensor addition permits multichannel pressure recording and calculation of detrusor pressure. The FDA-cleared indication is for adults in a clinic; uroflow volume is not continuous intravesical volume measurement.<sup>[[34]](#ref34)</sup> See the [Glean device hub](/docs/foundations/tools/biomaterials/adjunct-specialty/glean-urodynamics) for current hardware and evidence limitations.<sup>[[34]](#ref34)</sup>
docs/02-evaluation/ancillary-tests/urodynamics.mdx:400:| **Male LUTS** | Use selectively when the result can change management, rather than routinely before all outlet surgery. EAU 2026 weakly recommends urodynamics before invasive treatment only for specific indications: prior unsuccessful invasive treatment, inability to void more than 150 mL, bothersome predominantly voiding LUTS with Qmax above 10 mL/s, and predominantly voiding LUTS at age over 80 or under 50. With Qmax below 10 mL/s EAU considers obstruction likely and urodynamics not necessarily needed, though the cutoff is a weak discriminator on its own: among men with paired uroflowmetry and pressure-flow data in the ICS-'BPH' study (1,271 recruited), a free-flow Qmax below 10 mL/s had 70% specificity, a 70% positive predictive value and only 47% sensitivity for a pressure-flow diagnosis of bladder outlet obstruction. In exploratory UPSTREAM analyses cited by EAU, surgery helped more in men with IPSS above 16, age under 74, Qmax below 10 mL/s, BOOI above 47.6 and BCI above 123.<sup>[[26]](#ref26)[[27]](#ref27)[[45]](#ref45)</sup> |
docs/02-evaluation/ancillary-tests/urodynamics.mdx:470:<a id="ref29"></a>29. Clout M, et al. *Urodynamics tests for the diagnosis and management of male bladder outlet obstruction: long-term follow-up of the UPSTREAM non-inferiority randomised controlled trial.* *Health Technol Assess.* 2025. [doi:10.3310/SLPT4675](https://doi.org/10.3310/SLPT4675). [Report](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK615940/).
docs/05-special-populations/05e-womens-health/recurrent-uti.mdx:137:- **ImpresU trial** (Heltveit-Olsen et al., 2025; 289 women recruited, 281 analyzed; age ≥70 years). The modified intention-to-treat analysis estimated 25% fewer antibiotic treatments for UTI during 6 months with methenamine than placebo (IRR 0.75; 95% CI 0.57–1.0; p = 0.049); the complete-case analysis of 271 women gave IRR 0.77 (95% CI 0.58–1.0; p = 0.078). After discontinuation, the methenamine group had a higher incidence of antibiotic-treated episodes during the six-month follow-up (IRR 1.7; 95% CI 1.3–2.3).<sup>[[26]](#ref26)</sup>
docs/05-special-populations/05e-womens-health/recurrent-uti.mdx:254:- **Antimicrobial stewardship.** Recognition that repetitive antibiotic use causes collateral damage (rising resistance, microbiome disruption, and adverse effects) drives the search for alternatives.
