docs/04-surgical-techniques/04l-cosmetic-genital-surgery/suprapubic-lipectomy.mdx:214:- **Simple versus complex buried penis.** Before choosing liposuction alone, examine the patient standing and supine, compress the fat pad to assess the glans, meatus and viable shaft skin, inspect scrotal skin, and assess voiding symptoms. Suspected meatal or urethral stricture warrants evaluation because it may change the operative plan.<sup>[[33]](#ref33)</sup> Liposuction alone is described for isolated suprapubic fat with examinable, viable penile skin. Diseased skin, lichen sclerosus, scrotal lymphedema or contributory pannus may require multicomponent reconstruction. If the shaft cannot be exposed for examination, counsel about diagnostic uncertainty and plan assessment for occult skin disease.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[7]](#ref7)[[30]](#ref30)</sup> In a retrospective cohort of 203 patients who underwent buried-penis repair with penile-skin removal, pathology found lichen sclerosus in 35/123 nonexhumable cases (28.4%) versus 11/80 exhumable cases (13.8%), and penile cancer in 8/123 (6.5%) versus 2/80 (2.5%); the cancer difference was not statistically significant.<sup>[[30]](#ref30)</sup>
docs/03-clinical-conditions/03c-pelvic-support/obstetric-perineal-injury.mdx:179:Practice lags behind these positions. In a 2025 UK survey of 126 consultants (estimated response rate about 2.5%), 81.7% routinely examined women at the postpartum visit and 57.6% performed a rectal examination, but only 36.5% used an objective incontinence screening tool; 72.6% referred symptomatic and 34.2% asymptomatic women for anorectal studies, EAUS was the modality of choice (70%) and no respondent chose manometry, and almost 80% discharged asymptomatic women within 3 months.<sup>[[34]](#ref34)</sup> Earlier contact is achievable: in a US quality-improvement project (185 OASI cases), a care bundle of education and standardized electronic orders raised follow-up within 2 weeks from 16.3% to 52.8% and shortened mean time to follow-up from 33 to 18 days.<sup>[[35]](#ref35)</sup> In 81 women scanned with 4D introital ultrasound before discharge and at 3 months, residual EAS defects were seen in 17.3% and 13.6%, respectively; early scans were unassessable in 9.9%, showed an apparent defect absent at 3 months in 9.9%, and missed a defect found at 3 months in 4.9%.<sup>[[36]](#ref36)</sup>
docs/05-special-populations/05c-gender-affirming/revision-salvage-gas.mdx:38:Neovaginal prolapse is uncommon. A systematic review of 24 studies totaling 3,166 patients found an overall rate of 2.7% (2.5% after penile-inversion vaginoplasty, 3.5% after intestinal vaginoplasty); elevated BMI was the only identified risk factor, and sacrospinous ligament fixation combined with vaginal packing was the most commonly reported intraoperative prevention measure.[^43] After penile-inversion vaginoplasty, pessary use, vaginal repair and sacrospinous ligament fixation have been described.[^44] Published surgical treatment includes case reports of open or laparoscopic sacrocolpopexy; a 2023 case report documents recurrence after vaginal repair without mesh, followed by robotic sacrocolpopexy using an autologous medial umbilical ligament graft.[^56] Chapter authors caution that sacrocolpopexy in this setting carries a risk of bladder or rectal injury because a distinct rectovaginal septum may be absent.[^44]
