docs/03-clinical-conditions/03c-pelvic-support/apical-prolapse.mdx:83:**Other vault comparisons.** In SALTO-2 (Netherlands; 64 randomized plus a 115-woman prospective cohort), laparoscopic sacrocolpopexy and vaginal sacrospinous fixation had similar quality-of-life scores and 12-month composite success for the apical compartment (89% versus 86% in the RCT), which was a small, short trial.<sup>[[16]](#ref16)</sup> In the UK VUE vault trial (208 women), abdominal and vaginal suspension had similar 12-month symptom scores and serious adverse events, though anterior prolapse stage 2b or greater was less frequent after abdominal repair.<sup>[[15]](#ref15)</sup> An earlier randomized trial of 88 women with cervical prolapse to or beyond the hymen, or vault inversion of more than half its length with anterior vaginal wall descent to or beyond the hymen, compared bilateral sacrospinous suspension with paravaginal repair (n = 48) against abdominal sacral colpopexy with paravaginal repair (n = 40).<sup>[[25]](#ref25)</sup> At a mean 2.5 years (80 evaluated), surgery was optimally effective in 29% of the vaginal and 58% of the abdominal group, and unsatisfactory with reoperation in 33% and 16%; the vaginal group had shorter operating time but more urinary infection and incontinence.<sup>[[25]](#ref25)</sup> In CARE, women with stress continence who had sacrocolpopexy showed rising failure over seven years (anatomic failure 22–27%, composite prolapse failure 34–48% by arm) and a Kaplan-Meier mesh-erosion probability of 10.5%; modeled seven-year SUI failure was 62% with Burch urethropexy versus 77% without (risk difference −15.3 percentage points; 95% CI −26.8 to 3.0).<sup>[[18]](#ref18)</sup>
