docs/02-evaluation/imaging/ct-urogram.mdx:53:Request an excretory phase when the question is a urinary leak and a retrograde cystogram when bladder integrity is the question. Communicate prior surgery, diversion anatomy, stents, drains, renal function and the suspected communication. Delayed contrast in a collection supports a urinary source; absent opacification does not reliably exclude a small leak in a poorly functioning unit.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)[[6]](#ref6)</sup>
docs/02-evaluation/imaging/ct-urogram.mdx:57:For a vesicovaginal fistula, excretory CT or CT cystography can show vaginal contrast, a visible tract, associated ureteral dilation and pelvic changes; cystoscopy maps the fistula in relation to the ureteral orifices.<sup>[[26]](#ref26)</sup> For rectourethral fistula, CTU with a CT cystogram can show the communication, surrounding radiation fibrosis, associated ureteral or bladder involvement and pelvic abscess. In the irradiated pelvis, CT can map bladder wall thickening, ureteral dilation, pelvic masses and adjacent bowel for operative planning.<sup>[[26]](#ref26)</sup>
docs/02-evaluation/imaging/ct-urogram.mdx:111:**Delayed reactions** occur from one hour to about one week after injection; a pruritic rash or urticaria is the most frequent finding, though nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and hypotension can also occur. Severe delayed cutaneous reactions resembling Stevens–Johnson syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis or vasculitis have been reported; progressive or widespread manifestations may merit allergy or dermatology consultation.<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup> In one prospective single-center comparison, a delayed reaction occurred in 14.3% of patients receiving a low-osmolar nonionic agent versus 2.5% of a non-contrast control group.<sup>[[20]](#ref20)</sup> In a randomized, double-blind cardiac-catheterization trial of 2,001 patients, reactions before 24 hours occurred in 22.2% receiving ioxaglate, 7.6% iodixanol and 8.8% iopamidol; skin reactions after 24 hours through day 7 occurred in 4.3%, 12.2% and 4.2%, respectively.<sup>[[21]](#ref21)</sup> A prior iodinated-contrast reaction is associated with greater delayed-reaction risk.<sup>[[18]](#ref18)</sup> Delayed-reaction rates differed between contrast-enhanced and unenhanced CT groups and among cardiac-catheterization agents; a retrospective series of 4,875 patients recorded 52 late skin reactions that clustered seasonally and often involved sun-exposed areas.<sup>[[20]](#ref20)[[21]](#ref21)[[22]](#ref22)</sup>
