docs/02-evaluation/imaging/ultrasound.mdx:132:The direct evidence is limited. Nash and colleagues found SUG unreliable at predicting the depth of spongiofibrosis against full-depth biopsies in 36 patients. In 52 men, Gupta and colleagues reported SUG accuracy for spongiofibrosis of 42%, 56% and 83% for mild, moderate and severe degrees. The EAU states that the clinical relevance of assessing spongiofibrosis preoperatively remains to be established, and it describes sonourethrography with real-time elastography as a qualitative and quantitative option under investigation.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)[[15]](#ref15)[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
