docs/04-surgical-techniques/04k-gender-affirming-surgery/clitoroplasty-preputial-flap.mdx:127:**Urinary outcomes.** In the MtF surgical studies reviewed by Nassiri, meatal stenosis rates range 4–40% and usually require meatotomy.<sup>[[29]](#ref29)</sup> **Misdirected urinary stream**: rates were 9.5–33% in surgeon-reported cohorts versus 33–55% in other reports not from the primary surgeon in the Blasdel 2024 "surgical complication blind spots" analysis.<sup>[[28]](#ref28)</sup> In a retrospective before-and-after comparison (64 patients with labia minora and clitoral hood creation versus 103 without), Raigosa 2020 reported lower neomeatal stenosis (1.5% versus 15.5%, p = .003), lower hemorrhage (12.5% versus 31%, p = .006) and fewer aesthetic refinements (4.6% versus 20.3%, p = .004) with labia-minora and clitoral-hood creation.<sup>[[27]](#ref27)</sup> Wound dehiscence was higher in the labia-minora group (20.3% versus 9.7%, p = .05).<sup>[[27]](#ref27)</sup> This is the closest existing evidence on how periclitoral construction technique affects urethral outcomes.<sup>[[27]](#ref27)</sup>
