docs/03-clinical-conditions/03d-nlutd/neurogenic-bladder.mdx:202:| **Augmentation cystoplasty (including ileocystoplasty)** | **Refractory poor compliance or high storage pressures** despite medical therapy; hostile bladder | AUA/SUFU: may be offered in selected patients. EAU: strong recommendation when compliance is low or NDO refractory. An EAU-cited review of adult neuro-urological bladder augmentation, without an ileocystoplasty-only estimate, reports perforation 1.9%, mucus production 12.5%, metabolic abnormalities 3.35%, bowel dysfunction 15% and stones 10%. Lifelong follow-up after bowel-segment reconstruction includes at least annual focused history, examination, symptom review, basic metabolic panel, and urinary-tract imaging <Rec>(AUA/SUFU statement 58, Expert Opinion)</Rec>; investigate cancer warning symptoms rather than perform routine screening cystoscopy.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)[[9]](#ref9)</sup> |
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04k-gender-affirming-surgery/phallus-preserving-vaginoplasty.mdx:76:- **Congenital vaginal absence (MRKH) comparisons.** A randomized trial of 40 patients (26 peritoneal, 14 sigmoid) found less blood loss and shorter surgery with peritoneal vaginoplasty, similar neovaginal length and sexual satisfaction, and more abdominal discomfort and odor with sigmoid.<sup>[[14]](#ref14)</sup> A retrospective comparison of 50 patients (20 peritoneal, 30 sigmoid) reported shorter operative time, earlier oral intake, shorter mold use and higher sexual satisfaction with peritoneal vaginoplasty.<sup>[[13]](#ref13)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04k-gender-affirming-surgery/penile-inversion-vaginoplasty.mdx:95:A 2024 systematic review of 24 studies (n = 3,166; mean follow-up 22.5 months) reported neovaginal prolapse in 2.5% of penile-inversion cases.<sup>[[46]](#ref46)</sup> Sacrospinous-ligament or prostate-capsule fixation has been described for prevention, distinct from sacropromontory fixation used for sigmoid neovaginas (see [Intestinal Vaginoplasty](/docs/surgical-techniques/04k-gender-affirming-surgery/intestinal-vaginoplasty)).<sup>[[35]](#ref35)[[40]](#ref40)</sup> In a retrospective series of 62 patients undergoing right-sided sacrospinous fixation with a Deschamps ligature carrier passed medial to the ischial spine, fixation was achieved in all patients, 42 patients reported normal intercourse, and a minor anterior-vaginal-wall bulge in 3 patients resolved with simple excision.<sup>[[40]](#ref40)</sup> The penile-skin-plus-urethral-flap variant has also been fixed to the sacrospinous ligament, with good cosmetic and functional results in 77 of 89 patients (87%) and one rectovaginal fistula from intraoperative rectal injury in the same series.<sup>[[15]](#ref15)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04k-gender-affirming-surgery/penile-inversion-vaginoplasty.mdx:101:Reported complication rates vary with definition and follow-up: Gaither found postoperative complications in 95 of 330 primary PIV patients (reported as 28.7%; median follow-up 3 months), whereas Dreher reported 32.5% across 1,684 patients in 13 heterogeneous vaginoplasty studies.<sup>[[19]](#ref19)[[31]](#ref31)</sup> These estimates are not directly comparable: Gaither reported a single-surgeon retrospective series with median follow-up of 3 months, shorter than its 4.4-month median time to complication, whereas Dreher pooled 13 heterogeneous studies.<sup>[[19]](#ref19)[[31]](#ref31)</sup> Most complications are minor (Clavien–Dindo I–II).<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[16]](#ref16)[[31]](#ref31)</sup>
