docs/01-foundations/pharmacology/infection-prophylaxis/non-antibiotic-uti-prevention.mdx:143:- **MERIT RCT** (Hayward 2024, *JAMA Intern Med*): double-blind, placebo-controlled, 598 women with rUTI randomized from UK primary care; among 583 with primary-outcome data, 150/294 (51.0%) versus 161/289 (55.7%) contacted ambulatory care for a clinically suspected UTI over 6 months (primary-outcome risk difference −5 percentage points; 95% CI −13 to 3), with no significant difference in symptom burden, time to next UTI, antibiotic use, or serious adverse events, and similar results across pre-/postmenopausal subgroups<sup>[[16]](#ref16)</sup>
docs/01-foundations/pharmacology/infection-prophylaxis/non-antibiotic-uti-prevention.mdx:146:**Reconciling the discrepancy:** the favorable trials were open-label (vulnerable to expectation effects on symptom reporting and healthcare-seeking), enrolled women at the point of active cystitis, and recruited from specialist urology clinics; MERIT was blinded, enrolled women between infections, and recruited from primary care. A 2026 review proposed setting-specific use, but no responder subgroup is validated; counseling includes the negative blinded evidence.<sup>[[39]](#ref39)[[43]](#ref43)</sup>
docs/01-foundations/pharmacology/infection-prophylaxis/non-antibiotic-uti-prevention.mdx:154:Generally well tolerated; diarrhea is the most common adverse event, with rare vaginal burning, and MERIT found no excess serious adverse events.<sup>[[16]](#ref16)[[35]](#ref35)</sup> Adverse-event reporting in D-mannose trials was sparse, and glycemic effects during prophylaxis in patients with diabetes have not been established.<sup>[[35]](#ref35)</sup>
docs/01-foundations/pharmacology/infection-prophylaxis/non-antibiotic-uti-prevention.mdx:175:**Xyloglucan and herbal combinations.** EAU 2026 also weakly advises a xyloglucan-hibiscus-propolis combination, or the herbal combination of Centaurii herba, Levistici radix and Rosmarini folium, to reduce recurrent cystitis and antibiotic use. The EAU cites a meta-analysis of three RCTs (n = 178) for the xyloglucan combination in acute-cystitis treatment studies; its pooled endpoint was clinical or microbiological success (cure or improvement at the end of treatment, or microbiological resolution), and a small RCT (n = 90) in which the herbal combination was added to antimicrobial therapy and recurrence was assessed at 6 and 12 months; neither appears in the AUA 2025 recommendations.<sup>[[47]](#ref47)</sup>
