docs/02-evaluation/imaging/cystography.mdx:24:CT is not automatically superior for every indication. A low-dose conventional examination may answer a follow-up leak question without repeating a pelvic CT. Neither a routine contrast-enhanced abdominal CT nor its excretory phase substitutes for a properly performed retrograde cystogram.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)[[4]](#ref4)</sup> For suspected bladder injury after pelvic surgery, EAU strongly recommends postoperative cystography; direct inspection or cystoscopy evaluates a suspected injury during the operation.<sup>[[23]](#ref23)</sup>
docs/02-evaluation/imaging/cystography.mdx:48:An adult trauma volume is not forced into a child, a painful low-capacity bladder, a fresh repair or an augmented reservoir. Agree on a tailored filling endpoint with the treating team. Clamping a catheter to collect renally excreted contrast does not provide a reliable trauma cystogram.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)[[5]](#ref5)[[13]](#ref13)</sup>
docs/02-evaluation/imaging/cystography.mdx:58:In stable patients, AUA also recommends retrograde cystography for gross hematuria with a concerning mechanism or pelvic-ring fracture with clinical indicators of rupture (Statement 14b; Recommendation, Grade C).<sup>[[13]](#ref13)</sup> Concerning findings include inability to void, suprapubic pain, abdominal distension, unexplained low urine output, urinary ascites or otherwise unexplained pelvic fluid.<sup>[[13]](#ref13)</sup> A cystogram does not evaluate renal or ureteral injury adequately; choose upper-tract imaging separately.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
docs/02-evaluation/imaging/cystography.mdx:108:Historical small cohorts illustrate why a negative cystogram is insufficient. Melchior's 49-patient surgical series detected CVF with cystography in 5/30, CT in 25/41, and the poppy-seed test in 35/37 tested patients.<sup>[[14]](#ref14)</sup> These are cohort-specific yields with different denominators, not universal accuracy estimates or evidence that the poppy-seed test replaces anatomic investigation. It does not define the tract or exclude cancer.<sup>[[14]](#ref14)</sup> Najjar's 12-patient series likewise favored CT followed by evaluation for colonic malignancy.<sup>[[15]](#ref15)</sup>
docs/02-evaluation/imaging/cystography.mdx:118:New abdominal symptoms or unexplained systemic illness after augmentation require urgent urologic assessment, and a negative cystogram does not exclude perforation. Sensory impairment may blunt abdominal findings. Braverman's historical series found leakage on 12/13 cystograms, whereas Glass and Rushton's four-patient series demonstrated an abnormal cystogram in only one case despite confirmed rupture; ultrasound detected free fluid in all four.<sup>[[17]](#ref17)[[19]](#ref19)</sup>
docs/02-evaluation/imaging/cystography.mdx:138:After colovesical fistula repair, a postoperative cystogram may be reserved for a large defect or complex repair; the supporting 2025 Pau study was retrospective, with only 18 postoperative cystograms.<sup>[[22]](#ref22)</sup> After vesicovaginal fistula repair, a cystogram before catheter removal can theoretically miss a persistent fistula without a voiding phase or when the fistula lies at the bladder neck.<sup>[[34]](#ref34)</sup> After augmentation or neobladder construction, the operating team's reconstruction-specific pathway determines whether and when cystography or pouchography is needed.
docs/02-evaluation/imaging/cystography.mdx:142:*CT cystography for a postoperative leak check after ureteral reimplantation, using dilute gadolinium-based contrast in a patient with a prior severe iodinated contrast reaction. A, coronal unenhanced CT MIP before intravesical contrast; B, coronal CT cystogram MIP after intravesical dilute gadolinium-based contrast via a Foley catheter, with contrast opacifying the anastomosis (arrow) and no leak. From Holmes B, et al. Urol Case Rep. 2024;57:102856, Fig. 1 (CC BY-NC 4.0).<sup>[[28]](#ref28)</sup>*
docs/02-evaluation/imaging/cystography.mdx:146:After radical prostatectomy, cystography before catheter removal can identify a substantial vesicourethral-anastomotic leak. Men with LUTS, large prostates, prior TURP, bladder-neck reconstruction or intraoperative leakage may benefit most, but EAU states that heterogeneous data preclude a recommendation on routine use.<sup>[[35]](#ref35)</sup> Static images can also be read for bladder-neck position. The bladder-neck-to-pubic-symphysis (BNPS) ratio — the distance from the superior pubic-symphysis border to the bladder neck, divided by total pubic-symphysis height — is lower in continent men and rises with worse post-prostatectomy incontinence. In a retrospective series of 611 consecutive RARP patients, 215 had a postoperative cystogram: mean BNPS at 3 months was 0.39 in continent men versus 0.49 in incontinent men; at 12 months it was 0.40 versus 0.60 (12-month incontinence 2.8% in the lowest BNPS tertile versus 19.4% in the highest).<sup>[[26]](#ref26)</sup> A separate 203-patient laparoscopic/robotic cohort found a BNPS ratio above 0.59 was the only factor significantly associated with persistent incontinence at every follow-up interval to 12 months.<sup>[[27]](#ref27)</sup>
docs/02-evaluation/imaging/cystography.mdx:208:<a id="ref26"></a>26. Olgin G, Alsyouf M, Han D, et al. "Postoperative cystogram findings predict incontinence following robot-assisted radical prostatectomy." *J Endourol.* 2014;28(12):1460-3. doi:[10.1089/end.2014.0236](https://doi.org/10.1089/end.2014.0236)
docs/02-evaluation/imaging/cystography.mdx:212:<a id="ref28"></a>28. Holmes B, Sanampudi S, Ananthakrishnan L. "Diagnostic CT cystography with diluted gadolinium-based contrast: a viable alternative to an iodinated contrast-based cystogram." *Urol Case Rep.* 2024;57:102856. doi:[10.1016/j.eucr.2024.102856](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eucr.2024.102856)
docs/05-special-populations/05a-trauma-emergencies/testicular-torsion.mdx:148:A systematic review of 30 studies included 2,116 torsion patients overall. Among the 1,283 patients reported in six-hour time intervals, testicular survival was 97.2% at 0–6 hours, 79.3% at 7–12 hours, 61.3% at 13–18 hours, 42.5% at 19–24 hours, 24.4% at 25–48 hours, and 7.4% beyond 48 hours. Survival meant retention of the testis, irrespective of later atrophy or impaired function; these data support exploration even after prolonged symptoms.<sup>[[26]](#ref26)</sup>
