docs/01-foundations/pharmacology/infection-prophylaxis/prosthetic-infection-biofilm.mdx:80:- Carson 2004 retrospective manufacturer-form cohort: infection at 180 days was reported in 0.68% of 2,261 InhibiZone versus 1.61% of 1,944 untreated primary implants; mean follow-up was 5 versus 8 months. Carson 2011 voluntary manufacturer registry: infection-related revision occurred in 1.1% of 35,737 treated versus 2.5% of 3,268 untreated initial implants, with follow-up up to 7.7 years.<sup>[[13]](#ref13)[[14]](#ref14)</sup>
docs/03-clinical-conditions/03d-nlutd/nlutd-spina-bifida.mdx:78:- **Reoperation is common.** In a 413-patient spina bifida augmentation cohort (median follow-up 11 years), 10-year risk of any additional surgery was 44%: bladder stones 28% (recurrence in about half), perforation 9.6% with detubularized reconfigured ileocystoplasty versus 23.7% without, reaugmentation 5.3% versus 15.2%, bowel obstruction 4.5% and conversion to diversion 2.7%.<sup>[[14]](#ref14)</sup> An EAU-cited systematic review of adult neuro-urological bladder augmentation studies reported perforation 1.9%, mucus production 12.5%, metabolic abnormalities 3.35%, bowel dysfunction 15% and stones 10%; the underlying studies were heterogeneous and narratively synthesized.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup> Perforation in adults tracks with missed catheterization and substance use, so screen adherence before offering augmentation.
