docs/05-special-populations/05a-trauma-emergencies/ureteral-trauma.mdx:17:- In one traumatic-injury series, 5/40 injuries (12.5%) were missed at initial laparotomy.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup> Delayed diagnosis increases morbidity<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
docs/05-special-populations/05a-trauma-emergencies/ureteral-trauma.mdx:18:- **Iatrogenic** injury during pelvic surgery is a distinct population; one 103-patient series found delayed recognition in 20%, with more subsequent procedures and health-care use.<sup>[[6]](#ref6)</sup> A review reported that laparoscopic gynecologic surgery accounted for 64% of iatrogenic ureteral injuries, versus 26% from general surgery and 11% from urologic procedures; in separate procedure-type data, intraoperative detection was 12.5% for laparoscopic procedures, 43.5% for open procedures, and 62.5% for endourologic procedures.<sup>[[11]](#ref11)</sup> A 2015 review cited older reports of ureteral injury in 2.2% of hysterectomies and routine pelvic gynecologic procedures, up to 30% of radical hysterectomies, and up to 6% of laparoscopic hysterectomies.<sup>[[11]](#ref11)</sup> In a California inpatient cohort of 223,872 hysterectomies (2007–2011), ureteral injury was diagnosed within one year in 1,753 patients (0.78%); 1,094 of these injuries (62.4%) were not recognized on the day of surgery.<sup>[[25]](#ref25)</sup>
