docs/04-surgical-techniques/04g-prolapse-repair/apical/sacrocolpopexy.mdx:96:In E-CARE, the extension of an older open abdominal sacrocolpopexy trial, estimated seven-year anatomic failure was 27% with Burch and 22% without Burch; composite failure was 48% and 34%, respectively. These estimates use different definitions from ASPIRe and have substantial uncertainty and attrition: 215 entered the extension and 126 completed seven-year follow-up.<sup>[[8]](#ref8)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04g-prolapse-repair/apical/sacrocolpopexy.mdx:110:E-CARE estimated mesh erosion at 10.5% by seven years (95% CI 6.8–16.1), using older and varied operative materials.<sup>[[8]](#ref8)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04g-prolapse-repair/apical/sacrocolpopexy.mdx:136:In CARE, 322 women without preoperative SUI symptoms were randomized during open abdominal sacrocolpopexy. After the journal's 2016 coding correction, the three-month composite SUI endpoint was 33.6% with Burch versus 57.4% without Burch.<sup>[[10]](#ref10)</sup> CARE’s composite included symptoms, stress testing or treatment; its rate is not a risk estimate for bothersome leakage.<sup>[[9]](#ref9)[[10]](#ref10)</sup>
docs/02-evaluation/laboratory-studies/nutritional-assessment/vitamin-a.mdx:129:β-carotene does not ordinarily cause classic retinol toxicity; excess dietary carotenoids may discolor skin. High-dose β-carotene supplements are not harmless, however. In the randomized ATBC trial, 29,133 Finnish male smokers were assigned to β-carotene 20 mg/day or no β-carotene for five to eight years; supplementation increased lung-cancer incidence by 18% (95% CI 3–36).<sup>[[18]](#ref18)</sup> In CARET (18,314 smokers, former smokers and asbestos-exposed participants), β-carotene plus retinyl palmitate increased lung cancer, RR 1.28 (1.04–1.57), and all-cause mortality, RR 1.17 (1.03–1.33).<sup>[[19]](#ref19)</sup> Do not use these supplements for cancer prevention.<sup>[[18]](#ref18)[[19]](#ref19)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04d-upper-tract-reconstruction/anastomosis-repair/ureteroureterostomy.mdx:248:- When the distal stump is intact, UU can avoid bladder dissection; a retrospective laparoscopic iatrogenic-injury series reported shorter operative time and less blood loss than ureteroneocystostomy.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
docs/01-foundations/pharmacology/dermatologic-topical-urethral/high-potency-topical-corticosteroids.mdx:24:| **VI–VII — Low** | Low | Hydrocortisone 1–2.5%, desonide 0.05%, alclometasone 0.05%<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup> |
docs/01-foundations/pharmacology/dermatologic-topical-urethral/high-potency-topical-corticosteroids.mdx:254:| **Genital psoriasis** | Vulval disease: mid-potency steroid followed by low potency (hydrocortisone 1–2.5% is a low-potency option); penile disease: moderate-potency steroid initially (BASHH 2026, Grade 1C) | VI–VII or III–V, formulation-dependent | Adjust duration and frequency to the selected steroid and clinical response | Intermittent | Calcineurin inhibitors are off-label steroid-sparing options; calcipotriene may irritate genital skin.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)[[18]](#ref18)[[19]](#ref19)[[20]](#ref20)[[38]](#ref38)[[43]](#ref43)</sup> |
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04d-upper-tract-reconstruction/interposition-graft/ileal-ureter.mdx:97:9. **Stent and drain.** Provide intraluminal urinary drainage and Foley drainage; use an intra-abdominal drain according to the repair. No consensus device-removal schedule applies across reported series. In one robotic case report, the Foley catheter was removed on postoperative day 10 after a normal cystogram and a single intraluminal 15 Ch drain was removed endoscopically at four weeks; no intra-abdominal drain was placed.<sup>[[24]](#ref24)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04g-prolapse-repair/apical/sacrospinous-hysteropexy.mdx:70:SUPeR tested polypropylene Uphold LITE mesh sacrospinous hysteropexy against vaginal hysterectomy with bilateral USLS in postmenopausal women with prolapse beyond the hymen. The report tabulated cumulative first-failure proportions of 29/79 (37%) versus 42/78 (54%) at the five-year visit; these are not reoperation rates. The interval-censored primary analysis favored mesh hysteropexy (adjusted hazard ratio 0.58, 95% CI 0.36–0.94), but proportional hazards were not met, so that ratio summarizes a changing effect over follow-up. The specific mesh product is no longer marketed in the United States for transvaginal POP repair.<sup>[[11]](#ref11)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04g-prolapse-repair/apical/sacrospinous-ligament-fixation.mdx:93:**ASPIRe (2024)** is the higher-yield contemporary vault-prolapse trial: 376 women were randomized, 360 underwent surgery and 358 contributed to the primary analysis. Three-year modeled composite failure was 28% with sacrocolpopexy versus 43% with vaginal native-tissue repair (adjusted HR 0.57, 99% CI 0.33–0.98; P=.008). Among 134 native-tissue procedures actually performed, including crossovers, 110 (82%) were SSLF and 24 (18%) were uterosacral suspension. The result supports counseling about durability after total-hysterectomy vault prolapse.<sup>[[6]](#ref6)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04g-prolapse-repair/mesh-complications.mdx:63:In the Miklos 2016 three-center surgical-removal series (445 patients; 587 classified complications), the most common classification categories were spontaneous pain (1Be, 32.5%) and dyspareunia (1Bc, 14.7%); most patients presented &gt; 1 year after implantation (mean 3.68 years).<sup>[[6]](#ref6)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04g-prolapse-repair/mesh-complications.mdx:71:| Mesh erosion / exposure | 11.8% (transvaginal permanent mesh trials)<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup> | 1.9% (pooled SUI mesh studies)<sup>[[7]](#ref7)</sup> | 10.5% estimated at 7 years (abdominal CARE cohort)<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup> |

