## docs/03-clinical-conditions/03f-fistulas/in-females/vesicovaginal.mdx
139 
140 ### Surgical principles (apply to every approach)
141 
142 1. **Cystoscopic mapping.** Confirm fistula location and rule out ureteral involvement; place ureteral stents if either orifice is within 1 cm of the tract
143 2. **Exposure with viable tissue preservation.** Mobilize bladder and vagina for a flap-splitting repair; Latzko repair instead denudes the surrounding vaginal epithelium while retaining the tract.<sup>[[36]](#ref36)[[37]](#ref37)</sup>
144 3. **Selective fistula-edge / tract management.** EAU describes one RCT, while a 2024 systematic review identified two RCTs (140 women); the trials did not show improved closure with trimming, and failed trimmed repairs may leave larger recurrent defects. Tailor trimming, freshening or excision to approach, fibrosis, tissue viability and defect size.<sup>[[22]](#ref22)[[24]](#ref24)[[37]](#ref37)[[38]](#ref38)</sup>
145 4. **Tension-free closure suited to the approach.** Flap-splitting repair closes bladder mucosa and detrusor/fibromuscular tissue separately from vaginal wall, with offset suture lines; Latzko repair retains the tract, places a purse-string outside it, imbricates denuded vaginal tissue, then closes the vaginal epithelium.<sup>[[36]](#ref36)[[37]](#ref37)</sup>
146 5. **Vascularized tissue interposition when indicated.** Not routine for simple, non-irradiated primary VVF; strongly favored for radiation, recurrent, large, mesh-associated, or tissue-deficient fistulas<sup>[[7]](#ref7)[[18]](#ref18)[[26]](#ref26)[[27]](#ref27)</sup>
147 6. **Continuous catheter drainage.** WHO recommends 7–10 days after repair of a simple obstetric urinary fistula, supported by a noninferiority RCT that randomized 524 women with simple female genital fistulas (mostly obstetric) whose repairs remained closed at postoperative day 7; by 3 months, repair breakdown in the 501-patient per-protocol analysis was 10/250 (4.0%) after 7-day versus 8/251 (3.2%) after 14-day drainage (radiation and complex fistulas excluded).<sup>[[39]](#ref39)[[40]](#ref40)</sup> A 2026 IUGA/ICS Delphi consensus advises a minimum of 10–14 days of catheter drainage after obstetric VVF repair and a dye test at catheter removal.<sup>[[58]](#ref58)</sup> For post-surgical and complex repairs, EAU states that no high-level evidence determines catheter duration and weakly recommends continuous drainage until healing is confirmed; expert opinion suggests 10–14 days for simple or post-surgical and 14–21 days for complex or post-radiation fistulas.<sup>[[37]](#ref37)</sup
148 7. **Bladder rest.** Anticholinergics to suppress detrusor activity and avoid suture-line stress
