## docs/03-clinical-conditions/03f-fistulas/in-females/obstetric.mdx
175 - Mobilize sufficiently for tension-free closure while preserving viable tissue and blood supply; avoid unnecessary scar or edge excision.
176 - Edges must coapt without tension
177 - The repair should be watertight at the time of closure
178 - **Closure pattern.** Flap-splitting repair uses layered, non-overlapping suture lines; Latzko partial colpocleisis uses imbrication instead.<sup>[[34]](#ref34)</sup>
179 - **Circumferential defect** (bladder completely detached from the residual urethra). Mobilizing the bladder off the vagina and surrounding tissue and extending the dissection behind the pubic symphysis, high into the space of Retzius, allows the bladder to be advanced as a unit and anastomosed to the remaining urethra; this approach has been described for this rare, severe injury pattern.<sup>[[43]](#ref43)</sup>
180 - Continuous drainage until healing is confirmed. WHO recommends 7–10 days for simple obstetric fistulas.<sup>[[35]](#ref35)</sup> In the WHO-supported multicountry RCT, 524 women were randomized after a simple repair remained closed on day 7; multiple, radiation-related and cancer-related fistulas were excluded.<sup>[[39]](#ref39)</sup> In the per-protocol analysis, repair breakdown between 8 days after catheter removal and three months after surgery occurred in 10/250 (4%) after 7 days of catheterization versus 8/251 (3%) after 14 days, meeting the prespecified 10% noninferiority margin.<sup>[[39]](#ref39)</sup> The 2026 IUGA/ICS Delphi consensus sets a minimum of 10–14 days of continuous drainage after obstetric VVF repair (statement 37), noting that 7 days may suffice for simple cases; complex repairs need individualized longer drainage.<sup>[[36]](#ref36)</sup> These recommendations have different scopes and evidence bases.<sup>[[35]](#ref35)[[36]](#ref36)[[39]](#ref39)</sup> Once
181 - **Tissue interposition** (Martius, gracilis, omental flap) for large defects, urethral involvement, recurrent fistula, or radiation; the [Browning data](/docs/clinical-conditions/03f-fistulas/in-females/vesicovaginal#tissue-interposition) suggest interposition is not a default for routine obstetric VVF<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[14]](#ref14)</sup>
