## docs/01-foundations/pharmacology/infection-prophylaxis/non-antibiotic-uti-prevention.mdx
138 
139 - **Kranjčec 2014:** open-label 3-arm RCT (n = 308) in women with acute cystitis and rUTI history: 6-month recurrence 14.6% (D-mannose 2 g/day) versus 20.4% (nitrofurantoin 50 mg/day) versus 60.8% (no prophylaxis)<sup>[[36]](#ref36)</sup>
140 - **Porru 2014:** randomized crossover (n = 60): mean time to recurrence 200 days (D-mannose) versus 52.7 days (co-trimoxazole)<sup>[[37]](#ref37)</sup>
141 - **Lenger 2020 meta-analysis:** pooled RR 0.23 (95% CI 0.14–0.37; 125 D-mannose recipients and 123 controls) versus no prophylaxis, from the Kranjčec RCT and a prospective cohort; the Porru crossover study contributed to the separate antibiotic comparison<sup>[[38]](#ref38)</sup>
142 - **Han 2025 network meta-analysis:** largest single-agent effect among non-antibiotic interventions (RR 0.34; 95% CI 0.21–0.56; only a cranberry-probiotic-vitamin A triple combination ranked higher), again weighted by smaller / older studies<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
143 - **MERIT RCT** (Hayward 2024, *JAMA Intern Med*): double-blind, placebo-controlled, 598 women with rUTI randomized from UK primary care; among 583 with primary-outcome data, 150/294 (51.0%) versus 161/289 (55.7%) contacted ambulatory care for a clinically suspected UTI over 6 months (primary-outcome risk difference −5 percentage points; 95% CI −13 to 3), with no significant difference in symptom burden, time to next UTI, antibiotic use, or serious adverse events, and similar results across pre-/postmenopausal subgroups<sup>[[16]](#ref16)</sup>
144 - **WikiGuidelines 2024** concludes insufficient evidence to recommend for or against<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
145 
146 **Reconciling the discrepancy:** the favorable trials were open-label (vulnerable to expectation effects on symptom reporting and healthcare-seeking), enrolled women at the point of active cystitis, and recruited from specialist urology clinics; MERIT was blinded, enrolled women between infections, and recruited from primary care. A 2026 review proposed setting-specific use, but no responder subgroup is validated; counseling includes the negative blinded evidence.<sup>[[39]](#ref39)[[43]](#ref43)</sup>
147 
148 ### Dosing (when used despite the equivocal evidence)
149 
150 No standardized dose; early pilot studies tested about 200 mg to 2–3 g of D-mannose, with regimens varying by study.<sup>[[35]](#ref35)</sup> Typical regimen 2 g PO once daily or 1 g BID dissolved in water (the 2 g/day dose used in both Kranjčec and MERIT). Routine prophylactic use is not supported by the largest blinded trial; no prospectively validated responder subgroup establishes a specialist-only exception.<sup>[[16]](#ref16)[[43]](#ref43)</sup>
151 
152 ### Safety
153 
154 Generally well tolerated; diarrhea is the most common adverse event, with rare vaginal burning, and MERIT found no excess serious adverse events.<sup>[[16]](#ref16)[[35]](#ref35)</sup> Adverse-event reporting in D-mannose trials was sparse, and glycemic effects during prophylaxis in patients with diabetes have not been established.<sup>[[35]](#ref35)</sup>
155 
156 ### Prevention only — not treatment

