160 ## Follow-up, retreatment and durability
161 
162 EAU 2026 gives a weak recommendation to follow up patients after surgery and describes review four to six weeks after catheter removal to assess response and adverse events, including IPSS/ICIQ-MLUTS, uroflowmetry, PVR, and erectile and ejaculatory function.<sup>[[25]](#ref25)</sup>
163 
164 | Source | Population | Surgical retreatment |
165 |---|---|---|
166 | WATER (30–80 mL) | 5 yr | 5.1% surgical retreatment<sup>[[25]](#ref25)</sup> |
167 | WATER II (80–150 mL) | 5 yr | 3/101 (3.0%) underwent BPH surgery; 96.3% Kaplan–Meier freedom<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup> |
168 | Single-center, n = 330 | Up to 4 yr | 10/330 (3.0%) underwent TURP for residual anterior tissue (BPH retreatment); separately, 13/330 (3.9%) had any second procedure, including 2 for bleeding and 1 for bladder-neck disruption<sup>[[17]](#ref17)</sup> |
169 | Shin 2024 BJU Int network meta-analysis<sup>[[22]](#ref22)</sup> | 12-mo reintervention ranking | Ranked 2nd, behind TURP |
170 | Akgul 2025 systematic review<sup>[[23]](#ref23)</sup> | 5 yr | 4.4–6% |
171 
172 For comparison, the Akgul review reported surgical reintervention of 13.6% for UroLift and 11.1% for Rezūm at 5 years, 11.1% for iTIND at 4 years, 2.5% for Optilume BPH at 2 years, and 21% for PAE at 2 years.<sup>[[23]](#ref23)</sup> The Rezūm five-year pivotal trial reported 4.4% surgical retreatment, also reported by EAU 2026.<sup>[[25]](#ref25)</sup> It noted that industry sponsorship was high-risk in 65% of the included studies and that follow-up varied.<sup>[[23]](#ref23)</sup> The 2026 EAU endourology perspective describes AEEP as the reference standard for size-independent treatment, with long-term retreatment of about 1–2%.<sup>[[10]](#ref10)</sup>
173 
