## docs/04-surgical-techniques/04m-bph-male-luts/greenlight-pvp.mdx
55 - Obstructing median lobes can be treated.
56 - Patients of high ASA class who may not tolerate TURP have been included in these series.<sup>[[10]](#ref10)[[11]](#ref11)</sup>
57 - A PVR &gt; 250 mL was not a fixed exclusion in a 2024 retrospective GreenLight cohort that included men with chronic retention (defined as PVR &gt; 300 mL in those able to void). At 12 months, 77/88 men (87.5%) treated for preoperative retention were catheter-free, versus 80/80 (100%) treated for LUTS without retention.<sup>[[33]](#ref33)</sup>
58 
59 ---
60 
61 ## Clinical efficacy: equivalence to TURP (GOLIATH)
62 
63 GOLIATH is the main RCT supporting PVP. It randomized 281 men to 180 W XPS PVP or TURP at 29 centers in 9 European countries. Patients and physicians were not blinded; men unable to stop anticoagulants or antiplatelet drugs for 3–5 days, apart from low-dose aspirin, were excluded.<sup>[[12]](#ref12)[[13]](#ref13)[[14]](#ref14)</sup>
64 
65 - XPS was noninferior to TURP for IPSS, Qmax and the complication-free proportion at 6 months, and noninferiority was maintained at 12 and 24 months.
66 - Prostate volume, PSA and PVR reductions did not differ significantly between arms at 12 months.
67 - The 24-month complication-free rate was 83.6% (XPS) versus 78.9% (TURP).<sup>[[14]](#ref14)</sup>
68 - Time to stable health, catheterization time and length of stay were shorter with XPS.
69 - Early reintervention within 30 days was three times higher after TURP (p = 0.025), and overall reintervention rates were not significantly different.<sup>[[12]](#ref12)</sup>
70 
