docs/04-surgical-techniques/04m-bph-male-luts/aquablation.mdx:226:Compared with HoLEP, Aquablation had a smaller 12-month PVR reduction in the published network analysis, while its 12-month Qmax difference was imprecise.<sup>[[25]](#ref25)[[40]](#ref40)</sup> Separate long-term cohorts report about 3–8% surgical retreatment after Aquablation and 1–2% after AEEP, but WATER III has only 3-month comparative follow-up, so relative long-term durability remains untested.<sup>[[10]](#ref10)[[15]](#ref15)</sup> Aquablation also requires the AquaBeam robotic system. Limitations versus office-based MISTs (UroLift, Rezūm, iTIND, Optilume BPH) are the need for general or regional anesthesia and an OR setting. Bleeding remains a consent and perioperative-planning issue, and post-market reporting should be read with the limitations described above.<sup>[[11]](#ref11)[[26]](#ref26)</sup>
