147:6. **Continuous catheter drainage.** WHO recommends 7–10 days after repair of a simple obstetric urinary fistula, supported by a noninferiority RCT that randomized 524 women with simple female genital fistulas (mostly obstetric) whose repairs remained closed at postoperative day 7; by 3 months, repair breakdown in the 501-patient per-protocol analysis was 10/250 (4.0%) after 7-day versus 8/251 (3.2%) after 14-day drainage (radiation and complex fistulas excluded).<sup>[[39]](#ref39)[[40]](#ref40)</sup> A 2026 IUGA/ICS Delphi consensus advises a minimum of 10–14 days of catheter drainage after obstetric VVF repair and a dye test at catheter removal.<sup>[[58]](#ref58)</sup> For post-surgical and complex repairs, EAU states that no high-level evidence determines catheter duration and weakly recommends continuous drainage until healing is confirmed; expert opinion suggests 10–14 days for simple or post-surgical and 14–21 days for complex or post-radiation fistulas.<sup>[[37]](#ref37)</sup> Individualize duration and leak testing; a cystogram can miss a persistent fistula if it has no voiding phase or if the fistula is at the bladder neck.<sup>[[37]](#ref37)[[39]](#ref39)[[40]](#ref40)</sup>
