docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/implantable-tibial-nerve-stimulation.mdx:86:The pivotal OASIS trial enrolled 151 women with UUI. The primary endpoint, ≥50% reduction in UUI episodes 6 months after activation, was met by 76.4% (95% CI 68.7% to 82.6%) in the intention-to-treat analysis; a post hoc 12-month post-activation ITT analysis gave 78.4%.<sup>[[24]](#ref24)[[27]](#ref27)</sup> The two-year report gives 78% at 6 months among participants with diaries, and 79% of the 90 participants with 36-month diaries were responders.<sup>[[8]](#ref8)[[9]](#ref9)</sup> Quality-of-life improvements were clinically significant across all OAB-q domains. At 3 years, 83 of 87 survey respondents (95%) reported therapeutic satisfaction, and 74 of 90 participants (82%) reported feeling "much better" or "very much better".<sup>[[8]](#ref8)</sup> No device- or procedure-related serious adverse events occurred through 36 months.<sup>[[8]](#ref8)[[9]](#ref9)[[11]](#ref11)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/implantable-tibial-nerve-stimulation.mdx:90:The FDA pivotal study included 133 implanted or attempted-implant participants in the ITT population, with 68% achieving ≥50% reduction in UUI episodes after 48 weeks of stimulation.<sup>[[18]](#ref18)</sup> A smaller observed extension cohort reported 78% responders and 22% dry at 96 weeks; its denominator and uncontrolled design limit comparison with the pivotal cohort.<sup>[[7]](#ref7)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/implantable-tibial-nerve-stimulation.mdx:94:This pivotal IDE study enrolled 126 subjects implanted with the Medtronic leadless, rechargeable ITNM system. At 6 months, 59% achieved ≥50% improvement in UUI episodes/day (primary endpoint met, p &lt; 0.001 versus performance goal).<sup>[[14]](#ref14)</sup> In a post hoc, multiply imputed 12-month FDA subgroup analysis, 46.9% of 68 participants aged 65 years or older and 76.4% of 58 younger participants met the UUI responder threshold; FDA required age-specific postapproval follow-up.<sup>[[21]](#ref21)</sup> The trial publication reported adverse device effects in 20% and one resolved serious C. difficile event.<sup>[[14]](#ref14)[[21]](#ref21)</sup> FDA counted related events in 25/126 (20%) and also classified an implant wound infection requiring explantation as serious.<sup>[[14]](#ref14)[[21]](#ref21)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/implantable-tibial-nerve-stimulation.mdx:100:A systematic review of separate iTNM and [SNM](/docs/surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/sacral-neuromodulation) studies (20 studies, 1,766 patients) reported pooled UUI responder proportions of 71.3% and 71.8%, respectively. The studied tibial implants generally did not use an SNM-style temporary test phase.<sup>[[15]](#ref15)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/implantable-tibial-nerve-stimulation.mdx:108:- **eCoin:** The original FDA physician labeling contraindicates implantation in specified poor lower-leg surgical candidates (including open wound, local vascular disease/edema and local infection) and patients unable to operate the magnet controller if unwanted stimulation occurs.<sup>[[18]](#ref18)</sup> The implant is MR conditional only under its labeled conditions, including keeping it at least 20 cm outside the MRI bore at 1.5/3 T; the controller magnet is MR unsafe.<sup>[[18]](#ref18)</sup> Diathermy is prohibited. The patient manual also lists prior surgery in the implant area and lower-leg venous or arterial disease as contraindications, gives stimulation as 30-minute sessions every 3 days for 18 weeks and every 4 days afterward, and states battery life of 3 to 5 years at the nominal 6 mA setting.<sup>[[23]](#ref23)</sup> Confirm any later PMA supplement or label revision.<sup>[[18]](#ref18)[[23]](#ref23)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/implantable-tibial-nerve-stimulation.mdx:109:- **Revi:** The manufacturer lists inability to operate the system, men with obstructive BPH or other lower urinary tract obstruction, metallic implants within about 20 cm (8 in) of the implant site, nerve damage affecting treatment, implant-material allergy, high surgical risk or multiple illnesses or active general infections associated with excessive bleeding or delayed or nonhealing wounds, prior surgery or unhealed trauma at the implant site, lower-leg wound-healing risks (open wounds, edema, venous or arterial disease) and pregnancy among contraindications. The implant is MR Conditional at 1.5 T or 3.0 T (scanning at lower field strengths is also permitted) with a maximum spatial gradient of 30 T/m and maximum slew rate of 200 T/m/s, plus SAR and scan-duration limits that depend on the body region scanned. All external components and accessories are MR Unsafe and stay out of the scanner room. Shortwave, microwave and ultrasound diathermy are prohibited anywhere on or near the body because energy transfer can cause severe injury or implant damage. Full current model labeling governs screening and scanning.<sup>[[20]](#ref20)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/implantable-tibial-nerve-stimulation.mdx:114:- **eCoin:** Most adverse events relate to wound healing and infection at the implant site. In the pivotal trial, 16% experienced device-related events through 52 weeks, mostly minor.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)[[4]](#ref4)</sup> The patient manual counts 23 of 133 (17%) with device-related events.<sup>[[23]](#ref23)</sup> The FDA labeling reports device- or procedure-related events in 27 of 133 implanted patients (20%) through 52 weeks; 25 had nonserious and 4 had serious related events, with 2 participants counted in both groups.<sup>[[18]](#ref18)</sup> Three of the four serious related events were infections and one was contact dermatitis, all resulting in explantation. Four additional devices were explanted by 52 weeks: three for MRI needs and one for device failure; one device was revised for dislocation.<sup>[[18]](#ref18)</sup> No serious or unanticipated AEs were reported in the 2-year extension.<sup>[[7]](#ref7)[[18]](#ref18)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/implantable-tibial-nerve-stimulation.mdx:115:- **Revi:** No device- or procedure-related serious adverse events through 3 years of follow-up in the OASIS study.<sup>[[8]](#ref8)</sup> The earlier 36-patient feasibility study reported implant site pain (14%), suspected infection (22%) and procedural wound complications (8%); one system was removed for inflammation.<sup>[[16]](#ref16)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/implantable-tibial-nerve-stimulation.mdx:116:- **Medtronic ITNM:** In TITAN 2, 25/126 (20%) had related adverse events through 12 months; the publication counted one resolved serious event, while FDA also classified an implant wound infection requiring explantation as serious.<sup>[[14]](#ref14)[[21]](#ref21)</sup> FDA reported three device explants before the 12-month visit.<sup>[[14]](#ref14)[[21]](#ref21)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/implantable-tibial-nerve-stimulation.mdx:122:- **Avoids repeated in-office PTNS treatment visits.** At-home therapy delivery<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/implantable-tibial-nerve-stimulation.mdx:123:- **Maintenance burden.** Home-based stimulation avoids office PTNS maintenance visits, a barrier reported in a retrospective PTNS cohort; that cohort did not compare adherence with implants.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/implantable-tibial-nerve-stimulation.mdx:126:- **No SNM-style temporary test phase in the studied implant pathways.** Confirm the individual device's indication and preimplant evaluation; eCoin labeling also allows eligibility after a successful PTNS trial<sup>[[15]](#ref15)[[18]](#ref18)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/implantable-tibial-nerve-stimulation.mdx:132:The AUA/SUFU 2024 guideline lists implantable tibial nerve stimulation among the minimally invasive OAB therapies (Table 3), but its named recommendation for patients with inadequate response to or intolerance of drugs or behavioral therapy covers sacral neuromodulation, percutaneous tibial nerve stimulation and intradetrusor onabotulinumtoxinA rather than implantable devices, because the panel found no direct comparison or placebo-controlled data for the implantable systems (its review cites single-arm studies of 137 patients for eCoin, with 68% achieving a ≥50% UUI reduction at 48 weeks, and 36 patients for Revi).<sup>[[17]](#ref17)</sup> AUA/SUFU Statement 24 permits minimally invasive therapy without prior behavioral, noninvasive or drug trials through shared decision-making <Rec>(Expert Opinion)</Rec>; its named treatment offer in Statement 25 is a Moderate Recommendation (Grade A).<sup>[[17]](#ref17)</sup> The EAU 2026 female LUTS guideline recommends offering percutaneous or transcutaneous posterior tibial nerve stimulation as an option for OAB/UUI <Rec>(Strong)</Rec> but makes no statement on implanted tibial systems.<sup>[[22]](#ref22)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/implantable-tibial-nerve-stimulation.mdx:138:- The pivotal eCoin, Revi and Altaviva efficacy studies cited above were single-arm and open-label.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)[[9]](#ref9)[[14]](#ref14)</sup> Sham-controlled PTNS trials support tibial stimulation as therapy.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup> In a randomized sham-controlled Intibia trial of 208 implanted, predominantly female participants (139 active, 69 sham), more active-treatment participants exceeded the OABq-SF symptom-bother minimally important difference at 3 months (62.8% versus 41.8%, p = 0.0046); this planned secondary quality-of-life report did not present the primary UUI responder comparison.<sup>[[26]](#ref26)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/implantable-tibial-nerve-stimulation.mdx:139:- **No head-to-head randomized trials** comparing iTNM devices to each other, to SNM, or to onabotulinumtoxinA<sup>[[15]](#ref15)</sup>
