2243-Mechanism of action
2244-The Thulium:yttrium-aluminium-garnet laser (Tm:YAG) emits a wavelength of 2010 or 2,013nm in a pulsed or
2245-continuous wave (CW) mode. The laser is primarily used with bare-ended laser fibres [360]. Various applications,
2246:such as vaporesection (ThuVARP), have been published [361].
2247-
2248-Efficacy
2249:Several meta-analyses with pooled data from both RCTs and non-RCTs have evaluated ThuVARP versus M-TURP
2250-[362-364] and B-TURP [365-367]. The largest meta-analyses included nine RCTs and seven non-RCTs and
2251:reported no clinically relevant differences in efficacy (IPSS, QoL and Qmax) between ThuVARP and M-TURP or
2252:B-TURP at 12 months [366]. A multicentre RCT with 410 men reported that ThuVARP and TURP are equivalent
2253-in terms of IPSS but not Qmax, with TURP deemed superior at 12-month follow-up [368]. The beneficial effect of
2254-TURP in terms of Qmax was strengthened in men aged < 70 years and in those diagnosed with LUTS rather than
2255-urinary retention. No differences in individual patient-reported urinary symptoms were seen between arms, with
2256-the exception of some evidence to indicate potential reduction in nocturia in the TURP arm. Data from one RCT
2257:with long-term follow-up showed no difference in efficacy and reoperation rates between ThuVARP and M-TURP
2258:(2.1% vs. 4.1%, respectively) [369]. A prospective multicentre study on ThuVARP including 2,216 patients
2259-showed durable postoperative improvement in IPSS, QoL, Qmax and PVR for the entire eight years of follow-up
2260-[370].
2261-
2262-Tolerability and safety
2263-Two meta-analyses showed longer operation times, shorter catheterisation/hospitalisation times and less
2264-blood loss without significant differences in transfusion rates or in any other short-term complication rates for
2265:ThuVARP compared to TURP [362-367]. A significantly higher transfusion rate was reported after M-TURP in
2266-two meta-analyses [364, 366]. However, overall RCT quality was relatively low with limited follow-up, potentially
2267-accounting for under-reporting of late complications such as urethral stricture/BNC [366]. A multicentre
2268:RCT with 410 men followed up for 12 months reported that ThuVARP and TURP show similar operation,
2269-catheterisation and hospitalisation times between arms with no difference in the frequency or severity of
2270-surgical complications or in blood transfusions rate or haemoglobin change [368, 371]. Patients with urinary
2271-retention had similarly positive outcomes to those with LUTS [368, 371]. Data from three RCTs with mid- to long-
2272:term follow-up (18 to 48 months) showed no differences in late complication rates between ThuVARP and TURP
2273-[369, 372, 373].
2274-
2275-Haemoglobin drop was significantly higher in the bridging group in a retrospectively analysed case series of
2276:103 patients who underwent ThuVARP and received either low molecular weight heparin bridging or continued
2277-antiplatelet/anticoagulant therapy [374].
2278-
2279-Practical considerations
2280:As a limited number of RCTs with mid- to long-term follow-up support the efficacy of ThuVARP, there is a need
2281-for ongoing investigation of the technique.
