93 **ASPIRe (2024)** is the higher-yield contemporary vault-prolapse trial: 376 women were randomized, 360 underwent surgery and 358 contributed to the primary analysis. Three-year modeled composite failure was 28% with sacrocolpopexy versus 43% with vaginal native-tissue repair (adjusted HR 0.57, 99% CI 0.33–0.98; P=.008). Among 134 native-tissue procedures actually performed, including crossovers, 110 (82%) were SSLF and 24 (18%) were uterosacral suspension. The result supports counseling about durability after total-hysterectomy vault prolapse.<sup>[[6]](#ref6)</sup>
94 
95 The smaller SALTO-2 study randomized 64 women and also followed a separate, self-selected cohort of 115. At 12 months, it detected no difference in its primary disease-specific quality-of-life outcome. Apical composite success in the randomized comparison was 89.3% after laparoscopic sacrocolpopexy versus 86.2% after vaginal SSLF.<sup>[[11]](#ref11)</sup>

