## docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/sacral-neuromodulation.mdx
173 
174 ROSETTA randomized women with refractory UUI to SNM or 200 U onabotulinumtoxinA<sup>[[31]](#ref31)</sup>; the US label recommends 100 U for OAB.<sup>[[65]](#ref65)</sup> At 6 months, toxin produced a modestly greater reduction in UUI episodes but more UTI and intermittent catheterization; SNM avoids injection-related retention but carries device revision or removal risk. The planned 24-month extension, in women who had responded initially, reported similar sustained episode improvements (toxin retained higher satisfaction), so the six-month difference is not a durable superiority claim.<sup>[[31]](#ref31)[[38]](#ref38)</sup> The AUA/SUFU guideline cites a 263-patient retrospective OAB cohort: test-phase success was 66.7% after prior botulinum toxin versus 72.9% in toxin-naive patients; among those implanted after a successful test, 43/50 (86%) versus 115/137 (83.9%) were still using SNM to their satisfaction at last follow-up (mean 40.7 versus 54.18 months).<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[63]](#ref63)</sup>
175 
176 **Cost.** ROSETTA's planned economic evaluation (386 women, health-system perspective, 2018 US dollars) found no difference between arms in urgency incontinence episode reduction or QALYs at 2 years, but two-stage SNM cost significantly more than 200 U onabotulinumtoxinA: $35,680 versus $7,460 at 2 years within the trial and a modeled $36,550 versus $12,020 at 5 years (p &lt; 0.01 at both horizons). Modeled willingness-to-pay thresholds put the probability that SNM is cost-effective relative to toxin below 2.5% for any value under $580,000/QALY at 2 years and $204,000/QALY at 5 years, similar clinical effectiveness notwithstanding.<sup>[[48]](#ref48)</sup>

