## docs/01-foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/sachse-urethrotome.mdx
93 
94 The AUA 2023 guideline is explicit: surgeons should offer urethroplasty instead of repeated endoscopic management for recurrent anterior urethral strictures after failed dilation or DVIU <Rec>(Moderate Recommendation, Grade C)</Rec>.<sup>[[7]](#ref7)</sup> For posterior strictures after pelvic fracture, the Cochrane review found that in the first six months men treated with urethrotomy were more likely to require further surgery than those treated with primary urethroplasty (RR 3.39, 95% CI 1.62–7.07; one trial, 50 men); 64% (16 of 25) of the urethrotomy group required continued self-dilation or further surgery at 2 years versus 24% (6 of 25) of the urethroplasty group.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup> The 2026 EAU guideline gives a strong recommendation against more than two DVIU or dilations when urethroplasty is a viable option.<sup>[[8]](#ref8)</sup> In the OPEN randomized trial of 222 men with recurrent bulbar stricture, voiding symptom scores improved similarly over 24 months after cold-knife urethrotomy and urethroplasty, but reintervention was less frequent after urethroplasty (15 versus 29 men; HR 0.52, 95% CI 0.31–0.89).<sup>[[18]](#ref18)</sup>
