8:**Axonics**, now part of Boston Scientific, makes rechargeable and recharge-free sacral neuromodulation (SNM) systems. The [InterStim/SNM hub](/docs/foundations/tools/biomaterials/neuromodulation-devices/medtronic-interstim) covers broader trial comparisons, neurogenic selection and long-term reintervention.
12:The original Axonics r-SNM was the first FDA-approved rechargeable SNM system, designed to deliver therapy for at least 15 years.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup> Its design targeted two limitations of earlier non-rechargeable generators. Battery depletion required replacement surgery, and average battery life was about 4.4 years in the historical device.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)[[6]](#ref6)</sup> Early European experience (RELAX-OAB) preceded the US pivotal study (ARTISAN-SNM), and the FDA approved the system in 2019.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)[[10]](#ref10)</sup><sup>[[21]](#ref21)</sup> Boston Scientific acquired Axonics in 2024. The current F15 and R20 are successors to that first rechargeable generator.
19:| **R20** | Rechargeable, model 5101; 5 cc | Designed for 20+ years<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>. The Canadian implant manual lists recharge intervals of 10, 8, 6 and 5 months at 0.1, 1, 1.5 and 2 mA (14 Hz, 210 µs, continuous, 1200 Ω)<sup>[[19]](#ref19)</sup> |
21:R20 is not recharge-free. Older R15/ARTISAN charging schedules do not apply to R20. Neither projected battery life nor a small generator guarantees freedom from revision. Both current systems use constant-current stimulation. The generator, lead and accessories are selected together.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
23:The rationale for a long-lived generator is that a nonrechargeable device requires replacement surgery when the battery is depleted. A rechargeable device is expected to reduce replacement surgery, but revisions unrelated to the battery still occur, so a rechargeable system does not eliminate ongoing maintenance.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)[[11]](#ref11)</sup>
31:The patient needs access to the remote and follow-up support, and rechargeable models also require a sustainable charging plan. Neurological-origin urinary dysfunction, pregnancy and pediatric use carry labeling limitations. Device selection accounts for dexterity, cognition, caregiver support, anticipated MRI and the patient's preferences.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
58:MRI (Canadian patient guidelines): the R15 (model 1101), F15 and R20 generators with tined lead 1201 or 2201 are MR Conditional. The clinician programmer, remote control, charger and external trial equipment are MR Unsafe. With a whole-body transmit coil, the remote-control readiness check must pass and the battery-status light must be green for R15, F15 and R20. If the light is orange on rechargeable R15 or R20, charge the generator before scanning. With detachable head or extremity transmit/receive coils, stimulation is simply switched off. Patients need review first if the generator sits outside the posterior hip or upper buttock, or if they have a broken or disconnected lead or irregular stimulation.<sup>[[20]](#ref20)</sup>
62:ARTISAN was a single-arm prospective study of 129 people with UUI, 98% women, using an earlier rechargeable Axonics system.<sup>[[6]](#ref6)</sup> All participants received the lead and generator in a single nonstaged procedure without a test phase.<sup>[[6]](#ref6)[[7]](#ref7)</sup> Nine had previously passed an external trial without a full implant; excluding them did not change the responder rates.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup>
67:| One year | 89% across 129 participants | UUI episodes per day 5.6 to 1.4; all participants able to recharge; 96% found recharging acceptable <sup>[[7]](#ref7)</sup>|
68:| Two years | 93% of 121 completers, versus 88% of all 129 when missing or exited participants counted as failures | UUI episodes per day 5.6 to 1.0; 94% found recharging acceptable; no unanticipated or serious device-related adverse events <sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup>|
80:In a prospective series of 15 patients with fecal incontinence implanted in a single stage with the Axonics system, 13 (87%) were test responders at four weeks. Median weekly incontinence episodes fell from 8 to 1.5 at four weeks and 1.5 at six months. Of the 13 four-week responders, 12 were therapy responders at six months. Mean recharge time was 37 minutes once or twice a week, and all patients were moderately or very satisfied. There were no unanticipated device- or procedure-related adverse events.<sup>[[13]](#ref13)</sup>
84:One retrospective single-center comparison of 246 patients found revisions in 34% of 150 rechargeable recipients versus 13.5% of 96 nonrechargeable recipients (p &lt; 0.001). Difficulty charging accounted for 35.3% of rechargeable revisions.<sup>[[11]](#ref11)</sup>
86:Trial acceptability of charging (94% to 98% across the Axonics studies) contrasts with a single-center observational cohort of 38 patients who received a rechargeable InterStim Micro for fecal incontinence, 28 (74%) as replacements for nonrechargeable devices.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)[[7]](#ref7)[[10]](#ref10)[[14]](#ref14)</sup> At 24 months or more, 20 of 37 interviewed patients (54%) reported device regret, 75% connectivity problems and 96% device-management difficulty.<sup>[[14]](#ref14)</sup>Device choice should connect expected treatment benefit with the patient's ability to manage the specific system.
70:| Failure components | Anatomic recurrence was the most common component, often partial apical descent without prolapse beyond the hymen or symptoms | — | — |

