docs/03-clinical-conditions/03a-storage-incontinence/sui-female.mdx:170:| **Single-incision mini-sling** | Anchored midurethral | Shorter procedure; long-term data still maturing. EAU notes non-inferior patient-reported success for Ajust and Altis at 36 months but more mesh exposure, repeat SUI surgery and dyspareunia than conventional MUS.<sup>[[15]](#ref15)</sup> NICE NG123 limits single-incision short mesh sling insertion to clinical trials.<sup>[[14]](#ref14)</sup> |
docs/03-clinical-conditions/03a-storage-incontinence/sui-female.mdx:255:- [Single-Incision Mini-Sling](/docs/surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/single-incision-mini-sling)
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/female-slings-suspensions.mdx:20:Multiple professional societies, including AUA/SUFU, ACOG, AUGS, and EUGA, continue to support MUS as an appropriate primary surgical option for index patients with SUI after counseling about alternatives, mesh-specific risks, and the different risk profiles of retropubic, transobturator, and single-incision routes.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)[[4]](#ref4)[[5]](#ref5)[[6]](#ref6)</sup> A 2018 position statement from the International Urogynecological Community, reaffirmed in September 2025, and a 2014 statement from RANZCOG and the Urogynaecological Society of Australasia (UGSA) also support monofilament polypropylene MUS for SUI and distinguish it from vaginal mesh for prolapse. The international statement describes full-length retropubic and transobturator slings as the current "gold standard" for SUI surgery and is an ungraded position statement. RANZCOG C-Gyn 32 gives an evidence-based Grade A recommendation for MUS in routine SUI, but limits transobturator slings to exceptional circumstances after multidisciplinary or peer review and single-incision slings to trials.<sup>[[34]](#ref34)[[35]](#ref35)</sup> The earlier joint AUGS/SUFU position statement on mesh midurethral slings makes the same distinction. It calls polypropylene MUS the standard of care and cites up to 17 years of TVT follow-up, but its evidence and its FDA discussion end at 2013, so it predates later data and current regulatory context.<sup>[[36]](#ref36)</sup> It is likewise an advocacy statement, not a graded guideline.<sup>[[36]](#ref36)</sup> The 2021 AUGS/SUFU joint position statement, which AUGS reaffirmed in 2026, describes full-length polypropylene MUS as a standard of care for the surgical treatment of SUI and discusses the FDA's 2019 communication on surgical mesh for SUI; it does not give graded recommendations.<sup>[[43]](#ref43)[[44]](#ref44)</sup> NICE NG123 is more restrictive: it offers a retropubic mesh sling alongside colposuspension and an autologous rectus fascial sling, advises the transobturator route only when the retropubic approach should be avoided, and limits single-incision and top-down retropubic slings to clinical trials.<sup>[[31]](#ref31)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/female-slings-suspensions.mdx:30:| [Single-Incision Mini-Sling](/docs/surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/single-incision-mini-sling) | SIMS / SIS device selection, 3-year evidence, durability counseling |
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/female-slings-suspensions.mdx:69:| Need to minimize dissection, skin incisions, and early postoperative pain | SIMS | Contemporary SIMS trials show shorter operative time and less early pain, with noninferior patient-reported success through 3 years<sup>[[12]](#ref12)[[17]](#ref17)</sup> |
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/female-slings-suspensions.mdx:159:The UK SIMS randomized trial randomized 600 women (596 included after four post-randomization exclusions) across 21 hospitals and found the pooled adjustable anchored mini-sling group (primarily Ajust and Altis) noninferior to standard MUS for patient-reported success at 15 and 36 months.<sup>[[12]](#ref12)[[19]](#ref19)[[41]](#ref41)</sup> A 2025 meta-analysis of 24 RCTs (3,687 women; mean follow-up 13.4 months) found no statistically significant difference in patient-reported cure between SIMS and standard MUS.<sup>[[17]](#ref17)</sup> It reported shorter operative time and stay, lower day-1 pain, and less organ injury and groin pain with SIMS.<sup>[[17]](#ref17)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/female-slings-suspensions.mdx:196:| Berger et al. | 17,030 patients | Overall reoperation 6.0% at 9 years; mesh revision / removal 1.1% at 9 years; recurrent-SUI reoperation more common after single-incision slings than RMUS<sup>[[22]](#ref22)</sup> |
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/female-slings-suspensions.mdx:238:For women with objectively confirmed SUI undergoing native-tissue vaginal prolapse repair, a 2024 randomized trial found an Altis single-incision sling noninferior to a retropubic sling for a 12-month composite of bothersome SUI, SUI retreatment, or treatment for retention (29 [25%] versus 24 [20%] among evaluable patients).<sup>[[45]](#ref45)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/female-slings-suspensions.mdx:380:<a id="ref45"></a>45. Matthews CA, Rardin CR, Sokol A, et al. "A randomized trial of retropubic vs single-incision sling among patients undergoing vaginal prolapse repair." *Am J Obstet Gynecol.* 2024;231(2):261.e1-261.e10. doi:[10.1016/j.ajog.2024.04.036](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ajog.2024.04.036)
docs/01-foundations/tools/instruments/urethral-specialty/midurethral-sling-trocars.mdx:8:Device-specific passers used to place a midurethral sling through a retropubic (TVT) or transobturator (TOT) route, or to deploy a single-incision sling. These are different implant-and-passer systems, not one interchangeable tape that can be routed with any trocar. "Tunneller" appears in the early Petros / Ulmsten literature; this page uses **trocar** for the relevant passers while distinguishing the [Raz-Pereyra](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/urethral-specialty/raz-pereyra), [Stamey](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/urethral-specialty/stamey-needle), and [suprapubic catheter](/docs/foundations/tools/biomaterials/urinary-catheters/suprapubic-catheter) devices.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[2]](#ref2)[[14]](#ref14)</sup>
docs/01-foundations/tools/instruments/urethral-specialty/midurethral-sling-trocars.mdx:20:| **Single-incision mini-sling inserter** | Single-incision | Device-specific anchor deployment | Vaginal approach | Inserter and fixation vary by product; follow its IFU. In the SIMS RCT (596 women), mini-slings were noninferior to standard MUS for patient-reported success at 15 months (79.1% versus 75.6%; 72.0% versus 66.8% at 36 months); dyspareunia was reported by 11.7% versus 4.8% of 290 questionnaire respondents, and tape or mesh exposure occurred in 3.3% versus 1.9% over 36 months.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup> |
docs/01-foundations/tools/instruments/urethral-specialty/midurethral-sling-trocars.mdx:73:- **Guideline context.** AUA/SUFU 2023: in index patients who choose a midurethral sling, clinicians may offer a retropubic, transobturator or single-incision sling <Rec>(Conditional Recommendation; Evidence Level Grade A for retropubic and transobturator, Grade B for single-incision)</Rec>.<sup>[[13]](#ref13)</sup> EAU 2026: perform cystoscopy when inserting a synthetic MUS, and inform women that long-term outcomes are superior with the retropubic route (both strong). NICE: do not offer a transobturator approach unless specific clinical circumstances make the retropubic approach unsuitable.<sup>[[13]](#ref13)[[15]](#ref15)[[16]](#ref16)</sup>
docs/01-foundations/tools/instruments/urethral-specialty/midurethral-sling-trocars.mdx:79:- **Regulatory and guideline differences** require country-specific consent and device checks. In particular, NICE's transobturator and single-incision restrictions differ from US route options; vaginal prolapse mesh policy must not be silently substituted for SUI sling policy.<sup>[[13]](#ref13)[[15]](#ref15)[[16]](#ref16)</sup>
docs/01-foundations/tools/instruments/urethral-specialty/midurethral-sling-trocars.mdx:100:<a id="ref5"></a>5. Abdel-Fattah M, Cooper D, Davidson T, et al. "Single-incision mini-slings for stress urinary incontinence in women." *N Engl J Med.* 2022;386(13):1230–43. doi:[10.1056/NEJMoa2111815](https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2111815)
docs/01-foundations/tools/instruments/urethral-specialty/midurethral-sling-trocars.mdx:122:<a id="ref16"></a>16. National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. [Urinary incontinence and pelvic organ prolapse in women: management (NG123), Recommendations](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng123/chapter/recommendations), recommendations 1.5.10 (transobturator approach) and 1.5.11 (top-down retropubic and single-incision slings only within a clinical trial); accessed September 2026.
## Differences on existing WARWIKI pages

| Page and wording | Source finding |
|---|---|
| `docs/01-foundations/tools/instruments/urethral-specialty/midurethral-sling-trocars.mdx`: “In the SIMS RCT (596 women)” | **600 were randomized**; **596 remained after exclusions**. The unqualified parenthetical can be read as the randomized sample (NEJM p 1233, Figure 1). Its quoted efficacy and harm values agree with the source. |
| `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/female-slings-suspensions.mdx`: “Contemporary SIMS trials show shorter operative time” in a row citing SIMS | In SIMS itself, mean time was **39.2 vs 41.3 minutes**, adjusted difference **−2.2 minutes** (95% CI −5.9 to 1.6); this trial did not establish a shorter operative time (NEJM p 1235, Table 2; HTA scientific summary p vi). The row also cites a separate meta-analysis, which may support a pooled claim. |
| `docs/03-clinical-conditions/03a-storage-incontinence/sui-female.mdx`: “Shorter procedure” for single-incision mini-slings | The direct SIMS comparison showed **39.2 vs 41.3 minutes**, adjusted difference **−2.2 minutes** (95% CI −5.9 to 1.6), so a shorter procedure was not established by this trial (NEJM p 1235, Table 2). |

The remaining trial-specific claims in `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/single-incision-mini-sling.mdx`, `docs/01-foundations/tools/biomaterials/synthetic-meshes/polypropylene.mdx`, and the cited passages of `female-slings-suspensions.mdx` match the checked report values. The existing statement that 15- and 36-month outcomes met the 10-point margin is supported by the HTA scientific summary p v.

## Unverified or bounded points

- Direct publisher/NIHR PDF downloads were blocked by the web interface; the primary publisher article and selected full HTA sections were read through indexed text, while the HTA scientific summary was opened as a PDF. The full 190-page HTA volume was not reviewed page by page.
- The adverse-event differences are descriptive secondary findings. The trial was not powered to exclude meaningful differences in uncommon mesh complications.
- No FDA approval or clearance claim is made. NICE's older restriction is deliberately excluded from this trial-linked guideline section.

