104:- Hematuria, the most frequent (40%, 39/98 in the PINNACLE active arm regardless of relatedness), usually self-limited<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup>
105:- Urinary tract infection (14%, 14/98 in the PINNACLE active arm regardless of relatedness)<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup>
106:- Dysuria (9.2%, 9/98 in the PINNACLE active arm regardless of relatedness)<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup>
107:- In the PINNACLE active arm (regardless of relatedness), urge or mixed incontinence (8.2%, 8/98), mild stress incontinence (7.1%, 7/98) and bladder spasms (6.1%, 6/98)<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup>
110:In PINNACLE these events were typically mild to moderate and resolved within 1 month.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup> In the label's as-treated analysis, treatment-related events included hematuria (39.8%; 41 events in 39 men, median 34 days to resolution, fewer after the traction protocol), urinary tract infection (11.2%), dysuria (8.2%), mild stress incontinence (7.1%), bladder spasm (6.1%) and urethral stricture (4.1%). Six men (6.1%) had treatment-related serious events (5 device-related, 1 procedure-related), most often post-procedural hematuria (4 events), and one grade 3 bladder perforation is listed. No participant met the composite of major device-related complications, and there were no grade 4 or 5 related events.<sup>[[23]](#ref23)</sup> The PINNACLE report describes the five device-related serious events as four postprocedural hematuria events needing cystoscopic management or extended observation and one urethral false passage.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup> Complication rates in EVEREST-I were significantly affected by device diameter.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup> Systemic paclitaxel exposure was minimal in the PINNACLE pharmacokinetic subset (mean maximum plasma concentration 0.4 ng/mL).<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup>
116:EVEREST-I, PINNACLE (1 and 2 years) and SUMMIT reported preserved group-level sexual-function questionnaire results.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)[[5]](#ref5)[[6]](#ref6)[[8]](#ref8)</sup> In PINNACLE, ejaculatory dysfunction was reported as an adverse event in 4/98 treated men and 1/48 sham participants; no treatment-related de novo erectile dysfunction was reported.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup> Narrative reviews place it with UroLift and iTIND among the MISTs with the best sexual-function profiles, in contrast to TURP (retrograde ejaculation 60–75%, ED approximately 14%) and Aquablation (retrograde ejaculation 7–10%).<sup>[[3]](#ref3)[[9]](#ref9)</sup>
134:In PINNACLE, Qmax improved by +10.3 mL/s at 1 year.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup> The JAMA review reports +5.6 mL/s for Rezūm at 12 months, +4.3 mL/s for UroLift and +6.1 mL/s for PAE at 1 month, and +10.6 mL/s for TURP and +10.3 mL/s for Aquablation at 12 months<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup>; EVEREST-I reported +5.6 mL/s at 4 years<sup>[[22]](#ref22)</sup>. These cross-trial comparisons use different populations and, for UroLift and PAE, different time points.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)[[9]](#ref9)[[10]](#ref10)[[22]](#ref22)</sup> The Cornu network meta-analysis, which did not include Optilume BPH or Aquablation, found that TURP gave the largest flow-rate improvement, while symptom scores and quality of life with PAE, PUL and WVTT were similar to TURP.<sup>[[10]](#ref10)</sup>
142:| Optilume BPH | Surgical reintervention: 3/100 (3%) by 2 yr in the PINNACLE treatment arm; 1/80 (1.3%) by 4 yr in single-arm EVEREST-I, conservatively counting a prostatectomy for refractory hematuria. PINNACLE's separate composite symptomatic responder rate was 56/83 (67.5%) at 2 yr.<sup>[[8]](#ref8)[[22]](#ref22)</sup> | 2 yr (postrandomization treatment-arm follow-up), 4 yr (single-arm; 59/80 evaluable for symptom response) |
158:- PINNACLE reported a 1-year Qmax improvement of +10.3 mL/s after Optilume BPH.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup>
