 | **Refractory OAB** | FUTURE does not support testing everyone before invasive treatment; retain testing for a specific decision or diagnostic uncertainty.<sup>[[6]](#ref6)[[28]](#ref28)</sup> |
-| **Male LUTS** | Use selectively when the result can change management, rather than routinely before all outlet surgery. EAU 2026 weakly recommends urodynamics before invasive treatment only for specific indications: prior unsuccessful invasive treatment, inability to void more than 150 mL, bothersome predominantly voiding LUTS with Qmax above 10 mL/s, and predominantly voiding LUTS at age over 80 or under 50. With Qmax below 10 mL/s EAU considers obstruction likely and urodynamics not necessarily needed, though the cutoff is a weak discriminator on its own: among men with paired uroflowmetry and pressure-flow data in the ICS-'BPH' study (1,271 recruited), a free-flow Qmax below 10 mL/s had 70% specificity, a 70% positive predictive value and only 47% sensitivity for a pressure-flow diagnosis of bladder outlet obstruction. In exploratory UPSTREAM analyses cited by EAU, surgery helped more in men with IPSS above 16, age under 74, Qmax below 9.8 mL/s, BOOI above 47.6 and BCI above 123.<sup>[[26]](#ref26)[[46]](#ref46)</sup> |
+| **Male LUTS** | Use selectively when the result can change management, rather than routinely before all outlet surgery. EAU 2026 weakly recommends urodynamics before invasive treatment only for specific indications: prior unsuccessful invasive treatment, inability to void more than 150 mL, bothersome predominantly voiding LUTS with Qmax above 10 mL/s, and predominantly voiding LUTS at age over 80 or under 50. With Qmax below 10 mL/s EAU considers obstruction likely and urodynamics not necessarily needed, though the cutoff is a weak discriminator on its own: among men with paired uroflowmetry and pressure-flow data in the ICS-'BPH' study (1,271 recruited), a free-flow Qmax below 10 mL/s had 70% specificity, a 70% positive predictive value and only 47% sensitivity for a pressure-flow diagnosis of bladder outlet obstruction. In exploratory UPSTREAM analyses cited by EAU, surgery helped more in men with IPSS above 16, age under 74, Qmax below 9.8 mL/s, BOOI above 47.6 and BCI above 123.<sup>[[26]](#ref26)[[73]](#ref73)</sup> |
