## Disagreements with WARWIKI pages

`docs/04-surgical-techniques/04m-bph-male-luts/urolift-pul.mdx`:

1. "12-month paired results: IPSS from 21.8 to 11.1 (n = 123); Qmax +4.0 mL/s (n = 103)." The 3-year report, Table 3a (PDF p6, journal p7777), gives per-protocol IPSS 22.13 to 11.52 (n = 123) at 12 months and Qmax +4.03 (n = 102). The 11.1 is the intention-to-treat lift-arm value in the primary abstract (22.1 to 11.1, n = 140); 21.8 is the baseline of the 24-month paired set (21.82, n = 103).
2. "Mean procedure time in L.I.F.T. was 66.2 ± 23.8 minutes." The 3-year report, Table 2 (PDF p5, journal p7776), gives 66.16 ± 24.09 (per-protocol set, n = 137). The 23.8 could not be found; it may come from the 2013 full text (full lift set).
3. "40/140 treated men received a catheter by site protocol; 68/100 of the others avoided catheterization after a voiding trial." Neither the 3-year nor the 5-year report contains these counts. Both state that 32% required catheterization for a failed voiding trial (mean catheter duration 0.9 days over the whole cohort; 5-year PDF p4), which fits 32/100 but not 40/140. Unverified without the 2013 full text.
4. Table row "3 mo ... Qmax +4.4 mL/s" is the lift-arm figure from the abstract; the randomized comparison reports 4.28 (lift) versus 1.98 mL/s (sham). Not an error, but the sham comparison is absent.
5. "an 88% greater improvement" matches (5-year PDF p4, intention to treat).

`docs/04-surgical-techniques/04m-bph-male-luts/aquablation.mdx` (comparison table, UroLift row, citing the 2013 report as ref 30): "−11.0" and "+4.0 mL/s". The abstract's 3-month AUASI is a score of 11.0 (change −11.1); +4.0 mL/s is the 12-month Qmax change (3-month +4.4 in the abstract, 4.28 in the randomized comparison), and the row gives no timepoint.

`docs/04-surgical-techniques/04m-bph-male-luts/optilume-bph.mdx` (comparison table, UroLift row): "−11.0 at 1 mo; +4.3 at 1 mo" is cited to a JAMA review, not to L.I.F.T. L.I.F.T. 1-month intention-to-treat IPSS change was −9.89 (5-year PDF p6, Table 2a), and Qmax was not measured at 1 month in the tables read.

`docs/04-surgical-techniques/04m-bph-male-luts/itind.mdx` (UroLift row "−10.6 (paired); +4.0 (paired)", cited to Elterman 2021): identical to the L.I.F.T. 12-month per-protocol values (−10.61; +4.03), so confirm that Elterman reports those numbers rather than L.I.F.T.

Consistent with the sources: 206 men randomized 2:1; 13.6% retreatment at 5 years (19/140) and 10.7% at 3 years; the 24-, 36- and 60-month table values; 36% IPSS, 50% quality-of-life and 44% Qmax improvement at 5 years; removal of 10 encrusted and 3 prophylactic implants; 8.6 days to preoperative activity; no de novo sustained erectile or ejaculatory dysfunction.
- Whybrow 2017 (Trials 18:140), recruitment and equipoise qualitative study: not opened; not needed for results.
- Time trade-off utilities (Pharmacoecon Open 2019, PMC6861395): not opened; methods paper for cost-effectiveness.
- Eur Urol correspondence (Bansal et al. and the authors' reply, 2021; PMIDs 33143950, 33218824, 33293080): not opened.
- No long-term follow-up beyond the 24-month symptom outcome and the 24-48 month reintervention follow-up exists as a separate report.
- The HTA monograph is included at abstract level only; its detailed tables could not be read, so nothing beyond the abstract is quoted from it.

## Disagreements and inconsistencies in the sources (not WARWIKI)

- Primary report, internal: the Results text says the 12-24 month flow-rate comparison used 44 versus 63 participants; Table 2 gives 18/93 (19%) versus 13/104 (13%). The file follows Table 2 and notes the text.
- Primary report, internal: the Discussion gives serious adverse events of 10.9% versus 11.3%; Table 4 gives 7/82 (8.5%) versus 10/115 (8.7%). The file and page use Table 4 (the EAU guideline also gives 8.5% and 8.7%).
- Primary report, internal: Results says 1,262 men were identified; supplementary Table 1 totals 1,249 (page uses 1,262, the text figure).
- Primary report, internal: the percentages in the text (63% and 81%) do not match 69/108 and 90/112 (63.9% and 80.4%); they match the monograph's denominators 109 and 113 (63%, 80%). Counts are used instead of percentages.
- Primary report, internal: the sexual-function scale is 1 (very dissatisfied) to 5 (very satisfied) in Methods but reversed in the Table 1 footnote. The file records the direction conflict and the page states only that the groups did not differ.
- HTA monograph abstract: 95% CI for the primary difference is -1.78 to 1.02; the Eur Urol report prints -1.74 to 1.02. Both files keep their own source's value.
- HTA abstract: 109 and 113 allocated; the primary report's tables use 108 and 112 after two post-randomization ineligibility exclusions.
- The primary report abstract (accepted manuscript) says "ICIQ voiding symptom score"; the published PubMed abstract and Methods say the voiding symptom score from the stricture-surgery PROM (Jackson 2011). The page uses the latter.
- AUA guideline discussion (2023): describes OPEN as enrolling men with stricture "<2 cm in length"; the primary report has no length criterion (mean length 2.0 and 1.7 cm, recorded in 130 of 220 men). The same text states recurrence as "39% experienced a recurrence versus 19%", but the paper reports 39 and 19 men (39/112 = 34.8%; 19/108 = 17.6%). The page does not repeat either point.
- EAU 2026 text gives reintervention as 13.8% versus 25.9%; the counts (15/108, 29/112) give 13.9% and 25.9%.
The page's design facts that depend on the registry (September 2008 start, June 2009 primary completion, phase 4, double masking of participant and outcomes assessor) should be checked against the paper.

## Reports included

1. `sumit-2010` primary (abstract + registry).
2. `sumit-2011-ics` conference abstract (secondary analyses; `noDoi`).
3. `sumit-2012-step-24mo` (Neurourol Urodyn 2013;32(1):24-29, online 2012-06-05).
4. `sumit-2013-step-36mo` (J Urol 2013;189(6):2194-2201, online 2012-12-03; Crossref print date June 2013).

## Considered and left out

- OrBIT (Peters 2009, J Urol 182:1055): separate trial with its own file; used only as a relation (`extends`).
- MacDiarmid 2010 (J Urol 183:234-240, 12-month PTNS durability in 33 responders): a different cohort, not opened.

