### Drug-Coated Balloons

Optilume’s FDA indication covers adult men with symptomatic anterior strictures ≤3 cm.<sup>[[27]](#ref27)</sup> Guideline selection is narrower: AUA 2023 allows dilation or DVIU with a DCB for recurrent bulbar strictures &lt;3 cm <Rec>(conditional, Grade B)</Rec>,<sup>[[30]](#ref30)</sup> and EAU 2026 strongly recommends DCB for recurrent bulbar strictures &lt;3 cm after at least two endoscopic treatments when urethroplasty is unsuitable or declined.<sup>[[28]](#ref28)</sup> ROBUST III compared DCB with standard endoscopy, not urethroplasty. In the initially randomized DCB arm (n = 79), three-year Kaplan–Meier freedom from reintervention was 71.9% with an estimate potentially inflated by censoring participants who had treatment failure without a repeat procedure; among 56 participants with three-year IPSS data, 27 (48%) had at least 30% symptom-score improvement without retreatment, and the control arm had no follow-up beyond one year.<sup>[[24]](#ref24)[[27]](#ref27)[[28]](#ref28)[[29]](#ref29)[[46]](#ref46)</sup>

The label requires abstinence or condom use for 30 days, plus highly effective contraception and avoiding fathering a child for at least six months when the partner could become pregnant. Treat active UTI first; paclitaxel hypersensitivity and urologic implants such as an AUS or penile implant are contraindications. Repeated treatment of the same stricture is insufficiently studied.<sup>[[27]](#ref27)</sup> Paclitaxel was detectable in semen for up to six months in ROBUST III, which is the basis for the contraception advice; EAU considers evidence outside the ROBUST indications (penile, membranous, bladder-neck, VUAS, longer or first-time strictures) too limited for recommendations.<sup>[[28]](#ref28)</sup> See the [DCB technique and counseling page](/docs/surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/drug-coated-balloon-pointer).
