docs/04-surgical-techniques/04j-sexual-dysfunction/penile-traction-therapy.mdx:45:| **Joseph 2020 RestoreX open-label and follow-up phases**<sup>[[10]](#ref10)</sup> | Open-label use after the randomized phase (mean use 31 min/day; n = 64 at 6 mo) | **Among 37 original traction-arm men using RestoreX ≥15 min/day for 6 mo, 95% had length gains and 61% had curvature improvements**; no controlled comparison after 3 months |
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04j-sexual-dysfunction/penile-traction-therapy.mdx:95:In a 2017 prospective database of 51 men completing CCH, 69% used the Andropenis device (mean 9.8 h/week) and curvature improved a mean of 20.9° overall.<sup>[[21]](#ref21)</sup> Curvature improvement did not differ by frequency or duration of PTT, and stretched length changed by +0.4 cm with PTT and −0.35 cm without (p = 0.21).<sup>[[21]](#ref21)</sup> Whether the difference from the RestoreX registry reflects device design, dose or patient selection is not established. A 2024 meta-analysis of seven comparative studies (532 men with curvature data) found, in four length-reporting studies (346 men), 0.81 cm more length gain with heterogeneous CCH combinations than with CCH alone (95% CI 0.17–1.45), without a significant curvature difference; the pooled estimate cannot isolate RestoreX's effect.<sup>[[30]](#ref30)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04j-sexual-dysfunction/penile-traction-therapy.mdx:116:- **Treatment course (RestoreX in Peyronie's disease):** After 3 months of randomized treatment and 3 months of open-label use, men who used PTT throughout gained an additional 0.6–0.8 cm of length during months 3–6.<sup>[[10]](#ref10)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04j-sexual-dysfunction/penile-traction-therapy.mdx:147:Guideline endorsement of PTT in Peyronie's disease varies by society, and the Cochrane review rates the certainty of the traction evidence as very low.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup> The 2015 AUA guideline makes no recommendation for or against traction: three observational studies of 4.5–5.0 hours a day reported curvature improvement, and the panel classed traction among unproven "Other Treatments" needing replication in adequately powered randomized designs.<sup>[[24]](#ref24)</sup> The International Consultation on Sexual Medicine report (2015 meeting, published 2016) concluded that PTT is "a viable therapy to modestly improve penile length" as primary therapy, before prosthesis placement in men with decreased length, or after surgery for Peyronie's disease. It may have a role in the acute phase of Peyronie's disease but has inconsistent outcomes in the long-term phase.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup> The Fifth ICSM Peyronie's consensus, published in 2026, conditionally recommends penile extenders or vacuum devices to reduce deformity, alone or in multimodal care (moderate-quality evidence).<sup>[[31]](#ref31)</sup> For cosmetic lengthening, systematic-review evidence is of low quality and does not support graded recommendations.<sup>[[17]](#ref17)</sup> The EAU 2026 limited update offers traction and vacuum devices to reduce deformity or within multimodal therapy for Peyronie's disease <Rec>(weak)</Rec>, while noting limited data: a meta-analysis of five randomized and nonrandomized trials of penile traction therapy found about 15° of curvature improvement; traction devices were worn two to eight hours daily, with no serious adverse effects reported.<sup>[[25]](#ref25)</sup> As an adjunct to surgery or injection therapy, traction was associated with about 1 cm more stretched length without a significant curvature difference.<sup>[[25]](#ref25)</sup> In a separate meta-analysis, adding traction or vacuum to CCH showed no statistically certain extra benefit for curvature (mean additional decrease 0.3°, 95% CI −3.97° to 4.49°) or length (+0.5 cm, 95% CI −0.32 to 1.4 cm).<sup>[[28]](#ref28)</sup> For short penile size it considers traction as conservative treatment to increase length (weak; level 3).<sup>[[25]](#ref25)</sup>
docs/03-clinical-conditions/03g-genital-scrotal/peyronies-disease.mdx:23:- Strong association with Dupuytren's contracture<sup>[[11]](#ref11)[[12]](#ref12)</sup>: 26% (95% CI 17–35%) of men presenting for Dupuytren contracture reported PD-like symptoms in a survey of 85 respondents<sup>[[54]](#ref54)</sup>, and a genetic-association study found the WNT2 locus on chromosome 7, already linked to Dupuytren's disease, was also associated with PD, supporting a shared genetic basis.<sup>[[55]](#ref55)</sup>
docs/03-clinical-conditions/03g-genital-scrotal/peyronies-disease.mdx:189:  - **6-month open-label, as-treated subgroup (n = 37 using RestoreX ≥15 min/day):** 95% had length gains (mean 2.0–2.2 cm); 61% had curve improvements (16.8–21.4°)<sup>[[33]](#ref33)</sup>
docs/03-clinical-conditions/03g-genital-scrotal/peyronies-disease.mdx:190:  - **Combined with CCH (nonrandomized single-center registry; 113 of 287 men with traction-use data completed therapy with objective outcomes):** RestoreX plus CCH was associated with 49% mean curvature improvement versus 31% with CCH alone; the RestoreX group was 6.9× more likely than the other groups to achieve ≥20° improvement<sup>[[34]](#ref34)</sup>. Across heterogeneous CCH combination regimens in seven comparative studies (532 men), a 2024 meta-analysis found greater length gain than with CCH alone (mean difference 0.81 cm, 95% CI 0.17–1.45), but no significant additional curvature benefit.<sup>[[65]](#ref65)</sup>

