 ### Keep mesh evidence separate
 
-SUPeR tested polypropylene Uphold LITE mesh sacrospinous hysteropexy against vaginal hysterectomy with bilateral USLS in postmenopausal women with prolapse beyond the hymen. The report tabulated cumulative first-failure proportions of 29/79 (37%) versus 42/78 (54%) at the five-year visit; these are not reoperation rates. The interval-censored primary analysis favored mesh hysteropexy (adjusted hazard ratio 0.58, 95% CI 0.36–0.94), but proportional hazards were not met, so that ratio summarizes a changing effect over follow-up. The specific mesh product is no longer marketed in the United States for transvaginal POP repair.<sup>[[11]](#ref11)</sup>
+SUPeR tested polypropylene Uphold LITE mesh sacrospinous hysteropexy against vaginal hysterectomy with bilateral USLS in postmenopausal women with prolapse beyond the hymen. The report tabulated cumulative first-failure proportions of 29/79 (37%) versus 42/78 (54%) at the five-year visit; these are not reoperation rates. The interval-censored five-year analysis, a secondary objective of the trial, favored mesh hysteropexy (adjusted hazard ratio 0.58, 95% CI 0.36–0.94), but proportional hazards were not met, so that ratio summarizes a changing effect over follow-up. The specific mesh product is no longer marketed in the United States for transvaginal POP repair.<sup>[[11]](#ref11)</sup>
 
 In the 2026 ten-year SUPeR extension, composite failures accumulated in 35/88 women (40%) assigned to mesh hysteropexy and 46/87 (53%) assigned to hysterectomy plus USLS; only 112/175 analyzed participants entered the years 6–10 extension. These are composite failures, not apical reoperations. The adjusted time-to-failure hazard ratio was 0.64 (95% CI 0.41–1.00; p = 0.05), and the expected additional mesh-durability gain during years 6–10 was not observed. No between-group difference in patient-reported outcomes or clinically important complications was demonstrated.<sup>[[21]](#ref21)</sup>
 
