reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/nitti-oab/notes.md:44:- `docs/01-foundations/pharmacology/neuromodulation-adjuncts/botulinum-toxin.mdx` (section "Pivotal phase 3 — Nitti 2013"): "Significant improvements across urgency, frequency, nocturia, and QoL (I-QOL, KHQ)". The abstract says all other OAB symptoms improved (P <= 0.05) and quality of life improved across multiple measures (P < 0.001); it names no instruments, and nocturia is not named. Also "Most common AEs: UTI and urinary retention": in the registry's cycle 1 list, dysuria (14.4%) and bacteriuria (8.3%) were more frequent than retention (5.8%) after UTI (24.5%).
