reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/pessri/notes.md:19:- `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04g-prolapse-repair/obliterative-pessary/prolapse-pessaries.mdx`, line 49: “134 women ... used a ring with support and a Gellhorn pessary for 3 months each” can imply that all 134 completed both periods. The primary abstract says 134 enrolled, but three-month data were collected for only 94 ring periods and 99 Gellhorn periods (PubMed abstract, Study design and Results). The rest of that paragraph—the median POP-Q stage III, 94/99 available period counts, improvement in PFDI/PFIQ, and no clinically significant difference—agrees with the abstract.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/pessri/notes.md:20:- `docs/07-roots/surgeons/a-g/geoffrey-cundiff.mdx`, line 35: its description of PESSRI as a randomized crossover trial of ring versus Gellhorn symptom relief and life impact agrees with the abstract.
