reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/open/notes.md:39:- `docs/01-foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/sachse-urethrotome.mdx` line 94: "voiding symptom scores improved similarly over 24 months after cold-knife urethrotomy and urethroplasty". The control arm was urethrotomy generally, not cold knife only: 105 of the 115 men who received an endoscopic procedure had optical cold-knife urethrotomy, 1 hot-knife, 9 dilatation (AM PDF pp25-26, Supplementary Table 2). The same sentence's 222 randomized, 15 versus 29 reinterventions and HR 0.52 (0.31-0.89) match the source.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/open/notes.md:41:- `docs/03-clinical-conditions/03b-voiding-outlet/urethral-stricture.mdx` (lines 322, 356, 368, 422) and `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/minimally-invasive/dviu.mdx` (lines 122, 139-150): statements (similar symptom improvement, fewer reinterventions, HR 0.52 with 95% CI 0.31-0.89, higher cost) agree with the sources. The dviu.mdx row "Cost over 24 months ... Urethrotomy appeared more cost-effective" agrees with the BMC Urol analysis. No numeric disagreement found. Both pages cite the monograph (hta24610) for the Eur Urol results as well; the monograph abstract confirms HR 0.52 (0.31-0.89) and the two-versus-five complication figure only.
reports/2026-10-18/clinical-evidence/work/open/notes.md:42:- `docs/01-foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/otis-urethrotome.mdx` refs 15 and 16 are duplicate protocol citations (OPEN protocol v1.8) and are not the published trial; not a numeric issue.
