## Disagreements with the old Journal Club record

- "Primary outcome: Stricture recurrence": wrong. The primary endpoints were penile complications (PCQ) and IIEF-5 change; recurrence was a secondary endpoint (primary PDF p2 section 2; registry).

Pages citing the trial: only `docs/03-clinical-conditions/03c-pelvic-support/apical-prolapse.mdx` (lines 83, 105; reference 15 is the HTA report). The two other hits for "VUE" are the unrelated SpaceOAR Vue device pages.

- Line 83 (vault trial, 208 women, similar 12-month symptom scores and serious adverse events, anterior stage 2b or more less frequent after abdominal repair): agrees with HTA pp. 60, 63, 67 (Tables 26, 28, 33).
- Line 105 (uterine trial, 563 women, similar 12-month symptom scores and serious adverse events): agrees with HTA pp. 30, 37 (Tables 9, 16).
- Reference 15 is correct (authors, title, journal, volume, pages, DOI).
- No disagreements found. The page does not mention the uterine-trial reoperation imbalance, the cost findings or the 12-month patient preference for hysterectomy.

## Inconsistencies inside the source (how I handled them)

1. Abstract: "563 out of 565 randomised women had uterine prolapse surgery." Chapter 4 says 563 were analyzed after 2 post-randomization exclusions, and only 215 of 280 preservation and 254 of 283 hysterectomy participants received the allocated apical operation (Table 8). I used the chapter. The same wording is used for the vault trial (208 of 209).
2. Table 16 percentages do not follow the printed N: 14/262 = 5.3% (printed 5.4%), 5/262 = 1.9% (printed 2.0%). The file and page quote the printed n (%) without a numerator/denominator pair. 16/269 = 5.9% is correct.
3. The serious-event effect 0.82 (0.38 to 1.75) is a "risk ratio" in the abstract and an odds ratio in the Table 16 footnote; the file gives the number without a type.
4. Vault cost-effectiveness: the Chapter 8 text gives £285,000 per QALY, but Table 35 gives £142,500 (£570 / 0.004). The file omits the ratio. The vault QALY interval is -0.031 to 0.041 in the abstract, scientific summary and Table 35 and -0.030 to 0.040 in the Chapter 8 text; I used the former.
5. Vault urinary incontinence: the text gives any incontinence as 30.8% (abdominal) versus 24.0% (vaginal); Table 30 gives 32/85 (37.6%) versus 25/86 (29.1%). Not used on the page.
6. Uterine trial: the text calls the odds ratio 2.71 for urgency a "MD" and describes urge incontinence, while Table 13's row is "Urgency". The file uses the table label and notes the text wording.
7. Recruitment start is March 2013 (scientific summary) or January 2013 (Chapter 3 text); 45 centers (Chapters 4, 7) versus 36 vault-recruiting centers (Chapter 6). The page gives March 2013 and omits the center count.
8. The uterine-trial discussion says re-admissions are in "Table 16"; they are in Table 15.
9. Chapter 5 counts 16 versus 8 further prolapse operations (cost analysis); Table 15 gives 15 versus 8 at 12 months after surgery. I used Table 15.

## Judgment calls for the integrator

- Two trials in one family. Arms are four (`preserve`, `hyst`, `abdominal`, `vaginal`), so `TrialQuickHit` joins four labels with "versus". `TrialComparison` shows the first control arm (vaginal hysterectomy) and the first primary result (the uterine POP-SS) for both the `uterine-preservation` and `native-tissue-vault` lanes, which is correct for the former only. Splitting the family into two trial files was not an option under the task.
- The 2021 article records **6 sling events in 6 men** and **13 AUS events in 11 men** among 180 and 173 operated men. The full 2022 report records **8 events in 8 sling men** and **15 events in 13 AUS men** among 180 and 175 operated men, adding two sling mesh erosions and two AUS-arm operations. These are report-version differences. Source: Abrams 2021 PDF pp 817–819, Table 3/Figure 1; HTA PDF pp 49–50, Tables 5–6.
- At 24 months the strict endpoint met the stated 15-point noninferiority margin (21/140 versus 22/148 continent; adjusted difference −0.6 points, 95% CI −9.2 to 8.0; P = 0.001). The less-strict definition did not meet it: 44/140 versus 51/148 continent; adjusted difference −4.0 points, 95% CI −16.7 to 8.8; P = 0.045 against the one-sided 0.025 threshold. Source: HTA PDF p 53, Table 7.

## Disagreements with existing WARWIKI pages

- `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/male-sui/male-stress-incontinence-database.mdx:54` says: “In the 2021 article, 6 sling and 13 AUS recipients had serious adverse events.” The 2021 article reports 6 sling **recipients** but 13 AUS **events in 11 recipients**. Locator: Abrams 2021 PDF pp 817–819, Surgery and AEs/Table 3. The same page's immediately following description of the 2022 report's 8/180 and 13/175 **recipients** (15 AUS events) is correct.
- The other seven pages with `MASTER` in their body were checked: `docs/01-foundations/tools/biomaterials/prosthetics/artificial-urinary-sphincter.mdx`, `docs/03-clinical-conditions/03a-storage-incontinence/sui-male.mdx`, `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/index.mdx`, and `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/{adjustable-continence-devices,advance-sling,artificial-urinary-sphincter,male-urethral-slings}.mdx`. No further numerical disagreement with the opened reports was identified. The rounded 24-month further-surgery figures of 11% versus 2% correspond to 20/190 (10.5%) versus 4/190 (2.1%) in the HTA report.

## Old Journal Club record

- Its sample size, comparison, 15-point margin, 87.0% versus 84.2% primary values, and noninferiority conclusion agree with the opened primary report.
- “Male Synthetic Sling versus Artificial Urinary Sphincter Trial” is an abbreviated expansion; the published trial expansion adds “Evaluation by Randomised controlled trial” (HTA PDF p 30).
- The proposed “AUS as the benchmark for more severe incontinence” interpretation is stronger than this trial's baseline-severity subgroup supports. The 2021 paper says the pad-weight subgroup difference did **not** reach significance; the HTA reports no evidence that pad weight modified the treatment effect. Locators: Abrams 2021 PDF p 815, Primary outcome; HTA PDF pp 53–55, Table 8/Figure 5.

## Unverified limits

- The 2026 journal update was abstract-only, so its operative and safety tables could not be independently compared with the HTA tables. Its abstract rounds the 24-month symptom difference to 1.4 (95% CI 0.2–2.6), while the full HTA table prints 1.42 (0.18–2.65).
- No data-cutoff date was stated in the opened reports; report `cutoff` fields remain null.
- Registry masking is "DOUBLE" (investigator and outcomes assessor), the paper is titled single-blind; the file uses "single-blind (blinded outcome assessment)".
- Abstract: no exclusion for prior Peyronie's therapy; protocol/registry excluded men currently on intralesional injections and asked others to stop therapy for 4 weeks. Both are on the page.
- The registry's IIEF outcome (all men: +2.5 versus −1.3, P = 0.06) does not name the domain; the abstract's significant IIEF-EF result is in men with erectile dysfunction only.

## Disagreements and unverifiable items on other WARWIKI pages

1. `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/docs/04-surgical-techniques/04j-sexual-dysfunction/penile-traction-therapy.mdx` line 44: "**+1.5 cm vs 0 cm stretched length (p < 0.001), −8.5° vs −1.1° primary curvature (p < 0.001), and −11.7° vs +1.3° composite curvature (p < 0.01)**". The +1.5 versus 0 cm and −11.7 versus +1.3 figures match the abstract and registry (+1.5 versus 0.0 cm is length to the penile tip; length to the corona is +1.3 versus +0.3 cm, P = 0.001). The −8.5° versus −1.1° "primary curvature" values and their P < 0.001 appear in neither the abstract nor the registry; I could not verify them (Urology Times, a conference report on a different enrollment of 120, gave −9 versus −1.0 degrees). The "62 traction and 27 control men" matches the registry.
2. Same page, line 45 (and `docs/03-clinical-conditions/03g-genital-scrotal/peyronies-disease.mdx` line 189): "Among 37 original traction-arm men using RestoreX ≥15 min/day for 6 mo, 95% had length gains and 61% had curvature improvements" and "mean use 31 min/day". The Joseph abstract says 95% of men treated for 6 months had length gains and 61% curve improvements, and separately that the as-treated analysis used at least 15 minutes per day; it does not state that the 95% and 61% denominators are the 37 men who used the device at least 15 minutes daily, nor that 31.1 minutes is per day. The 37 comes from the registry (original traction men with 6-month data). Unverified without the full text.
3. `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04j-sexual-dysfunction/penile-traction-therapy.mdx` line 116: "gained an additional 0.6–0.8 cm of length during months 3–6". The abstract gives 0.6 to 0.8 cm of additional length without naming the interval (the report covers 6 and 9 months).
4. `docs/03-clinical-conditions/03g-genital-scrotal/peyronies-disease.mdx` line 189 describes the 6-month result as an "as-treated subgroup (n = 37 using RestoreX ≥15 min/day)"; same issue as item 2.
5. `docs/03-clinical-conditions/03g-genital-scrotal/peyronies-disease.mdx` line 188 and `peyronies-disease/index.mdx` line 156 agree with the abstract and registry (110 men, 3:1, 62 versus 27, −11.7 versus +1.3, +1.5 versus 0 cm).
6. Nothing found that contradicts the primary report on `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04j-sexual-dysfunction/peyronies.mdx`, `male-cosmetic.mdx` (those cite the prostatectomy trial) or `docs/07-roots/surgeons/s-z/matthew-ziegelmann.mdx` / `landon-trost.mdx` (the Landon Trost page says he invented RestoreX; the protocol's conflict-of-interest section confirms this).

## Disagreements with the old Journal Club record

- `primaryOutcome`: "Change in penile curvature and stretched length". The primary outcome was safety (abstract and registry); length and curvature were secondary.
- [Nager et al., 2014, sham-incision blinding analysis](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4346086/): considered; it evaluates masking rather than a new clinical treatment endpoint.
- [Concomitant anterior-repair secondary analysis](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5995601/) and [pooled sexual-function analysis](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7483870/): considered; both combine OPUS with other trials and answer different questions.
- Searches did not identify a randomized OPUS report extending the sling-versus-sham outcome beyond 12 months. The SUPeR five-year sling analysis is a different trial and was not filed as OPUS follow-up.

## Disagreements and unresolved details

- **Old Journal Club record:** Its `name` is the acronym expansion, not the primary paper's published title. Its outcome values, randomized sample, comparison and approximate 12-month NNT match the primary paper. The short `primaryOutcome` wording omits that the 3-month endpoint is a composite including treatment and that 12-month incontinence is measured despite interval treatment (Wei 2012, Methods: Study Outcomes; Table 2). No numerical discrepancy was found.
- **Primary paper itself:** The PubMed/PMC abstract states major bleeding complications as 3.1%, while full-text Table 4 lists 5/164 (3.0% to one decimal). The trial file and page use the table's count, denominator and rounding.
- **WARWIKI pages searched for `OPUS`:** `docs/03-clinical-conditions/03c-pelvic-support/pelvic-organ-prolapse.mdx` (line 332), `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04g-prolapse-repair/anterior/anterior-colporrhaphy.mdx` (lines 142 and 146), and `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/female-slings-suspensions.mdx` (line 236). Their OPUS-specific numbers and statements agree with Wei 2012, Tables 2 and 4, and the Methods; no page-level source disagreement was found. The AUA/SUFU *guideline copy*, outside those site pages, describes the 27.3% versus 43.0% OPUS composite as SUI; Wei 2012 defines it more broadly as stress, urge or mixed incontinence (Methods: Study Outcomes; Table 2). The appraisal uses the paper's definition.
- The full text reports `327/337` one-year follow-up completion but only `287/337` evaluable 12-month primary-outcome data (Wei 2012, Figure 1 and Results: Study Population). The latter distinction is retained in the trial file and page.
- No FDA approval or clearance statement was added because none was needed to describe OPUS and no OPUS-specific FDA action was verified.
## Disagreements and flags
- Pages (not edited) that use numbers I could not confirm from the abstract (all attributed to Skinner 2015): `docs/04-surgical-techniques/04c-urinary-diversion/t-pouch-modification.mdx` (423/260; 22% vs 13%, p<0.01; 44 cm reservoirs; Leadbetter/Bricker), `modified-studer-pouch.mdx` (423, 260, 124 deaths, 39 without data), `camey-ii-neobladder.mdx`, `double-t-pouch.mdx` (423/260; three-year cumulative incidence of secondary surgery), `bowel-segments.mdx` (237 vs 247 matches the abstract).
- Confirmed from the abstract: 484 randomized (237 T-pouch, 247 Studer), Feb 2002 to Nov 2009, eGFR -6.6 vs -6.4 mL/min/1.73 m2, p=0.35, multivariable p=0.63, "mean eGFR change" wording (the abstract says "decreased by", not "mean"). Pages that call the change the "mean eGFR decline" are consistent with the direction.
- `t-pouch-modification.mdx` lists Ahmadi's questionnaire correctly (68%, 4.5 y, 47%/72%, 22.3%, 62.5%, 9.5%) and the abstract matches; it says "male participants from both USC-STAR arms", which the abstract supports only as patients "enrolled in a clinical trial" randomized to T-pouch or Studer.
- Pages and the old Journal Club record call the pouches "Studer-type refluxing" and the trial "settled the reflux debate"; the abstract's conclusion is narrower (T-pouch did not prevent a moderate eGFR reduction at 3 years). Old record: "484" and "-6.4 vs -6.6" match; "added complexity" and "more secondary surgical procedures" are supported only as "increased diversion-related secondary surgery". The old `design: RCT` is correct; it omitted that the trial was single-center and unmasked.
- Registry versus report: ClinicalTrials.gov lists 529 enrolled (actual) and a primary outcome covering renal function, upper-tract anatomy and need for medical or surgical intervention measured 3 years after the last patient enrolled; the report's primary endpoint is eGFR change from baseline to 3 years among 484 randomized. Not stated on the page.
- Pagination: PubMed gives pp 433-439 (used, matches the site pages); Crossref gives 433-440. Ahmadi authors: Crossref/publisher lists Simma-Chiang as third author; PubMed lists six authors without her; the publisher list is used.
- design.phase is "not applicable" (ClinicalTrials.gov); PubMed labels the publication type phase III.
- `verdict: negative` rests on the abstract: the prespecified comparison favored neither arm on the primary endpoint and the T-pouch had more secondary surgery.
- Closure technique: text says continuous closure in 3 studies but cites 4 references [20,21,23,25]; with 2 interrupted and 1 not stated the counts reach 6 or 7. The page says "most trials".
- Inclusion criteria say over 18 years; Table 1 and Results give an age range of 17 to 72 years.
- Gulani 2019 is described in its own title as "a comparative study" and used lingual mucosa; it is nonetheless pooled as one of the seven RCTs. Four trials used quasi-randomization (text [19-22]; Figure 2A shows high risk for sequence generation in the same four).
- Methods list six primary outcomes (liquid and solid tolerance separate); Results report them as one combined diet outcome.
- Table 1 sums: nonclosure 15+21+72+16+18+24+25 = 191, closure 15+21+63+16+16+26+25 = 182, total 373, matching the abstract. Arm sizes in the file come from this sum (Table 1).
- Events in Figures 3, 4, 6 and 7 are counted per time-point subgroup, so patients are counted more than once in the "total" rows; the file notes say so.
- Source text typos: "One reviewers independently identified"; reference 11 cited for the Cochrane risk-of-bias tool (it is Barbagli 2005).

## Disagreements with WARWIKI pages

Searched `docs/` for Güler, the DOI, nonclosure/non-closure and secondary-intention wording.

- `docs/01-foundations/surgical-principles/grafts/buccal-mucosa.mdx` line 42: "included seven studies, four using quasi-random allocation and one involving lingual grafts. It suggested less early pain with nonclosure" agrees with the source. Reference 21 gives `2023;21(1):18` (no end page; the pages are 18-30). Line 40 and the Hwang line (three RCTs in the main analysis, two quasi-randomized in sensitivity analysis) could not be checked: only the Hwang abstract was read (abstract says five studies, 346 randomized patients, 260 completed).
- Pages citing Soave 2018 for "noninferior for oral pain" (`dorsal-onlay-omg.mdx` line 80, `bmg-onlay-ureter.mdx` lines 107 and 217, `mouth-retractors.mdx` line 100, `oral-cavity.mdx` line 75, `buccal-mucosa.mdx` line 40) were not checked against Soave (not opened). Güler's Figure 2A rates Soave low risk for sequence generation and allocation concealment but high risk for participant blinding, outcome blinding and attrition; it is the largest trial (135 of 373 patients).
- No page states a number from Güler that disagrees with the source.


