docs/01-foundations/surgical-principles/incisions-closure.mdx:76:This was a technique package: the small-bite arm used approximately 5-mm bites/spacing, 2-0 PDS Plus II and a 31-mm needle; the large-bite arm used 10-mm bites/spacing and USP 1 double-loop suture with a 48-mm needle. The trial cannot isolate bite size from suture caliber, needle size and tissue capture. Emergency laparotomy was excluded.<sup>[[6]](#ref6)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/male-sui/male-stress-incontinence-database.mdx:54:- **MASTER RCT** (Abrams 2021, n = 380): male sling met the 12-month noninferiority criterion (87.0% versus 84.2% remained incontinent under a strict any-leakage definition; continence-success risk difference −3.6 percentage points, sling minus AUS, 95% CI −11.6 to 4.6, within the noninferiority margin; the abstract prints the same difference with a positive sign); the ICIQ-UI SF mean difference at 12 months was 1.4 (95% CI 0.2 to 2.6) in the 2021 article versus 1.30 (95% CI 0.11 to 2.49, p = 0.032) in the full HTA report — the same comparison, both favoring AUS.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)[[14]](#ref14)</sup> In the 2021 article, 6 sling and 13 AUS recipients had serious adverse events; the full HTA report counts 8 of 180 sling and 13 of 175 AUS recipients (15 AUS events because one man had three), a difference that was uncertain.<sup>[[14]](#ref14)</sup> At 24 months, further surgery occurred in 20 sling (11%) versus 4 AUS (2%) recipients; the ICIQ-UI SF mean difference was 1.4 (95% CI 0.2 to 2.6) favoring AUS.<sup>[[13]](#ref13)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/anastomotic/jordan-vessel-sparing-epa.mdx:99:The Scandinavian randomized trial (151 patients) compared tEPA with BMG, not Jordan VS-EPA. It found more penile complaints after tEPA but no significant IIEF-5 difference. Recurrence, defined as need for retreatment, was 12.9% overall: 12.2% after tEPA (74 operated) and 13.0% after BMG (77 operated) at 12 months<sup>[[11]](#ref11)[[20]](#ref20)</sup>; the trial was not designed to establish equivalent recurrence risk.<sup>[[11]](#ref11)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/flap/pedicled-prepucial-tube.mdx:116:| Wiener 1997<sup>[[14]](#ref14)</sup> | 74 (tube) vs 58 (onlay) | Proximal hypospadias | Tube **36%** vs Onlay **31%** | 14% vs 17% (NS) | NS | **Tube 12.2% vs Onlay 0% (p = 0.016)** | 20.3 mo |
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/flap/pedicled-prepucial-tube.mdx:123:1. **Diverticulum formation is the hallmark complication of tubularization.** Wiener 1997 found diverticula in 12.2% of tubularized versus 0% of onlay repairs (p = 0.016).<sup>[[14]](#ref14)</sup> Wang 2019 reported 12.8% diverticula in 320 patients, with 31.7% of diverticula associated with concomitant strictures (p &lt; 0.001).<sup>[[12]](#ref12)[[14]](#ref14)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/male-urethroplasty-incisions-approaches.mdx:80:| **Non-transecting** | Corpus spongiosum is not fully transected, so antegrade blood flow is preserved | Cotter 2019 multi-institutional data: 2,098 anterior urethroplasties (10 surgeons, 2010-2017): bulbar excisional repairs performed without transection rose 430%. Includes [Jordan vessel-sparing EPA](/docs/surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/jordan-vessel-sparing-epa), [non-transecting bulbar](/docs/surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/non-transecting-bulbar), and the entire [ANTA / MsANTA / MANTA / ntAAU](/docs/surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/manta) family<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup>. A 151-patient randomized trial of transecting EPA versus buccal-graft urethroplasty for bulbar strictures ≤2 cm reported perceived penile shortening in 26% versus 5.4% and reduced glans stiffness in 19% versus 6.8% at 12 months; recurrence requiring retreatment was 12.2% versus 13.0%.<sup>[[30]](#ref30)</sup> |
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/anastomotic/non-transecting-bulbar.mdx:114:- **Scandinavian Urethroplasty Study (Nilsen et al.), randomized trial (151 patients with bulbar strictures ≤2 cm and no previous urethroplasty, 12-month follow-up):** Compared transecting EPA to non-transecting BMG. tEPA had significantly more penile complications (p = 0.02), particularly reduced glans filling (p = 0.03) and shortened penis (p = 0.001). No difference in IIEF-5 scores. Recurrence, defined as need for retreatment, occurred in 12.9% overall: 12.2% after tEPA (74 operated) and 13.0% after BMG (77 operated) during 12-month follow-up<sup>[[9]](#ref9)[[20]](#ref20)</sup>; the study was not designed to detect differences in recurrence. The penile complications questionnaire was not validated.<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/anastomotic/non-transecting-bulbar.mdx:118:**2026 trial update:** Holm and colleagues restate the 12-month results: more penile shortening and reduced glans filling after tEPA than BMG, with one or more penile complications in 33% versus 16% (p = 0.02) and recurrence of 12.2% versus 13.0%. They note that no randomized trial has compared tEPA with ntEPA, so the superiority of ntEPA remains a hypothesis; follow-up of at least 6 years is planned.<sup>[[20]](#ref20)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/anastomotic/excision-primary-anastomosis.mdx:117:The 151-patient Scandinavian randomized trial compared transecting EPA with BMG for bulbar strictures ≤2 cm in adults aged 18–70 without prior open urethroplasty; median stricture length was 1 cm in each arm.<sup>[[18]](#ref18)</sup> At 12 months, reduced glans stiffness during erection was reported by 13/70 (19%) after transecting EPA versus 5/74 (6.8%) after BMG, and perceived erect penile shortening by 18/70 (26%) versus 4/74 (5.4%), respectively.<sup>[[18]](#ref18)</sup> IIEF-5 scores did not differ significantly. Recurrence, defined as need for retreatment, was 12.9% overall at 12 months: 12.2% after transecting EPA (74 operated) versus 13.0% after BMG (77 operated).<sup>[[18]](#ref18)[[19]](#ref19)</sup> The trial was not powered to establish equal recurrence risk.<sup>[[18]](#ref18)[[19]](#ref19)</sup> A 2026 update of the trial, reporting the same 3- and 12-month data, found one or more penile complications (reduced glans stiffness, loss of glans sensation, shortening or changed direction of the erect penis) at 12 months in 23 men (33%) after transecting EPA versus 12 (16%) after BMG (p = 0.02); the authors advise avoiding transection of the corpus spongiosum when possible, and follow-up of at least 6 years is planned.<sup>[[19]](#ref19)</sup>
