docs/01-foundations/tools/biomaterials/prosthetics/artificial-urinary-sphincter.mdx:62:### AUSCO Prospective Study
docs/01-foundations/tools/biomaterials/prosthetics/artificial-urinary-sphincter.mdx:64:AUSCO was a prospective, single-arm study of the AMS 800 in 115 men at 17 sites. At 12 months after activation, 91/97 with pad-weight data achieved at least a 50% reduction; 61/101 reported no pad use. Nine of 115 underwent revision, with none attributed to device infection; 17/115 (15%) had serious adverse events, including 10/115 (8.7%) adjudicated device- or procedure-related.<sup>[[7]](#ref7)</sup> The full report separately records three postoperative wound infections that resolved without erosion or explantation. Denominators are available-case, there was no randomized comparator, sites were high-volume, and follow-up was one year. See the [procedure evidence summary](/docs/surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/procedures/artificial-urinary-sphincter).<sup>[[7]](#ref7)</sup>
docs/01-foundations/tools/biomaterials/prosthetics/artificial-urinary-sphincter.mdx:145:<a id="ref7"></a>7. Kaufman MR, Wood HM, Terlecki R, et al. The Artificial Urinary Sphincter Clinical Outcomes Trial: Primary Results. *J Urol.* 2026;215(2):194-202. Published online October 3, 2025. doi:[10.1097/JU.0000000000004796](https://doi.org/10.1097/JU.0000000000004796)
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/male-sui/male-stress-incontinence-database.mdx:19:AUA/GURS/SUFU 2024 recommends discussing AUS for mild-to-severe SUI <Rec>(Strong)</Rec>, describes it as the most predictable and reliable option, and recommends counseling that effectiveness likely declines over time and reoperation is common.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup> Before implantation, confirm that the patient has the physical and cognitive ability to operate the AUS pump <Rec>(Clinical Principle)</Rec>.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup> In the single-arm AUSCO trial, 91/97 (94%) achieved ≥50% pad-weight reduction and 61/101 (60%) reported zero pad use at 12 months after device activation.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup> In a French first-implant cohort of 8,475 men, reintervention-free survival was 71%, 57% and 40% at 2, 5 and 10 years, respectively (Lenfant 2025).<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup> In the MASTER RCT (n = 380), a fixed male sling met its prespecified 12-month noninferiority margin against AUS on a strict any-leakage endpoint, but 87.0% versus 84.2% still reported some incontinence, and several secondary symptom and satisfaction outcomes favored AUS.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup> At 24 months, the trial's secondary continence analysis met noninferiority under the strict no-leakage definition (21/140 sling versus 22/148 AUS) but not under the less-strict definition (44/140 versus 51/148).<sup>[[13]](#ref13)[[14]](#ref14)</sup> Further surgery occurred in 20 sling (11%) versus 4 AUS (2%) recipients.<sup>[[13]](#ref13)[[14]](#ref14)</sup> Fixed slings remain an option for selected mild-to-moderate non-radiated SUI; severe leakage and prior radiation are associated with poorer sling results.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[2]](#ref2)</sup> EAU also advises counseling that prior transurethral surgery may worsen nonadjustable sling outcomes <Rec>(Weak)</Rec>.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup> A 2025 systematic review of 62 cohorts (5,696 men after prostate surgery) graded the associations of irradiation, greater pad use and greater 24-hour urine loss with fixed-sling failure as high certainty, and those of prior urethral stricture, previous incontinence therapy, detrusor overactivity, higher MSIGS score and an unfavorable repositioning test as moderate certainty.<sup>[[16]](#ref16)</sup> ATOMS is an adjustable option, including after failed fixed slings in observational series, but head-to-head evidence has not established superior outcomes from adjustability.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)[[6]](#ref6)</sup> Male urethral bulking has limited, heterogeneous evidence and a niche role; the guideline gives a strong recommendation to counsel that efficacy is low and cure is rare.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/male-sui/male-stress-incontinence-database.mdx:55:- **AUSCO trial** (Kaufman, 115 implanted at 17 sites): in this single-arm study, 91/97 (94%) achieved ≥50% pad-weight reduction and 61/101 (60%) reported zero pad use at 12 months after device activation; 9/115 (7.8%) underwent device revision (mechanical malfunction 3, erosion 2, none for infection) and 15% had a serious adverse event.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
