The AUA/SUFU NLUTD guideline (2021):<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup>

- **Statement 40 <Rec>(Strong Recommendation; Grade A)</Rec>:** in NLUTD patients with SCI or MS refractory to oral medications, clinicians should recommend onabotulinumtoxinA to improve storage parameters, decrease incontinence, and improve QoL<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup>
- **Statement 41 <Rec>(Conditional; Grade C)</Rec>:** in NLUTD patients other than SCI or MS who are refractory to oral medications, clinicians may offer onabotulinumtoxinA; evidence is more limited<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup>
- **Statement 42 <Rec>(Clinical Principle)</Rec>:** in NLUTD patients who void spontaneously, discuss the specific risk of retention and possible need for intermittent catheterization before choosing toxin<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup>

The EAU Neuro-Urology guideline (limited update March 2026) also recommends detrusor injection of botulinum toxin A to reduce NDO when antimuscarinic therapy is ineffective (Strong; level 1a). It reports that repeated injections appear possible without loss of efficacy, up to 10 cycles in some series, and that 100 U may lower the catheterization rate in MS.<sup>[[53]](#ref53)</sup> Post-treatment urodynamic studies may be needed to monitor bladder pressure in patients with NDO.<sup>[[53]](#ref53)</sup>

### Pivotal phase 3 — MS and SCI

The registration program (Sanford 2014 review) produced:<sup>[[10]](#ref10)</sup>

- Significant reductions in UI episodes and MCC increases with matching detrusor-pressure reductions
- **200 U versus 300 U:** in the two pivotal trials (Cruz 2011, n = 275; Ginsberg 2012, n = 416), both doses beat placebo with no clinically relevant efficacy or duration advantage for 300 U.<sup>[[55]](#ref55)[[56]](#ref56)</sup> CIC initiation among patients not catheterizing at baseline rose with dose (Ginsberg: 10% placebo, 35% at 200 U, 42% at 300 U), so 200 U is the labeled adult dose.<sup>[[55]](#ref55)[[56]](#ref56)</sup> The AUA/SUFU guideline likewise finds no efficacy difference between the doses and a dose-dependent increase in retention and CIC; in its pooled MS analysis of the pivotal trials, 31.4% (200 U) and 47.1% (300 U) started CIC versus 4.5% with placebo<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup>
- Most common AEs: UTI and urinary retention
- Among noncatheterizing adults in the label’s pooled NDO trials, 30.6% required CIC after 200 U versus 6.7% with placebo; discuss the relevant population and possible prolonged catheterization.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>

### Low-dose 100 U for noncatheterizing MS — Tullman 2018

Placebo-controlled evidence supports symptom benefit from 100 U in noncatheterizing MS patients; 200 U remains the recommended adult NDO label dose:<sup>[[11]](#ref11)</sup>

**AUA/SUFU/AUGS genitourinary syndrome of menopause guideline** (2025). Chaired the joint guideline on identifying, counseling, and treating patients with genitourinary syndrome of menopause.

</KeyPublication>

<KeyPublication publication={publicationData.keyPublications[1]} citations={citations}>

**Artificial Urinary Sphincter Clinical Outcomes trial** (2026). Led the prospective multicenter AUSCO study reporting continence, safety, and quality-of-life outcomes after AMS 800 implantation.

</KeyPublication>

<KeyPublication publication={publicationData.keyPublications[2]} citations={citations} />

