 succeeded in 0ms:
46-The AUA/SUFU OAB guideline (2024) says clinicians *should offer* sacral neuromodulation (SNM), percutaneous tibial nerve stimulation (PTNS) and/or intradetrusor onabotulinumtoxinA to patients with an inadequate response to, or intolerable side effects from, pharmacotherapy or behavioral therapy <Rec>(Statement 25; Moderate Recommendation, Grade A)</Rec>.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup> In shared decision-making, clinicians may offer these minimally invasive therapies without requiring prior behavioral, non-invasive or drug trials <Rec>(Statement 24; Expert Opinion)</Rec>, and it is reasonable to go straight to toxin after β3-agonist failure or when a patient declines oral drugs.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup> PVR must be measured before injection <Rec>(Statement 26; Clinical Principle)</Rec>; the pivotal RCTs used PVR exclusion thresholds above 100–200 mL, so the panel advises caution above those thresholds.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup> Reassess about two weeks after the initial injection to assess symptom improvement and rule out retention, and check PVR if symptoms have not improved or have worsened (Statement 27).<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup> A patient with an inadequate response to 100 U and minimal side effects may be offered 200 U off label, above the FDA-labeled 100-U OAB dose, with counseling about higher retention and UTI risk.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[5]](#ref5)</sup> Repeat injections are typically needed every 3–12 months, and toxin should be offered cautiously when symptoms are mainly nocturnal.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup>
47-
48-The EAU non-neurogenic female LUTS guideline (limited update March 2026) recommends bladder-wall injection of onabotulinumtoxinA 100 U for OAB or UUI refractory to conservative or drug treatment <Rec>(Strong)</Rec>. It also recommends warning patients about limited duration, UTI and possible prolonged intermittent catheterization <Rec>(Strong)</Rec>. It finds no difference between suburothelial and intradetrusor injection (level 1a), and it notes that other doses and other toxin brands are not licensed for OAB in Europe.<sup>[[52]](#ref52)</sup>
49-
50:### Pivotal phase 3 — Nitti 2013
51-
52-The registration trial (n = 557 adults with OAB / urge urinary incontinence refractory to antimuscarinics) remains the anchor data:<sup>[[6]](#ref6)</sup>
53-
54-- **UI reduction:** −2.65 episodes/day (onabotA 100 U) versus −0.87 (placebo); p &lt; 0.001<sup>[[6]](#ref6)</sup>
55-- **Positive treatment benefit:** 60.8% versus 29.2% (p &lt; 0.001)<sup>[[6]](#ref6)</sup>
56-- **Complete continence:** 22.9% versus 6.5%<sup>[[6]](#ref6)</sup>
57-- Significant improvements across urgency, frequency, nocturia, and QoL (I-QOL, KHQ)
58:- Most common AEs: UTI and urinary retention; urinary retention 5.4% in this trial; retention and initiation of clean intermittent catheterization (CIC) are distinct outcomes<sup>[[6]](#ref6)</sup>
59-
60-The companion phase 3 trial (Chapple 2013, n = 548; 100 U as 20 injections of 0.5 mL) gave a similar result: −2.95 versus −1.03 incontinence episodes/day at week 12 and a positive treatment benefit in 62.8% versus 26.8%. CIC was started in 6.9% versus 0.7%, and mean PVR at week 2 was 46.9 mL versus 10.1 mL.<sup>[[54]](#ref54)</sup>
61-
62-### Long-term efficacy — 3.5-year extension
--
96-The registration program (Sanford 2014 review) produced:<sup>[[10]](#ref10)</sup>
97-
98-- Significant reductions in UI episodes and MCC increases with matching detrusor-pressure reductions
99-- **200 U versus 300 U:** in the two pivotal trials (Cruz 2011, n = 275; Ginsberg 2012, n = 416), both doses beat placebo with no clinically relevant efficacy or duration advantage for 300 U.<sup>[[55]](#ref55)[[56]](#ref56)</sup> CIC initiation among patients not catheterizing at baseline rose with dose (Ginsberg: 10% placebo, 35% at 200 U, 42% at 300 U), so 200 U is the labeled adult dose.<sup>[[55]](#ref55)[[56]](#ref56)</sup> The AUA/SUFU guideline likewise finds no efficacy difference between the doses and a dose-dependent increase in retention and CIC; in its pooled MS analysis of the pivotal trials, 31.4% (200 U) and 47.1% (300 U) started CIC versus 4.5% with placebo<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup>
100:- Most common AEs: UTI and urinary retention
101-- Among noncatheterizing adults in the label’s pooled NDO trials, 30.6% required CIC after 200 U versus 6.7% with placebo; discuss the relevant population and possible prolonged catheterization.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
