 ## History
 
-The original Axonics r-SNM was the first FDA-approved rechargeable SNM system, designed to deliver therapy for at least 15 years.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup> Its design targeted two limitations of earlier non-rechargeable generators. Battery depletion required replacement surgery, and average battery life was about 4.4 years in the historical device.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)[[6]](#ref6)</sup> Early European experience (RELAX-OAB) preceded the US pivotal study (ARTISAN-SNM), and the FDA approved the system in 2019.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)[[10]](#ref10)</sup><sup>[[21]](#ref21)</sup> Boston Scientific acquired Axonics in 2024. The current F15 and R20 are successors to that first rechargeable generator.
