 ## EPA vs. Other Urethroplasty Techniques
 
-The 151-patient Scandinavian randomized trial compared transecting EPA with BMG for bulbar strictures ≤2 cm in adults aged 18–70 without prior open urethroplasty; median stricture length was 1 cm in each arm.<sup>[[18]](#ref18)</sup> At 12 months, reduced glans stiffness during erection was reported by 13/70 (19%) after transecting EPA versus 5/74 (6.8%) after BMG, and perceived erect penile shortening by 18/70 (26%) versus 4/74 (5.4%), respectively.<sup>[[18]](#ref18)</sup> IIEF-5 scores did not differ significantly. Recurrence, defined as need for retreatment, was 12.9% overall at 12 months: 12.2% after transecting EPA (74 operated) versus 13.0% after BMG (77 operated).<sup>[[18]](#ref18)[[19]](#ref19)</sup> The trial was not powered to establish equal recurrence risk.<sup>[[18]](#ref18)[[19]](#ref19)</sup> A 2026 update of the trial, reporting the same 3- and 12-month data, found one or more penile complications (reduced glans stiffness, loss of glans sensation, shortening or changed direction of the erect penis) at 12 months in 23 men (33%) after transecting EPA versus 12 (16%) after BMG (p = 0.02); the authors advise avoiding transection of the corpus spongiosum when possible, and follow-up of at least 6 years is planned.<sup>[[19]](#ref19)</sup>
+The 151-patient Scandinavian randomized trial compared transecting EPA with BMG for bulbar strictures ≤2 cm in adults aged 18–70 without prior open urethroplasty; median stricture length was 1 cm in each arm.<sup>[[18]](#ref18)</sup> At 12 months, reduced glans stiffness during erection was reported by 13/70 (19%) after transecting EPA versus 5/74 (6.8%) after BMG, and perceived erect penile shortening by 18/70 (26%) versus 4/74 (5.4%), respectively.<sup>[[18]](#ref18)</sup> IIEF-5 scores did not differ significantly. Recurrence, defined as need for retreatment, was 12.9% overall at 12 months: 12.0% after transecting EPA (9/75 randomized) versus 13.2% after BMG (10/76 randomized).<sup>[[18]](#ref18)[[19]](#ref19)</sup> The trial was not powered to establish equal recurrence risk.<sup>[[18]](#ref18)[[19]](#ref19)</sup> A 2026 update of the trial, reporting the same 3- and 12-month data, found one or more penile complications (reduced glans stiffness, loss of glans sensation, shortening or changed direction of the erect penis) at 12 months in 23 men (33%) after transecting EPA versus 12 (16%) after BMG (p = 0.02); the authors advise avoiding transection of the corpus spongiosum when possible, and follow-up of at least 6 years is planned.<sup>[[19]](#ref19)</sup>
 
