 - **Multi-institutional analysis (Chapman et al., 352 patients):** At six months, the study's composite sexual-dysfunction outcome (a SHIM decline of at least five points or a patient-reported adverse change) occurred in 4.3% after non-transecting versus 14.3% after transecting repair (p = 0.008). On multivariate analysis, only the transecting technique was independently associated with sexual dysfunction (p = 0.01); age, stricture length, etiology, and surgeon were not.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
 - **Meta-analysis (Zhao et al., 1,683 patients):** ntBU had a significantly lower incidence of erectile dysfunction compared to transecting bulbar urethroplasty (tBU); no differences in other perioperative outcomes.<sup>[[8]](#ref8)</sup>
 - **Meta-analysis (Oszczudlowski et al., 19 studies):** ED risk ratio 0.73 (95% CI 0.49–1.08), trending toward benefit but not statistically significant. Penile complications were significantly lower: RR 0.47 (95% CI 0.28–0.76, p &lt; 0.05).<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
-- **Scandinavian Urethroplasty Study (Nilsen et al.), randomized trial (151 patients with bulbar strictures ≤2 cm and no previous urethroplasty, 12-month follow-up):** Compared transecting EPA to non-transecting BMG. tEPA had significantly more penile complications (p = 0.02), particularly reduced glans filling (p = 0.03) and shortened penis (p = 0.001). No difference in IIEF-5 scores. Recurrence, defined as need for retreatment, occurred in 12.9% overall: 12.2% after tEPA (74 operated) and 13.0% after BMG (77 operated) during 12-month follow-up<sup>[[9]](#ref9)[[20]](#ref20)</sup>; the study was not designed to detect differences in recurrence. The penile complications questionnaire was not validated.<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup>
+- **Scandinavian Urethroplasty Study (Nilsen et al.), randomized trial (151 patients with bulbar strictures ≤2 cm and no previous urethroplasty, 12-month follow-up):** Compared transecting EPA to non-transecting BMG. tEPA had significantly more penile complications (p = 0.02), particularly reduced glans filling (p = 0.03) and shortened penis (p = 0.001). No difference in IIEF-5 scores. Recurrence, defined as need for retreatment, occurred in 12.9% overall: 12.0% after tEPA (9/75 randomized) and 13.2% after BMG (10/76 randomized) during 12-month follow-up<sup>[[9]](#ref9)[[20]](#ref20)</sup>; the study was not designed to detect differences in recurrence. The penile complications questionnaire was not validated.<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup>
 - **Lumen et al. (75 patients, 55 bulbar and 20 posterior, median follow-up 30 months):** Stricture recurred in 8%. De novo ED was reported by 7 of 32 evaluable patients (21.9%) and post-void dribbling by 2 of 42 (4.7%) at 3 months.<sup>[[10]](#ref10)</sup>
 - **Mixed-urethroplasty IIEF cohort (Frankiewicz et al., 103 patients).** Higher baseline erectile-function scores and younger age were associated with better postoperative scores, while diabetes was associated with worse three-month scores; these estimates are not specific to ntBU.<sup>[[15]](#ref15)</sup>
 
