+- **MASTER RCT** (Abrams 2021, n = 380): male sling met the 12-month noninferiority criterion (87.0% versus 84.2% remained incontinent under a strict any-leakage definition; continence-success risk difference −3.6 percentage points, sling minus AUS, 95% CI −11.6 to 4.6, within the noninferiority margin; the abstract prints the same difference with a positive sign); the ICIQ-UI SF mean difference at 12 months was 1.4 (95% CI 0.2 to 2.6) in the 2021 article versus 1.30 (95% CI 0.11 to 2.49, p = 0.032) in the full HTA report — the same comparison, both favoring AUS.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)[[14]](#ref14)</sup> In the 2021 article, 6 sling recipients had serious adverse events, while 11 AUS recipients had 13 serious adverse events; the full HTA report counts 8 of 180 sling and 13 of 175 AUS recipients (15 AUS events because one man had three), a difference that was uncertain.<sup>[[14]](#ref14)</sup> At 24 months, further surgery occurred in 20 sling (11%) versus 4 AUS (2%) recipients; the ICIQ-UI SF mean difference was 1.4 (95% CI 0.2 to 2.6) favoring AUS.<sup>[[13]](#ref13)</sup>
 - **AUSCO trial** (Kaufman, 115 implanted at 17 sites): in this single-arm study, 91/97 (94%) achieved ≥50% pad-weight reduction and 61/101 (60%) reported zero pad use at 12 months after device activation; 9/115 (7.8%) underwent device revision (mechanical malfunction 3, erosion 2, none for infection) and 15% had a serious adverse event.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
