# IMPROVE source and consistency notes

## Reports opened and used

| Report | Source opened | Read level | Bibliography check |
|---|---|---|---|
| Rahn et al., *JAMA* 2023;330(7):615-625, DOI 10.1001/jama.2023.12317, PMID 37581673 | [JAMA full text](https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/fullarticle/2808329); [PubMed record](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37581673/) | Full text | PubMed and publisher agree on authors, year, journal, volume, issue, pages, DOI and PMID. No local article copy identified. |
| Rahn et al., *Am J Obstet Gynecol* 2024;231(2):263.e1-263.e10, DOI 10.1016/j.ajog.2024.04.042, PMID 38710269 | [PubMed record and abstract](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38710269/) | Abstract | PubMed confirms authors, 2024-05-04 online date, journal, volume, issue, pages, DOI and PMID. [Publisher PDF](https://www.ajog.org/article/S0002-9378%2824%2900568-4/pdf) could not be fetched. No local article copy identified. |

The local `reports/audit-v2/sources-local` DOI search found audit logs mentioning the paper, but no article text or PDF. Filename searches in `~/Documents/Medicine` and `~/Downloads` found no matching Rahn or IMPROVE copy. PMC article pages presented a browser check, so the accessible JAMA publisher full text was used for the primary report. DOI redirects were inaccessible through the web tool; PubMed and publisher records supplied the metadata checks.

## Reports considered but left out

- Rahn et al., "Effects of Preoperative Intravaginal Estrogen on Pelvic Floor Disorder Symptoms in Postmenopausal Women with Pelvic Organ Prolapse," *Am J Obstet Gynecol.* 2023;229(3):309.e1-309.e10, DOI 10.1016/j.ajog.2023.05.023, PMID 37244454: a preoperative symptom ancillary analysis. The PMC page challenged the browser, the publisher page blocked access, and the PubMed page did not return readable content. Search snippets alone were not treated as an opened report. Its findings were not entered into the trial file or appraisal.
- Rahn et al., "Design of a Randomized Clinical Trial of Perioperative Vaginal Estrogen Versus Placebo with Transvaginal Native Tissue Apical Prolapse Repair," *Female Pelvic Med Reconstr Surg.* 2021;27(1):e227-e233, DOI 10.1097/SPV.0000000000000899: protocol/design paper, without surgical outcomes; the primary report provides the implemented methods. The PMC page challenged the browser.
- No guideline or regulatory section was added to the page: the guideline material encountered addresses prolapse or GSM generally, rather than a recommendation citing or resting on IMPROVE. No FDA approval or clearance was checked for this trial's perioperative use.

## Disagreements and source details

- **Old Journal Club record, `n: 206`:** 206 were enrolled; 199 were randomized (102 estrogen, 97 placebo), and 186 were operated and analyzed (93 per arm). JAMA full text, Results/Study Population and Figure 2. The old record did not distinguish these denominators.
- **Old Journal Club bottom line, “Debunks routine perioperative vaginal estrogen as a way to strengthen a native-tissue prolapse repair”:** the prespecified primary endpoint did not show benefit, but its adjusted HR was 1.97 with a wide 95% CI of 0.92–4.22. The trial supports “no demonstrated improvement in repair success,” not a precise exclusion of any possible benefit or harm. JAMA full text, Results/Primary Outcome and Limitations.
- **WARWIKI `docs/01-foundations/pharmacology/hormonal-therapies/preoperative-hormonal-priming.mdx`, 12-month results table, “Failure components | Anatomic recurrence was the most common component, often partial apical descent without prolapse beyond the hymen or symptoms”:** the JAMA report identifies anatomical recurrence as the most common primary failure component, but discusses the predominance of partial, asymptomatic apical descent in **one per-protocol analysis**, not as a breakdown of all primary-analysis failures. JAMA full text, Results/Primary Outcome and Discussion. The page should keep that distinction if revised.
- **Primary article's own discrepancy:** JAMA Table 2 gives placebo suture exposure as 15/87 (17%) after month 6, while the article prose says 15/97 (17%); 15/97 is 15.5%. The trial file follows the table's explicit assessment denominator. JAMA full text, Table 2 and Results/Adverse Events.
- No other confirmed source disagreement was found on the WARWIKI pages returned by the exact `IMPROVE` search. The separate `IMPROVE-multi` blood-pressure trial was excluded from this comparison.

## Verification limits

# MASTER evidence and consistency notes

## Reports opened

1. **Primary, full text:** Abrams P, Constable LD, Cooper D, et al. *Eur Urol.* 2021;79(6):812–823. DOI [10.1016/j.eururo.2021.01.024](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eururo.2021.01.024); PMID [33551297](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33551297/). Read the owner's PDF at `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/reports/audit-v2/sources-local/pdfs-2026-10-06/master-abrams-2021-eur-urol.pdf` with its local text extraction. The PubMed record confirms author order, online date (February 4, 2021), journal, volume, issue, and pages.
2. **Full trial and economic report, full text:** Constable L, Abrams P, Cooper D, et al. *Health Technol Assess.* 2022;26(36):1–152. DOI [10.3310/TBFZ0277](https://doi.org/10.3310/TBFZ0277); PMID [35972773](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35972773/). Read the [184-page NCBI Bookshelf PDF](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK583141/pdf/Bookshelf_NBK583141.pdf), particularly journal pp 3, 19–31, 46–47. No local copy read. PubMed and the report confirm author order, August 2022 publication, journal, volume, issue, and pages. The exact publication day was not given; August 1 is marked approximate in the trial file.
3. **24-month journal update, abstract:** Constable L, Drake MJ, Cooper D, et al. *Eur Urol Focus.* 2026;12(4):636–643. DOI [10.1016/j.euf.2026.07.012](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.euf.2026.07.012); PMID [42648940](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42648940/). Read the [PubMed abstract](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42648940/) and publisher search excerpt. PubMed confirms August 26, 2026 online date, September issue, authors, volume, and pages. The ScienceDirect full article returned 403; no local copy was found. The fuller 2022 report supplies counts and operative detail for this same 24-month timepoint.

Guideline opened: local `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/reports/audit-v2/sources-local/eau-male-luts-2026.txt`, pp 72–75 (§5.6.5), including its explicit discussion of MASTER and its **Weak**, **Weak**, and **Strong** recommendations. I also read `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/reports/audit-v2/sources-local/aua-ipt-2024-unabridged.txt`, pp 3 and 19–22 (statements 17–24); it does not cite the MASTER report by name or DOI, so it was not used for a trial-linked recommendation on this page. Neither AUA website nor an FDA source was opened.

## Reports considered but left out

- The [2018 MASTER protocol](https://doi.org/10.1186/s13063-018-2501-2) was identified in the primary paper and WARWIKI's references but not opened or included: the primary paper and full HTA report provide the methods needed here, and the protocol contains no outcomes.
- The HTA report's nonrandomized preference cohort, qualitative interviews, and economic model are sections of the 2022 report, not separate trial-family publications. The file includes the randomized economic result but does not mix the preference cohort with randomized arm outcomes.
- No five-year outcome publication was verified. The HTA report described five-year follow-up as future work; no result was inferred from that plan.

## Source reconciliation

- The 2021 article's **abstract** prints the adjusted difference as `+3.6%` with a CI of `−11.6 to 4.6`; its Results and Table 2 give **−3.6 percentage points** for the sling-minus-AUS *continence* difference. The 2022 full report re-estimates the same endpoint at **−3.4 points** (95% CI −11.7 to 4.8). The trial file and page use the 2021 Results/Table 2 value for the primary report and preserve the difference in the file note. Source: Abrams 2021 PDF pp 812, 814, 817; HTA PDF p 53, Table 7.
- The 2021 article records **6 sling events in 6 men** and **13 AUS events in 11 men** among 180 and 173 operated men. The full 2022 report records **8 events in 8 sling men** and **15 events in 13 AUS men** among 180 and 175 operated men, adding two sling mesh erosions and two AUS-arm operations. These are report-version differences. Source: Abrams 2021 PDF pp 817–819, Table 3/Figure 1; HTA PDF pp 49–50, Tables 5–6.
- At 24 months the strict endpoint met the stated 15-point noninferiority margin (21/140 versus 22/148 continent; adjusted difference −0.6 points, 95% CI −9.2 to 8.0; P = 0.001). The less-strict definition did not meet it: 44/140 versus 51/148 continent; adjusted difference −4.0 points, 95% CI −16.7 to 8.8; P = 0.045 against the one-sided 0.025 threshold. Source: HTA PDF p 53, Table 7.

## Disagreements with existing WARWIKI pages

- `/Users/joyboy/Documents/WARWIKI/warwiki/docs/04-surgical-techniques/04f-incontinence-procedures/male-sui/male-stress-incontinence-database.mdx:54` says: “In the 2021 article, 6 sling and 13 AUS recipients had serious adverse events.” The 2021 article reports 6 sling **recipients** but 13 AUS **events in 11 recipients**. Locator: Abrams 2021 PDF pp 817–819, Surgery and AEs/Table 3. The same page's immediately following description of the 2022 report's 8/180 and 13/175 **recipients** (15 AUS events) is correct.
